      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <p>GROCER FOR MoDOUGALL'S PATENT SELF—RAISING FLOUT? THE EXAMINEI, FRI D -V, MAP CH 9 1900</p>
        <p>WATtRINGION FOOTBALL CLUB.</p>
        <p>•••xnere are so many gossips about here, ana r folks talk so," said Ella. "Really, I would rather (go back by myself, Mr. Cartleigh. ' Her guilty conscience told her that she was wronging her lover in tolerating this man's presence. "But if you walk alone they will talk still more , about you. Besides, a ' lone maiden out here will be subjected to the attentions of .objectionable persons, perhaps. Better have a friend than a stranger."</p>
        <p>She ceased to remonstrate further. They were now on the highway pear Woodhouses, where many young folks, arm-in-arm, passed to and fro. Ella Rayburn was perceived by some of her acquaintances, and she overheard many whisperings concerning herself. _ _</p>
        <p>The maii at her side spoke but little until they were again traversing a dark lane, when, catching at her arm, he slipped it through his, saying: "We might as well be like the other couples about here."</p>
        <p>She strove to disengage her arm, but he held it tightly.</p>
        <p>-"Y-ou don't mind a friend assisting you through these dark places in this fashion, Miss Rayburn, do you ? " No reply. "For you do regard me as a friend, do you not ? " "Oh, I don't know," she answeit,u, "Yes, of course you are a friend. But I don't think it's right that—that—" t„ She hesitated._ _ _</p>
        <p>"You don't think it's right that I should be such a close friend, eh ? " He drew her closer to him, but instinctively she drew back.</p>
        <p>"You must let me walk alone, Mr. Cartleigh, or I shan't regard you as a friend any longer." Her indignant tone surprised him, and he released her at once.</p>
        <p>"She's a precious, fickle tease!" he said to him- self. But aloud he said to her: "All right, Miss Rayburn, as you wish. I'll be your shadow, your disregarded shadow again," and he stepped back a pace. A short laugh came from the girl at she bounded quickly on. In a few minutes they were near Dalesworth and Ella Rayburn beheld the lights of her mother's cottage. Darting through the turn- stile at the Rorean-road, unmindful of her follower, she was hurrying on when his voice hailed her. "You'll say good-night,' Miss Rayburn ? " "Oh, yes. Pardon me, I forgot. It is late, I mustn't stay. Good-night," and she was gone. "Good-night," he called after her, with masked politeness. 'Good-night, my cherub," he said to himself as he strode up the road. "Pleasant be your dreams of me, sweetest! H'm ! Not bad for a start, I think._ She has, at any rate, tolerated me, and that a time when I could least ex- pect it. Oh, thili is going to be a very fine play, I can see. The first act is proceeding famously. Before the curtain falls on the second act, I can, in the language of the real stage villain, exclaim: Ha! ha! The girl is mine!' " Cartleigh returned to his home earlier that night than was his custom. He had no need to use his latch-key, for the hall-door was open. His appearance in the dining-room at 10.30 p.m. surprised his parents greatly. Mrs. Cartleigh, normally cold and impassive, looked on her youngest son with amaze as he sat down before her at the supper-table. "Really, is it you, Ralph ? r she cried, in hard, sarcastic tones.</p>
        <p>"It is I, mother," he answered, blandly,, taking up a knife and fork. "It is I—none other. '</p>
        <p>-His father looked over the top of his evening paper, and frowned severely; but Ralph, supremely unconcerned, heeded not the reproving scowl. "It is months since you last had supper with us," said Mrs. Cartleigh, handing over a cup of coffee to her son.</p>
        <p>"Years, maybe," said Ralph, flippantly. It was his mother's turn to scowl now.</p>
        <p>"Why do you talk so ? " she asked, petulantly. "Why do you talk and act so?" he returned, acidly. "A nice reception you give a fellow when he comes home!"</p>
        <p>"What do you mean ? " roared his father, becoming purple with rage, and throwing down the paper in his wrath. "What do you mean, sir ? " "Nothing, sir; I mean nothing," replicd Ralph, flashing a contemptuous glance at his sire, as he leisurely reached across the table for the carving- knife. He proceeded to cut a slice of beef from the huge joint before him. James Cartleigh took up his paper, held it before him wrong side up, reversed it, spread it out on the table, stroked out the creases with his thick, broad hand, then, excitedly catching it up again, he shook it in the face of his aggravating son. "I tell you, boy, I'll have none of this nonsense ! I'll have none of it, I say!" He struck the table violently, causing the silver ware to jingle noisily. "D'ye hear? You're a disgrace to your family; a positive disgrace. You-" He stopped suddenly, coughing and barking by turns as was his wont when excited to unspeakable wrath.</p>
        <p>"Yes, I've heard all that before," said the young man, quietly, when a lull in the storm permitted .him to speak. "I've heard all that before, thank you.,, He munched his bread and beef with evident relish, not being in the least disconcerted by the paternal outburst. As James Cartleigh struggled to give utterance to his pent-up wrath, his wife rose majestically, and closed the door.</p>
        <p>"You've heard it before? Yes, and you'll hear it again!" the irate parent cried. "But not for long will you hear it—not for long, I say. I'll not have this house disgraced with your presence any longer." Ralph plied his knife and fork coolly. His father's threatening words did not appear to make the slightest impression upon him. "It's monstrous," continued James Cartleigh. "Monstrous, I say, that one should be insulted by a scapegrace son like you I" "I wasn't aware that I had insulted you," quietly returned the young man. "Wasn't aware, eh? Wasn't aware! You are always insulting me; you are always insulting your mother; you are always insulting everybody here." "I merely remarked that I had heard all that before, when you, in your amiable manner, again informed me that I was a disgrace to the family. I am quite aware of the fact; you have told me so often, and—repetition becomes wearisome." - He assumed a bored expression, and this, coupled with his irritating speech, roused his parent anew. "Repetition bores you, eh? You shan't be bored much longer, young man. I'll not repeat those words much longer. I wonder if ever father was spoken to by his son as you speak to me?" "Oh, yes," replied Ralph, quite cheerfully. "I know of quite a number of fellows who speak much as Ido to their respected fathers, Of course, they have fathers who are much like yourself." James- Cartleigh was no match for his son in sarcastic speech. The full force of this last thrust was lost upon him. "You are hopeless, Ralph Cartleigh! Oh, shame that you should be son of mine! You, a Cartleigh You, the gambler, the drinker, the debauchee !" "S-s-sh, James For Heaven's sake!" Mrs. Cartleigh, who had until now calmly listened to the war of words, stayed her raging husband ere he could utter the last foul epithet. "There must be one black sheep in the flock, father," Ralph said, as he pushed his chair back from the table and commenced leisurely to use his toothpick. "We can't all be well-conducted lambs." This was a sly dig at his brother Henry, whose mild demeanour had ever been a source of irritation to flippant Ralph. James Cartleigh refused to be inveigled into further disputation with his incorrigible son, and left the room with his wife.</p>
