      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>(Copyright.</head>
        <p>(Copyright.</p>
        <p>STEVE, THE OUTLANDER.</p>
        <p>BY ARTHUR LAYCOCK, Author of "The Young Gaffer," "Seaside Sketches,* "Lancashire Sketches," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF PRECEDING CHAPTERS: Steve Hart, a factory operative, in love with Ella Rayburn, another factory hand, is compelled by the maliciousness of young Cartleign, the factory manager, to leave his post. He determines to go to South Africa. Cartleigh intends, meanwhile, to win Ella from him. As soon as Hart has gone he lays his plans, and seeks Ella's company in and out of work hours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>CHAPTER V.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER V.</p>
        <p>After a few moments' desultory chat the two women entered their respective dwellings, and the coast being clear, Cartleigh walked quickly across the fields in the direction he thought Ella Rayburn would be likely to pursue. Through the hamlet of Woodhouses he passed, halting for a moment at a turnstile opening on a footpath winding to Daisy Nook. He was undecided whether to proceed along this by-path or continue forward on the highway. He kept to the latter, however, and soon came to the canal side, and a little further on to Rhine Lake, an extensive stretch of water spreading out to the westward of the canal.</p>
        <p>Hereabouts, on this bright summer night, were congregated scores of youths and maidens, loitering on the bridge which spanned the canal, sitting along the grassy banks on the lake side, or on the seats of the small landing-stage. Numerous boats were skimming across the water, their occupants making high revelry. There a party was loudly singing a topical song. A couple of amateur oars- men in canoes were challenging each other to race. Girls were sculling clumsily, some gracefully; laughing, shouting, singing, bandying jokes with friends on the bank, all were intent on enjoying themselves. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Truly 'twas a gay and refreshing scene. Although factory chimneys loomed upon all sides, work and dreary drudgery was far from the thought of these happy young folks. They were completely given up to Saturday evening gaiety. Cartleigh entered the ale-house which overlooked the lake. As he looked out of window a trio of girls, whom he recognised as weavers at his father's mill, were pulling a boat along as they conversed loudly with someone on the opposite bank. "Come on, Ella, come in the boat. You look lonely there by yoresel'," one of the girls shouted. As Cartleigh strained forward he descried the person addressed, Ella Rayburn. She was alone, and looked ill at ease under the light-hearted bantering of her friends in the boat. "Come along, Ella. If he's gone away in a big boat you come an' sail in a little 'en, an' we'll be as happy as if we were all courtin'." The girl turned away and walked along the tow- path in the direction she had traversed before when Steve last accompanied her. Her face wore an expression of injured pride and anger. Cartleigh, from the window of the hostelry, regarded her keenly. "I 'wonder what she is thinking of Steve, the Outlander, now? Most likely blessing—h'm ! cursing him for his neglect of her, leaving her to the taunts and jibes of her friends. It would be interesting to know just what she really thinks about him at this moment. I'll follow her."</p>
        <p>He passed out of the ale-house, through the crowd assembled on the bridge, whose inquisitive eyes followed him, and descended to the tow-path. Soon he saw before him the girl whom he intended, sooner or later, to ensnare. She turned to the right and followed the path which dipped into the Vale of Medlock. Her manner was abstracted ; she was evidently engrossed in deep reflection. At the moment Ralph Cartleigh left the tow-path and walked leisurely behind her she was thinking with bitterness of her forlorn, neglected state. Thought of herself was all-paramount and superseded all thought of Stephen Hart.. "It's too bad," she reflected, "to be left all alone by oneself. I was miserable in the house and lam miserable out here. Everyone else is happy enough and enjoying life. Oh, lam miserable ! And now all the time that Steve is away, 'for good- ness knows how long, I shall have to wander about by myself or stay at home." There was no thought of the motive which had prompted her lover to go out to a far-off land and leave her thus, no thought of what his loneliness would be like this night as he sailed away from his native shores. No, all her pity and concern was for herself. She hated to be snubbed and slighted by the girls who did not now desire her company. Those who did not slight her pitied her, and that was even more hateful. She felt herself to be an outcast now, without girl-friends or men-friends. But she would have the latter. Oh, yes. She wouldn't be taunted or pitied ,by the girls who passed her with their "fellows. There were plenty of fellows about who would be glad to chaperone her, oh, yes, plenty of them And while she wouldn't forget Steve or be false to him she would- _ _ _</p>
        <p>A light footstep behind disturbed her rumina- tions. She turned instinctively, as a figure glided to her side. _</p>
        <p>- " 0 Mr. Cartleigh, how you startled me !" - -</p>
        <p>Like a flash th; remembrance of her last con- versation with this man came vividly' before her. He had proposed that he should visit her, and now, ere Steve had scarce departed, he was with her.</p>
        <p>She blushed guiltily and turned her face to hide her confusion. "Startled you, did I.? Am I such a fearful ogre, then ? " _ _</p>
        <p>I didn't mean that. I was thinking, and you came up so quietly that, for a moment, I was quite alarmed." "You were completely wrapped in thought noticed. Why, I've been walking behind you for the past quarter of an hour." "Oh, have you? " She wondered if he had been near when the girls were chaffing her. Well, it didn't matter anyhow. She would shew him, if needs be, that she was indifferent to their taunts and gibes. "I had no idea that I was being shadowed like that," she said. "No, I suppose not," he rejoined, lightly. "Your attention seemed to be so wholly riveted on your own beautiful shadow in front of you that you couldn't give consideration to the other poor shadow behind. But perhaps I'm not worth a thought, at least one of yours ? " She looked at him half-questioningly, then seemed disposed to remonstrate with him for this self-disparaging remark. He noted her difficulty. "Well, Miss Rayburn, I mustn't expect to have much of your thought, especially at such a time." He was treading on dangerous ground and knew it. The girl moved her lips tremulously, her fair face suddenly became marked with anguish, and tears gushed from her eyes. "0 Mr. Cartleigh, don't speak to me, oh, please don't 1" She buried her face in her hands and sobbed convulsively. He led her along a less frequented path by the river side. Here all was quiet save for the twittering of the birds in the trees and the prattling of the water over its rocky bed. • Faintly the strains of the band and merry laughter of the dancers at Daisy Nook drifted through the vale. The sun had sunk in the west and dark night was fast creeping on. "I must be returning home," said Ella, glancing fearfully around. The young man, pressing her arm, peered into her startled face as if to read her true feeling. "I hope I have not offended you, Miss Rayburn ?" he said, apologetically. "No, oh' no," she replied, hesitatingly, as she withdrew her arm. "It is not you who are to blame, it is—it is myself. It was silly of me to break down. I must go home now, Mr. Cartleigh." The changed manner in which she spoke led Ralph Cartleigh to believe that he had restored her equanimity. Her tone was kindly and respectful. Evidently she bore him no By the exercise of a little tact he would completely gain her favour. "May I accompany you home ? " he asked, politely. "No, thanks, I'd rather go by myself, I would really." She turned about quickly and ran a few paces. He followed, scarce knowing how far he could safely proceed. "It is too dark for you to g 9 alone," he called out. The girl halted irresolutely. She wanted his company now. To be by herself was fearful. She was afraid of her own thoughts. "Come, I will go with you," he said, and together they ascended the hill-path.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>CIIAPTER VI.</head>
        <p>CIIAPTER VI.</p>
        <p>James Cartleigh took much pride in the fact that he had risen from the ranks. From the lowest rung of the ladder to the highest; from little piecer to factor-owner he had risen. His ascension, how- ever, had not been one continuous, glorious success. Whilst engaged in the upward climb, he had been compelled to trample down much that he would rather have preserved. This was in the early stages, when, in his anxious ambition to secure wealth, his better nature would occasionally revolt against the sordid aims; when he had qualms of conscience, which caused him many hours' uneasiness. But, as the hard, commercial spirit grew stronger within him, the Voice of the better spirit gradually became stifled, and at last it might truly be said that James Cartleigh had conquered himself—his better self—and stood triumphant as the hard, soulless, business man. And having arrived at this successful stage, possessing considerable wealth, having social position, his wife and family about him, living in a home beautified by much grand furnishing, James Cartleigh felt that looking back on the incessant toil of the past and on his present state, he ought now to be supremely happy. But he was not, There was a fly in the ointment.</p>
        <p>His wealth was a continual source of worry and anxiety to him. Much of his money was invested in cotton fact ones other than his own, and the cotton trade had not flourished of late. Matters domestic were not wholly conducive to happiness and concord. His wife, whom he had married "for her brass," as the gossips in the district said, appeared to have much of that metal in her con- stitution. She was cold, austere, unsympathetic, severe with everyone—her husband, her children, her servants.</p>
        <p>Not a loving nature by any means. The union of two natures, each sadly lacking the finer human qualities, resulted disastrously for two at least of their offspring. Marie, the only daughter, combined in her character most of the prominent peculiarities of her parents. She was even more proud, more haughty and overbearing than her mother. Ralph, as we have seen, was a curious mixture of temperaments. He too, in large part, had inherited most of his father's failings; but his nature was less cold and austere than his mother's, in consequence of an inherent tendency to-flippant humour—humour of the biting, sarcastic kind, truly, but nevertheless humour which, in the hardest and most selfish temperaments, must needs have a softening, humanising effect. Henry, the festhetic, was quite unlike any of his kindred. He was quiet, reserved, studious, and of refined tastes, with no liking whatsoever for com- mercial pursuits. As a youth his parents regarded him as a "milksop" ; and while reckless, wilful Ralph was spoilt he was repeatedly snubbed. In manhood his kindly personality exerted a magnetic influence on all about him save envious Ralph, and Henry became first favourite in the household. In Henry, therefore, James Cartleigh saw some- thing of his old self, his better self before the spirit of greed wholly possessed him, and 'uncon- sciously he was drawn towards his eldest son. So, too, Mrs. Cartleigh, in the gentle, kindly Henry, - recognised the true man, the man she had never known in her own husband. Passive, self-absorbed Marie, also, was secretly proud of her brother "who was studying for the ministry," and 'by virtue of lais exalted calling more than his personal good qualities, she paid deference to him. Ralph, the reprobate, alone refused to pay homage to his brother. He sneered at the "meek and mild, psalm-singing parson," called him a fraud and humbug, and sought at every opportunity to hold him up to ridicule. Henry never retaliated, never rebuked his taunting brother, never "preached" at him, and, on this account, Ralph hated him the more.</p>
        <p>James Cartleigh had much difficulty in endeavouring to reconcile the conflicting elements in his household. Wife and daughter he could manage fairly well by permitting them to have much of their own way, but Ralph, whatever he was allowed to do, was absolutely unmanageable. "Hopelessly incorrigible," said his father. He had repeatedly threatened to turn his son adrift if he did not amend his ways. Then his pride would sting him. "Turn him out and have all the gadding tongues of the town talking of his. family affairs ? " No, he could not stand that. He would give the young scamp another trial. So Ralph was always "on trial." For days together he would be absent from home and from his work at the mill, never vouchsafing any explanation on his return.</p>
        <p>"He has been to Blackpool or Douglas," his parents concluded, until later they discovered that he was a regular attendant at race-meetings, and a patron of certain places of disrepute in the district. Then they ceased to. conjecture or trouble them- selves concerning him, dreading only that his conduct should become more widely known in Dalesworth and neighbourhood. On the Monday morning following on the heated conversation with his son James Cartleigh walked down to Fernleigh Mill and had a long interview with his cashier, Mr. Meadstone, an old and trusted servant. The two men were closeted together for more than an hour, during which time the sales-books kept by Master Ralph were closely examined. The tense, apprehensive look on James Cartleigh's face as ha made copious notes from the sales-books indicated that something was wrong. - _ _ _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>"What-time do you expect Ralph here?" asked the mill-owner, closing the books and preparing to leave the office.</p>
        <p>"He usually comes in about eleven o'clock, sir," said the cashier. "When he comes tell him that I wish to see him at home, or perhaps it would be best to send up for me and I will come here."</p>
        <p>"All right, sir, I'll send up." "I'm going to have this matter ended once and for all, said James Cartleigh, decisively. "If my son can't keep himself on the allowance I make him, I'll not have him here tampering with the books and swindling me. I'll not have him here at all."</p>
        <p>"Oh, I'd give him another chance, Mr. Cartleigh," said the cashier, who, be it said, had a sincere liking for the reckless Ralph. "I'd give him one more chance. Apart from this he's been ever so well-conducted of late, at least about the mill."</p>
        <p>"Haven't I given him enough chances, Mead- stone ? You know that no man's son has had more chances than he. I'll endure it no longer. You must get another clerk. I'll not have him about the office, at any rate, and he shall not act as salesman any more.' "Very' good, sir," said Meadstone, "I'll get another clerk. Mr. Ralph will still continue as manager, I presume?" "Well, yes, for a time." . The cashier was glad to get the irate father to concede this much.</p>
        <p>"So I have no need to trouble you if Mr. Ralph comes ? " "No." And James Cartleigh withdrew. Ralph came jauntily into the office at noon as the mill-hands were leaving for dinner, and the cashier at once took him aside and talked seriously with him, telling of his father's decision. "Oh, well, if the guv'nor objects to my style of book-keeping he is at liberty to get a fresh man, / don't care. And as for bossing the show generally, well, I'm not particular whether I do or not, but as the guv'nor desires it, well, I oblige him. I'm nothing if not obliging, eh, Meadstone ? " The old cashier tried to scowl reprovingly, but, meeting Ralph's mischievous gaze, he turned away with a smile.</p>
        <p>"I'm afraid he's hopeless," said the old man to himself. "Dear! dear! To think that this care- less young man should be the son of James Cartleigh I It is extraordinary. Nature plays some curious tricks with human kind, and like dies not always breed like." -- • (To be continued."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE, I have noticed that the coat and the bolero are as much worn as ev,r, and in the latest the fronts grow longer, hanging down at their side in points. I think a basque a useless thing and only serves to detract from that slimni,ss which is the delight of all women. The b.:A0.0 undergoes uhamsieon- like changes, though rather in form than in colour and it swoops clown some cases and in others nearly covers tile or extends for only a couple of inches in front oi the arms, falls to the waist, or stops short half-way. It is said that for pretty effects, stitchery will be responsible in the near future. It is the cheapest form of trimming there is, requiring only time, a sewing in,. me, and a steady hand, and is sufficiently sxtita.),-)le te the home dressmaker.</p>
        <p>Two hats I saw lately took my fancy immensely. One was a hat of cloth or felt, threaded through with a couple of quills and finished with ostrich tips; the other a velvet toque embroidered with silk and a very few sparkling sequins, and com- pleted by an aigrette of lilies-of-the-valley and their leaves. Very soon we shall see the pretty flower toques, composed entirely of violets, lilies- of-the-valley, cowslips, and other spring flowers that dare the winds of March with beauty, which the milliners prepare every year to accord with the bright days of early spring. The violet is the flower that will be much worn everywhere.</p>
        <p>I saw a lady with a very nice dress the other afternoon at a friend's and I had an excellent opportunity of examining it. The collar is white or ivory silk veiled with lace, and the front is similar. There is no fulness whatever on the hips. That at the back is provided by a douqle box- pleat, that is arranged as flat as possible on the upper part of the skirt. This is now the accepted fashion for the new skirts, both for morning and evening wear. The-new gathered skirt is ex- tremely well suited to such aerial fabrics as silk muslin, chiffon and crepe-de-chine.</p>
        <p>I may now tell you a little about the most usefu blouses that I have seen lately. The first I will speak to you about is merely a tight silk bodice for evening wear, trimmed with a lace bolero, hanging in deep points over a waistband in coloured panne. The shoulder straps and rosettes are in similar panne held down by fancy paste-buttons. The silk bodice may be covered closely with crepe-de- chine. The second blouse is in mauve tucked silk, with a vest of gathered white chiffon and a double collar, the upper one being in mauve panne, the lower in mauve silk, embroidered in white, and matching the centre pleat of the blouse. The third is an evening bodice, in satin, the colour a butter- cup yellow. The lace forms a deep bertha, which crosses over to the left, the end disappearing under the velvet waistband. The elbow sleeves are finished with lace matching that of the bertha, with a crossing of velvet about matching that in the waistband.</p>
        <p>For the benefit of those who like to know what fur is in favour, I may remark that chinchilla does not now enjoy nearly BO much favour as ,sable. It Is as fragile as sable is durable, and this quality tells in the end, even with women who can afford new sets of furs with every winter. A tall lady in a coat of dark sable wore a toque of similar fur with striped black and white ribbon arranged in loops as trimming, and clusters of violets.</p>
        <p>Your loving J EITNIE.</p>
        <p>OX-CHEEK STEWED.—lngredients : Ox cheek, three onions, three turnips, three carrots, three bay leaves, three roots of celery, spice, sweet herbs, pepper, salt, a piece of butter, flour, and ketchup. Put the vegetables in the saucepan and the ox cheek on top; just cover the boiling water snd boil gently three hours. SAVOURY FINORES (cold).—Order the required number of tiny finger rolls from the baker (the rolls should be 3 inches long and about 2 inch wide), split them open and take out a little of the crumb. Fill with salad tossed in mayonnaise, and put together again; or butter and spread thickly with potted meat, 'salmon or shrimp. CELERY FLAVOURING. —Soak for a fortnight oz. seeds of celery in half pint brandy. A few drops of this will flavour a pint of soup very nearly as well as if a head of ecilery were dewed in it.</p>
