      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>AN ALLEGED CORNER IN SETTS.</p>
        <p>hJ rneeting of the Warrington Town Council w9s in toe Council Chamber at Itu Town Hall on Tuesdry evenirig. The Mayor (Ildeirnaa R3bert) presided, ',,ort there vrera also peesent FAitciongh, dut7 chinson. and T El Seton,Councillors J Baird, A Bennett, Jartes Evans, Jini Evans, ía Eilton, L Greing, R Heaton, A Joley, A j Jolley, J Lee, F W Monks, A Pea- cock, WPierpoint, G Plumpton„f P.oWrte, FR Roberts. W Ro4ers. J SiL ok, J Stnetlitus, 0 Tionion, kinson, wi:h MeArs J Lyn.' W'-ottle (sown clerk), S Wllson (deputy town clerk). J.- Fairhurst (borough treacurer), T Lmgdin (borough surveyor), J H (mf-dical offic,r), J Haas (wAter arid sanitary ettgineer), C Madeley (lihrorink), W S fiaddwk (gasengineer). The Late Councillor Joseph Wilkinson.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR te.d a letter he had received from Mr. Jbn Wilkinsbh (now Councillor Wilkincon) thanking the Coutcil for the letter of sympathy sent on trieir be- half on the occasion of the death Gf his lather,Ccuncillor Joseph Wilkinson, one of th repre.scritwives of the Orford Ward, and also thinking the Mayor ior his kind exprt ssion f sympathy. • •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>Late- Mr. T. G. Rylands</head>
        <p>The Late- Mr. T. G. Rylands</p>
        <p>lhe Maros said he very much regretted as be was sure esery memher of the Council regrettrd the fact that dePth had been very bask in their ranks, aLd that they bad I,Bt Mr T. Glazehrook Rylands, an ex-Mayor of Th • Corporation, a magistrate, and for so Any years a very Active townsman in all mAc.ers appertaining to the wellbeing of the borough, Re Was a member of a very illustrious family, in their loc.l annals, and 'tr., was !hare foie fitting that they 8 houtd express -regret at his 'decease. 113 Worship then submittf d the usual formal resolution, to the effect that the Council received with dsep regret the announcement of the death of Mr rits.lands, and expressed its wncere sympathy nd condo- lence with Mrs Hylands and family in their sad bereave- ment. I.:l,.!rman SUTTON sec:lndel, and sail the deceased iltlok a giev, interest in all educ,tional work, and they were indebted to him as a borou4o for•many advantages they prssessed in that respect. When tr, was a boy be attended me decelsed'= school. and derived a good many valuable lessons from him. • The motion was• c•irrie:!. The Late Councillor Dr. Cannel!. •</p>
        <p>The MAYOR said that they had that day seen carried to his last resting plac4 another member of the Council, namely CatthEi.ilOt Dr. Cantlell. WbateVer ()piAbili they might have had which differed slightly la some matters from him, they all agreed that in any work connected witu t'ne Council he was most ass;duelus and attentive ; and in some branches he gave an amount of time, en,rgy,and devotion which was particularht cianracteris- tic of him, and vaulted in great benefit, Hs WO 21id to know for a fact that in his profession. he was must kind-hearted, especially to the poor. (Hear, hear). ' Councillor BEATON seconded and said no had known Dr. Cannel' for 30 years: ALjerinan BOLTON in upportini said the deceased had been a most sincere Hand to him, and he vas very con- scientious in his municipal dUtie.S. The tiescettiold e. presf d his opinion without fear or favour, and he he- lieved the Council had lost. one of its most useful mem- bers. He was particularly assiduous in connection with matters, relating to the Town Hall, Park and Baths Com- mittee, and to the public gardens, (11e.r, hear.) Councillor BARNETT, As one of. the representatives of the Howley Ward, supported, and said on all hinds lie heard must el,cfient tectiat.-mics to D. Catir.ell's kin- tress of heart. (Hear, hear.) There Nris not a more popular man in Howley Ward, and he would be griatly missed. Councillor LEE spoke of the doctor's kindness to the poor, especially to children. A similar motion to that submittal in the case of Mr. 'Wands was put and carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>Late Mr. Joseph Garner.</head>
        <p>The Late Mr. Joseph Garner.</p>
        <p>The MAY,II hen alluded to the k.te Mr. Joseph Garner who be said was at on., time a member of the Council. Be was a most inoffensive, unassuming, and diligent servant of the town. He was sure his place would be difficult to fill in the circle in which he moved. (Hear hear ) Hs submitted a similar resolution as in the last case. Councillor Jams EvArrs seconded and referred to the good character Mr. Garner obtained in toe dric.; which he lived. - - -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>Re-election in Orford Ward,</head>
        <p>The Re-election in Orford Ward,</p>
        <p>The MAYOR announced the election of Mr. jotm Wil- kinson as one of the representatives ot the above ward, in succession of his late father. Auditors.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR also announced tne re-31ection of Mgrs. John Harrison and James Bolton as elective aufittorx, and appointed Conn...Aar Bennett as his own au ntor for the ensuing twelve months. •- -</p>
        <p>Watch Committee. Serving Constables while on Duty. At a meeting of the above committee it was resolved that the Chief Const.ble address a letter to lic9nscd victuallers calling their attention to the penalties for serving c4nstatles while on duty, and informing MOM that they may rely upon the support of the committee in refusing to supply constables. The MAYOR moved the adoption of the minutes. Alderman STJTI•ON seconded, and it was agreed to. Finance and Estate Committee. The Overseers of the Poor.</p>
        <p>At a meeting held on the 26,h February it was resolved that Messrs James Fairclough, Arthur Bennett, J Shaw Green, and Frederick William Monks be appointed over- seers of the poor of the township of Warrington for the ensuing year. Alderman FAIRCLOUGII proposed the adoption of the minutes. Councillor Lys, in seconding, said he had omitted to, state at the election that the thanks of the committee were due to the overseers for their past services. Their duties last year were rather more difficult than usual on account of the transfer of the assi,tant overseers's duties. fie moved that the overseers be thanked far their services. The minutes were carried, and the Couricil'a thanks were given to the overseers. Street Improvement and Lighting Committee</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>Sheds in Backyards.</head>
        <p>Sheds in Backyards.</p>
        <p>At a meeting.held onlthe 22nd February,a large number of persons were summoned befell° the Committee for offences against the bylaws by erecting sheds in the yards attached to the premises occupied by them, and an order was made in each case for the removal of the shed. The minutes were confirmed, on the motion of Aide:- man Surros, seconded by Alderman B wrox, Public Abattoirs.</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON proposed the ,minutes of ,the Health Committee, the MAYOR seconding. Councillor PIRRPOINT asked when the Committ.e was going to talc.) into serioui cansidera.tion the impartaut question of providing abattoirs in the Borough. He had no very serious charge to formulate against private slaughter-houses, but Warrington was an important and growing borouczn, and public slaughter houses should be provided. (Hear, hear). The Hospital Extensions.—Another Discus-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>sion.</head>
        <p>sion.</p>
        <p>Councillor PLUMPTON referred to the extensione of the fever hospital. The Council had been given to under- stand that the entrance out of Lovely-lane should not be used for admitting fever cases. There had been a deputation of property owners and reeidente of White cross Ward to the Health Committee and that deputation was assured that the entrance from Lovely-lane should not be made the entrance for the fever cases. They were also led to believe that the fever patients entrance would be down Aiken-street and on the west side of the present entrance. So far as he could see now, the entrance instead of being from Goulden-street was to be from Monks-street, which was only a few yards away. The feeling of the people in the neigtrhourheod was that the committee were taking undue advantage of the de- putation which waited upon them. He must on this occasion move an amendment that the minutes of the committee referring to the laying out of the land be not cenfirmed. Councillor James EVANS said that as a representative of Whitecroes Ward he felt hound to second the amend ment. It was not a question of personal opinion, hut of representation and he voiced the opinion of the majority. Alderman BOLTON said they were bound to construct this street. and Monks-street, on the term for the purchase of the land. The MAYOR said it was originally proposed to construct a lodge and entrance in Goulden-street, but in deference to the views of the deputation the lodge was deferred. As 53011 as they found they were bound under the land purchase agreement to make a street higher up they decided to put the lodge there. The promise given to the deputation that that entrance should not be used for the purpose of conveying fever patients to the hospital still held good. (Hear, heaee) Councillor SMETHURST ssid they had actually had offers for the purchase of a piece of land on the left hand ' side of the hospital entrance, for tee rurpose of building cettage property on it. Councillor PLUMPTON : Bogus offers? The Mavon No I offered to tuy it myself and the offer stands to-day. Aldsrman SUTTON said there was no intention of using the street they were goicg to make for the purpose of conveying patients. Councillor PLUMPTON said he would withdraw his amendment on that assurance. In reply to Councillor Nerpoint, Alderman SUTTON said they had the abbatoir question before there continually, and they had every desire to proceed with the provision of abbatoire immediately. There had been so much to do of late however. that they found it mpossible to go into the question yet. As aeon as the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>AMINEtt, FRP PAY, MARCH 9 1900.</head>
        <p>AMINEtt, FRP PAY, MARCH 9 1900.</p>
        <p>f 1 II</p>
        <p>ceimmiVme wfre in a position to take steps far th..! pro- vision of abattoirs-they-would do so. . Ttio minutes were then coofictned. THE PURCHASE OF SETTS.</p>
        <p>STANDING ORDERS V13;..d.7-1.,D.</p>
        <p>Lively Discussion.</p>
        <p>the rr,sp-,:szibility in regard tl the '.31.1.1 of the commit- , ee, b.st tstP present was not the first tiro,: they had gone ,utsi,se the pmv;sions of standing os-ders ho:...aus--i goods couW t ohtaiqed t t • cheskplr rate A a rule they ad- vertisf-A tor h.at they rerviired.- AkitTmln SUTTON was in f.sysur of evrytbing bsing dory.,in ievil manner, ani mat the/ shoul,l follow the standing', od bat then were cases in whic:l it wis neci-ssary tor the committee to have a discretionary</p>
        <p>Councillor PEACOCK supported the amendrivint on principle, and couten•led' that they would as a Council benefit in the end by adtieriug to the standing orders.</p>
        <p>Councillor F. R. ROBERTS asked vrby the smlll to whom refereßce oad buen made could not also tender. Councillor lixtaon replied that they were not known. , _</p>
        <p>The MAYOR assured the Councillor that that was not so They had been gettinz Macclesfield setts in War- rington for 20 years. They knew them all. Councillor TINNION.—You should do ;i is your busi- ness. , _</p>
        <p>Councillor F. R. ROBERTS Colliti net Understand why the people to whom reference had been made should not tender in answer to an advertisPment.</p>
        <p>-- Councillor Tztonas : The small people would never See it. Councillor F. R: ROBERTS said the standing orders were the result of the collective wiicloin of th., Council and of years of experience.. It was no answer to say that advertising would have given the a,bole show away. , The MAYOR said the result of the minutes being passed Would be to destroy tie value of any standing order. If each committee was to be a law unto itself they might as well rub out the orders, and let the Council do as it liked. lie woeld like to correct several statemeets which had been made in ignorance. The setts proposed Ito be bought ueder the resolution were grit setts. The setts about which there had been this difficulty for years were granite Fetts, and there was no comparison in quality and durability. Years ago he suggested a cheaper sett for the side streets than the 41n granite cube, but it had eot been conaidered expedient by the Paving and Sewerage Committee until they found it to be impossible to get a sufficient supply. With regard to the supposed value of deputations to various quarries, he could tell thena as the 'result of a long busi- ness experience that if they were not capable of making all their purchases from their own office there was something seriously wrong. It would pay them betar to keep tne committee at home and the borough surveyor to attend to the legitimate duties of his office than to send him all over the country buying actts. If with all his professionel experience he was net qualified to advise the committee, the sooner they got a new barough surveyor the better. People who did fifty times the business of toe Corporation did not require to send'cleputatious and if they advertised in the trade jour- nals they would get a perfect response. The action of- me committee was most absurd, and it remained to be , proved that the tendcrs received under advertisement were not as good and cheap as those which were re- ceived without. The same committee had advertised within the last fortnight for 4,500 tons of the very same class of setts that wag proposed to be purchased under the resolution. Coule anything be more inconsistent ? If it wasnot necessary to advertise in the first iutance why shoulc it be advertised in the paper in the second in- stance. Then was not a merchant in Warrington who was connected with any thine whatever in regard to the purchase of setts, and he objected to the deliberate CX elusion of the ratepayers of the town from an oppor- tunity of supplying material to the Corporation. They asked for no favoar, and they did uot want any. All that they asked tor was for a jtut and legal tender to be ..ffered. With a view of keeping down the poni- bility of mischief and in the iaterescs of fair play he hoped the amendment wouid be carried. Councillor MONIES said he was perfectly satisfied with the explanation of Cauncillor Tinnion. He admitted that the committee might have acted ultra virtu, and were, perhaps, introducing a danterous principle, in departing from the standing orders, but the circum- stances of the case appeared te be exceptional and justi- fied the action of the committee in not advertising for these particular tenders. The Council always reserved to itself the right of refusing to confirm the committee's action. Alderman FAISCLOUGH said he should support the action of the committee, as the case was an exceptional one. Councillor Rewrote, in defending the action of the committee, stated that when in March, 1899, the com- mittee advertised for 2,500 tens of setts, the lowest ten- d:sr was 32s Gcl per ton, and that was something like Ts 21 a ton :nore than they had previously paid. The committee went to a granite quarry direct and pur- chased 2,500 tons of Petri at a price which was £1,375 less than the tenders put in by the merchants of War- rington'. The MAYOR: You are entirely wrong. Councillor HEATON said the figures he had in his pos- session bore out what he had`said and the saving effected by the committee was equal to £2 93 for the paving of each cottage. The MAYOR: How many of the setts you bought have you actually get? Councillor TINNION said he learned thattof the order of 2 500 tons there were 400 or 500 tons still to be delivered. , - - . -</p>
        <p>--Cin—a—y—o.te being taken,there voted for the amendment: The Mayor, Gmachlors Peacock, j41119i Evane, Green- ing, A J Jolley, Pierpoint, Ellisn, F R Roberts, John Roberts and Bennett. -</p>
        <p>For the motion :—Lldertnen Hatehiosen, Sutton, Bolton, Feircleueh, Councillors Piumpten, Stnethuut, finnion. B 4ird, k. Jolley, John Evans, Salooek, Heaton, Moaks, Wilkinson, and Rogers. The MAYOR announced that the amendment was lost by 15 votes to 10, and the minutes wi,re then carried. The MAYoR : This vote will seriously aff4ct any noel- lion, audit may go to the extent of deciding whether I shall retain the chair or not for the remainder of the 5 ear. I should like to have an expression of opinion on the matter whether this violation of the standing orders is likely to be repeated. If not, lam quite preparel to leave the matter where it stands, but I shall not oceupy this chair if it is going to be repeated. Councillor HEATON . I should be eleas-d if there was a resolution passed by this Council that we are never to do this. I will see I don't do it. The MAYOR : Tne resolution is on the Council pro- ceedings. Councillor HESTON : There is more in this than ap- pears on the surface. The MAYOR: I shall not occupy the chair if the stand- ing orders are to be violated withow; good reason. Alderman &amp;mole : I shall support you in that, Mr. Mayor. I believe iR everything being done correctly. Alderman Boraces said he was sorry the Mayor should attempt to use threats to coerce the Council. If he did so he would not sit in the Council, hecauen the com- mittee had dene its hest. Alderman Boleon was abeut to leave the chamber, when ' Councillor PIEEPoINT said he was sorry to hear the Alderman use such language. Alderman B3LTON : I seal' do my duty without fear or favour. (Cries of "Sit down.") The Alderman then sat down. Councillor Piearcnser If you don't like to hear the truth you may gO. Alderman BOLTON , I will hear it. Councillor PIERPOINT, continuing, said he believed the members of the committee had done thoir duty, as they thought, honestly ; at the same time he was esktitled to hold the cpinian that the staudine orders should he held inviolate unless by the permission of the Council assembled at the monthly meeting. The Council had been placed in an awkward position, and he did hope that what the Mayor had said, perhaps rather heatedly, would now he allowed to drop. He was sure the good sense of the Council would assert itself, and that they would have no more of this kind of thing in the future. The MAYOR: I have only ask d for an expression of opinion from the committee because I consider a very vital principle is involved, but I say deliberately that with the opinions I hold in regard to the standing orders provided for the deliberations of the Council that if they are not to be fairly and strictly observed-- Councillor SMITHI7EST (interrupting) : I will assure you that it shall not occur again with me. I hope you will dismiss the case with a caution. (Laughter.) Ccuncillor PLIMPTON : The committee ought to have an opportunity of discussing the matter. I have never known a discussion hke this after the minutes have been passed. It is entirely out of order and the members of the Paring and Sewerage Committee ought to decide what they shall do. The MAYOR: lam simply asking for an expression of opinion from the Chairman as representing the commit- tee in r‘gard to its future business. Councillor Treeiox : I suggest you leave the matter to-night. It can be brought before the committee. The Chairman is absent. May I suggest that the matter be allowed to drop for the present until the committee have had a meetitag ? lam quite prepared to discuss the matter from any stendpoint. It is rather a pity to say anything further. Let the matter drop. Councillor HEATON asked if the caneing orders were kept by the other committees. The MAYOR replied that they were so far as the com- mittees were cone rued with which he was connected. He could not say more than that. Tee sutjeet was afterwards allowed to drop. Another Schoe! Attendance Inspector.</p>
        <p>Councillor Sluttcoor in moving the miwutes of the School Attendance Committ.e, referred to the resolution for an additional school attendance inspector at 4 salarY of 304 per week. Ho said the continued increase of the inhtbitants of the Borough necessitated an additional officer.</p>
        <p>The Architect for the New Technical Institute</p>
        <p>The MAYOR in moving the confirmation of the minutes of the Museum, Library, Arts and Technical Instruction Committee. said Mr. Owen hid been empowered to pre- pare plans for the new institute. Councillor Gnextii.wo seconded the adoption of the minutes. Councillor BENNETT ststed thst while he had every possible respect tor Mr. Owen, he thought it was hardly fair that their younger architects wire not given an</p>
        <p>opportunity of being the architect. He thought the plans would htve been thrown opal for competition, the eame as was the case with the new police buildings. The IVIAYaa said the Council had already empowered the commute to appoint an architect. _</p>
        <p>C)uncilior BENNE.TT : N )6bing was said as to the way the apOoiatment was_to_be made.</p>
        <p>CotiOcillor LEE aeked whether this f was not a case Wht.ll3 the standiog orders ou.-ttit to ha obeyed. It is a quE•stien where more than £2O is involved and according to the standing orders ought to have been thrown open. _</p>
        <p>Councillor TINSION askqd whether the discussion was not out of order. It was not on the minutes.</p>
        <p>The Maros said it was strictly not in order. It had arisen on an addendum of his in moving the minutes.</p>
        <p>Councillor PLIIMPTON said the appointment was con. trary to standing (Tiers. The minutes were then pamed. The Alterations at the Cemetery.</p>
        <p>Councillor Evass moved the adoption of the minutes of the Cemetery Committee and remarked that the committee decided to make separate chambers in all the three chapels. Since then the Roman Catholic clergy bad intimated that they objected to this separate chamber, and as tar as that coapel was concerned no alteration would be made. It was anticipated that the cost of the alterations about to be made was £7OO. Councillor PEACOCK SOCO,&amp;d. Councillor MONKS : The committee might take into consid,ration the cost of a crematorium.</p>
        <p>The minutes were then plssed. Assistance to the Volunteers. Reduction of Rent.</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON moved the confirmation of the minutes of the Town Hail, Park and Baths Committee, which recommended that the design of the railings and gate to be erected at the Kendrick—street corner of the Par be approved, and that the rent of the Volunteer Quarters be reduced to the sum of £5 per annum.— Alderman Bolton informed the Council that the rent of the Volunteer quarters had previously been £5O per annum. He theug,,ht the Corporation ought to do all it could to assist the Volunteer movement. The volunteers had proved their worth recently, and they were a good fighting force in case of emergency. They did not know at the present momeGt what the G wernment intended to do to assist the Volunteers, but the present reduction was of a temporary kind and cstud be altered afterwards if the Corporation wished. The officers of the Volunteers were undertaking a large expenditure at the present time, and the assistance of the Corporation would be appreciated. Alderman FAIRCLOUGH seconded, and said the new a-rmagernent was subject to six months' notice. Councillor BENNETT asked. whether the proposed rail- ings were of the same design as those in Crosfield- street.</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON said tlaey were ot the same pattern but of a better kind.</p>
        <p>Councillor riBAT011• objected to the reduction of the rent for the Volunteer quarters and contended that the value of toe land on which they stood was worth £l2O a year. Whmever the Corporatio,, bourit propPrtv it seemed to depreciate the value o it. The money which was about to be spent by tlie Volunteer officers was simply for a billiard room. Ile objec;ed tl tilts gift to the Volunteers.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR : Legally we cannot make a specific grant. Councillor HELTON : I would not do anything illegal.</p>
        <p>The Mayor: It is not an illegal proposal. We can not make a grant of money to the volunteeis but we are at liberty to reduce the rent of the volunteer quarters. Councillor linaTos said tle CArpOratlol2 refused to provide places for children to play on, bit they were willing to provide quarters for volunteers and a place for gentlemen to meet in at Bridge Foot. (Laughter.) He proposed an amendment that the minute referring to the volunteer qtiarters be referred back. Councillor PZACOCIE seconded the ameadment and said the Corporation was not in a position to throw away such a sum as £45 a year. Councillor BEnNiarr said Councillor Heaton gauged everything by the money value which was the most miserable standard for anyone to adopt. He admired the splendid valour of the volunteers and was in favour of the proposed reduction of rent of the volunteer quarters. Councillor TINNION thought the support of the volun- teers ought to be a charge on the Imperial Exchequer. (Hear, hear.) As long as local boliee were found will jog to support their volunteers, the G wernment would withold turther assistance. He had considerable sym- pathy with the amendment but did not wish to do any- thing that would appear to minimise their estimation of the local volunteers. - - - -</p>
        <p>Councillor F R ROBBUTS Said his sympathies were entirely with the amendment and he should support it. The resolution was premature. They ought to wait and see what the Goverrmeet intended to do for the volun- teers. There was a large body in the country which was against the military spirit of the age and as voicing their opinions he was against the resoluton. Alderman SUTTON thought as the Volunteers were do- ing so much on blhalf of the country the Corporation ought to assist them all it possibly could. . The MAYOR said it was oaly fair to state that the pro- posal had not enaanated from [he officers.but members of the Corporation happened to know that their position in regard to the future was an extremely difficult one to face. The officers had a capital eap mditure to face which would have to be paid by private subscriptions. He thought the spirit which had animated the volunteers during the last few months was such as to deserve very serious recognition from them as a municipal body. When he remind.-d them that Nottingham had spent £200,000 for cproviding a shooting range he did nut think they would be d )ing anything ia the shape of a crime if they reduces the rent of the volunteers quarters from £5O to £5. On a vote being taken the amenitnent was lost, only four voting for it. The minutes were then passed. The minutes of the remaining committees, which con- tained nothing of importance, were passed without com- ment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>THREAT TO RESIGN BY THE</head>
        <p>THREAT TO RESIGN BY THE</p>
        <p>MAYOR.</p>
        <p>, The Paving and Sl:ewe:age Committee's minutes :in- cluded a number.of tenders which had been accepted. It was resolved that instructions he given teet the streets are to be cleated of anew in future kete with, Whether the fall occurs on a Sunday or not, and that the cestre of Witderspool-causeway between the outer lines of the proposed tramways be meds good witn macadam. . • Councillor lineootor moved the adoption of the minutes. . Councillsr SMETHURST seconded. Councillor GREENING ',eked why the following tenders for eats, detailed on the minutes, and recommended for accepeance,were not duty advertised,in sceordaane with the standing order .—Messrs. Williamson and Sons (Macclesfield), 20 tons per week for 12 months; Mr G Sutton (Macclesfield), 15 tons per week for 12 mouths, and curbs ; Messrs Bradbury and Biddulpb, 500 tons of 51n., at 60 tone per month, and 500 tons of 4in., at 60 tons per month; Mr S Sheldon, 500 tons of sin., at 80 tons per month, and 500 tons of 4iu., at 80 tons per mooth ; Messrs Walsh and Sons, 200 tons of 6;n.; Mr D T Alderson, 250 tons of 61n., at 40 tons per month; eir J Jackson, 500 tons of 6in., at 40 tons per mouth, and 500 tons of 5 in., at-40 tons per mentie Councillor BEATON t We have received the tenders, but whether they have been advertised for or not I do not know. The ation I know they have not been advertised for. Councillor GREENING moved an amendment thee the minutes of the conainittee be confirmed with the excep- tion of the recommendation refee-ed to. He felt very stropely in this matter. They ha 1 a standing order to the effect that anything over £2O should be duly advertised, and be open to free competition. He wished to know why the committee had not advertised. It was ;only fair that their towahneople should have an oppor- tunity of tendering. Councillor ELLISON seconded. This, he said, was a question that was threshed out some time ago, and it was understood that these sort of contracts should be advertised. Councillor PIERPOINT asked the Town Clerk if the tenders received without having been advertised for were not ultra vireo Tbe TOWN CLERK: It depends:upon the action of the Council. Councillor PIERP3INT said they had here a standing order which defiecd the business of the Council, and the manner in which it shouldjbe done. Surely they were not !going behind their stsnding order-;. He did not care whether it was Tom, Dick, or Harry who benefited, the Council should stick to its standing orders. That was honest and business-like. Alderman BOLTON asked for it to be stated what aavastsge they were getting by not advertising. If they were trying to get the best of some trade c he threreht the Committee had a right to make the best bargain that was possible for the Corporation. They might wink at a standing order in a case of this kind. Councillor MoNsts said that if an explanation was given now as to why the standing order waif departed from it might save time. COUnCiaOr PLUMI.TON replied net the committee had no other object in view than to da their best for the ratepayers. Councillor. PIERPOINT :—No one questions that. Councillor PLIMPTON said they had effected a saving for the small property-owners who had to make the streets. If those peopleovere asked to pay the price they (the committee) were asked to pay for setts it would ruin them. The reason the committee had obtained the setts as they had was because that if they went into the quarry to buy a large quantity of stones, and did not keep it quiet, they could not get the same price next time the people were hot at. Councillor PIERPOINT said they had contravened the stendine order and he wanted an explanation. Councillor TINNION said the Gas Committee did it for i'-4R Coat and cannel.. Alderman BaLTIN : We do not. 'The MAYOR: The Gas Committee advertienin the trade journals. Councillor PLUMPTON : We are advertising now. The MAYOR: You are not. It is the grossest perver- sion of re standing order, I have seen in the 15 years I have been a memter of the Council. • Counciihr PLIMPTON said the matter made no differ- ence to him. His interest was the interest of the property owners and the ratepayers generally. The MANOR You Irive not given us any explana- tion. Councillor JAMES EVANS Said it appeared . to him that the committee had acted for the purpose of striking a bargain. He had heerd it said that it they aevertieed they woul t not have got the setts for anything like the money. He wanted to know how the committee knew that. (Hear, hear.) The committee had no right ti violate a standing order without the consent of the Council. Councillor TINNION said he was sorry so much personal heat had been introduced into the discussion. As a member ot the committee he was proud in tawnier; had a hand in the acceptance of the tenders. He had no interest to serve himself, but the interest's of the town, and there appeared to be semething behind all this. If anybody had anything to say questioning the integrity of the Committee, or his personal integrity, he was prepared to accept the chal- lenge and go to the root of the matter. He could not understand why there should be a strong objection to what was called the breaking of the standing orders. He had been a member of the Council long enough to know that if the standing orders had not been broken many times in the letter they had been in the spirit. The only object they had in view was to purchase setts at a price that was something more like the value. •In March 1899, he believed, there was a ring formed. At that time they used largely Irish setts, and in conse- quence of the ring they agent up 15 per cent to 50 per ! cent.. in price, which was a very considerable item. The effect upen prooerty-owners was that they would have had to pay £2 9s. 101. oer house. The MAYOR. Your comparison is not a fair one,and his Worship explained why that was the case. The Irish sett was a granite sett and the one the committee was using was a gritt sett, and if Councillor Tinnion knew anything ebeutethe difference he would not have made the remark he did. Councillor Trenton said the gritt setts were not ussd for the main streets, but only for the small streets. They had been met by a combination of quarry owners, assisted by a combination of merchants, or their agents, and the committee thought the best way out of the difficulty was to adopt the course which bad been adopted. They knew they were bound to some extent by the standing orders, but it was thought that if they could overcome an Illegal combination it wonkl be to the advantage of the town, the property owners and the inhabitants, as they would have cheaper setts The speaker explained that quarries had been visited—some fresh ones—and pointed out that when it became known they were like'y to purchase largely, some people had gone in before them and bought up the eette and they had had to pay.for what these people called " bucinf es forethought" If they had had to advertise it would have defeated the object they had in view of going out- side the ring.. Councillor A. J. JOLLEY endorsed the spirit with which Councillor Tinnion commenced his remarks, but, per- haps some of them were calculated to promote heat. There was no intention to reflect on the Committee. He knew that several of the members were men of great business capacity, but here was a principle involved, and eve, granting all Councillor Tinnion had eaid, and that a large sum of money bad been saved, still he would oppose it; The question of principle was more important than that ever, of saving money for the rate- payers. Any tampning with the standing orders was fraught with danger, and they would lose by it in thee • long run. (Hear, hear.) Councillor SMETRURST had no idea the Committee were trams:teeming the law. Orders for thousands of setts bad been given and they could not get them ' delivered although frequent cemplaints were being made by the ratepayers that etreets were being neglected. With regard to the standing orders, they had saved the ! ratepayers and the property owners from 39 to 50 per cent. in obtaining the setts in then manner in which they had been obtained. Councillor BENNIITT supported the minutes, although _ . _ _ _</p>
        <p>hp admitted the principle which was involved, and to which reference had been made by Councillor A. J. Jolley ; it was one they could Lot afford to ignore, but everything else being equal they ought to he willing to allow their own tradesmen to have the first chanvs. They ought also to encourage paving with woo ,4 rather than with setts owing to the noise which resulted frem the laying down of the latter. Ccmneiller Jorni ROBERTS thought the Committee had aced wkely in the purchase of the setts in the interest of the ratepayers. He could not help thinking,however, that the standing orders of th.s Corporatiou should be adherf d to, or some day they would be serieusly "landed." They ought to put their foot down, and on these grounds he would support the amendment. Alde-man Htercyrrxson said he ...mid take lll4 "'ln"</p>
