      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>Streets and Back Passages.</head>
        <p>Streets and Back Passages.</p>
        <p>Councillors ELmsos and PEACOCK called the attention of the Paving and Sewerage Committee to the condition of the streets and back passaees in the wards they represented, viz., Latchford and Orford Wards. A large number of streets and passays were mentioned, and ultimately the motions were withdrawn on the promise of the attention by the committee as soon as possible. The Council then rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Discovery of a Well in Sankey Street.</p>
        <p>On Saturday morning'a number of workmen were engaged in taking up the flags forming the foot- path in Sankey-street opposite to Trinity Church for the purpose of laying down the electric cable when they unearthed an old and very deep well, which contained a large quantity of water. It was stated that it was at one time used as a public well, a pump having been erected on the footpath for the purpose of enabling the public to draw what water they required, but the well had been closed for about 35 or 40 years. An Old Woman Burnt to Death.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse conducted as inquest in the Parade Room at the Police Station on Monday' relative to the death of Elizabeth Crook (77), who lived at 25, Catherine-street. Elizabeth Jane Houghton said the deceased was her mother, and was the wife of Robert Crook, a railway porter. Witness lived with her,and on the 13th ult. she left the house, and,returning in ten minutes, found her mother with her clothing on fire, endeavouring to put out the flames. She told her that her clothes became ignited when she was putting some coal on the fire.—Another witness stated that the deceased had informed her that she was holding a piece of paper in front of the flte to act as a "drawer," when it ignited and caused the accident. The death took place on Wednesday evening. A verdict of acci- dental death was returned.</p>
