      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION. LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION. Millom Gain Points at Warrington's Expense.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION. LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION. Millom Gain Points at Warrington's Expense.</p>
        <p>The visit of Warrington to Millom, especially in view of their recent invincible form, attracted all the spare enthusiasts to the Saithouso ground. With Latham ab- sent with a damaged ankle, sustained while at work, rho Millom committee decided to play H Wilson, a smart lad who has recently done good service for the once glorious Askam P.C. This was the only change in both teams. Millom opened aggressively, and repeatedly upset the passing tactics which the Warrington men tried. In one of their invasions the " blues "seemed to have a nice opening, till J Whitehead dranpe,, at goal with no result. Next, the new man, Wilson, traubled Hallam, but at once Marshall and Eigere had 'o drop back and defend the Millom ground. With the Millem backs playing a waiting game, W,rrington came to home greund grandly, but a transformation scene oc- curred when in his own 25 J. Whitehead snapped the leather from an opponent and raced off, with Eigers in atteedance. On reaching Hallam, Whitehead passed to Evers, who sprinted from the 25 line with as sensational a try as was ever scored. Hackett failed at racial. Taylor, by a free kick, sent to Narchmore, who responded well, and Eagers and Hallam exchanged kicks. Millom were gradually driven back, till Lamb: Beecham, and Dunn improved matters by clever play. Whaa Knowla‘, cat oa Hallam in his own 25 things looked better tor Millom Still,they could only muster three points to nothing at half-eitne,ju4t previous to which Fish made an excrl,ent eff-irt • for his side by running three-parts the length of the field. but without avail. The second half opened with tedious kicking exchanges, and Warrington getting the better of these Fish had no difficulty in landing a goal from a place from Bate's mark. With the Millom backs mulling, Warrington did a lot of pressing, but the tackling of the" blu:s " always accounted for any attempts by the speedy Warrington wings. Wry ! revere pressure by the visitors was averted, and Millom got to the other end, where Narthinore lost the ball at the critical moment, and where Leek failed woefully at a drop, the ball being charged. Still the Millom forwards would not be denied, and at the close they were wear- ing their opponents down in pure shoulder work, the play of Scott, Bellh,m, and Dunn bfing especially clever. Wilson made a mark, hut the range proved too great, and the" blues" ran out victors by three points to two. Comments.</p>
        <p>It was not a game which set the average spectator ablaze with enthusiasm. Pwsibly the smarter back team and the speedier team in every depart- ment but half-back, was Warrington, but even this remark is only evidence to the attack whichthey met. In loose play the Warrington forwards were the better, and they had In excellent leader in Antrho, who was very prominent. In pack work the " Wirepullera " tired, aura toward the close the Millom men were distinctly superior, and Bato was not as good as Duckworth, who did all the " donkey " work, but both these "gents" met capable oppinuats in Northmore and Lack, who however might have paid more attention to the men behind them. Fish was a clever threequarter both in kicking and running—in fact all the visitors' quartette were go,ti. On toe home side Wdaon was very obviously suffeTing from lack of experience, but he showed signs of coming on well. At full back Eagers was much in front of Hallam. To take down Warring- ton,r,however is what Bev3lal teams had failed to do for a few weeks previous to Saturday, ar.d if the Millom mcn were rather lucky to win they only got their own back from several engigements against better teams than Warrington during this, their first, season in the Senior Competition.</p>
        <p>The Last Leagu Match. Wigan Thrashed at Wilderspoot.</p>
        <p>Warrington played their last League Match on Mon- day, when Wigan made their postponed visit to the W.irriugton enclosure. The weather wt.s of the best from a footballer's point of vie w.—Coo)l and calm,whilo, the turf could not have been improved upon. Warrington relied upon the team which fled at Millom, and the Wiganers also k rought a crong combination, in- cluding C Wood, late of Warrington. A late kick-off had been arranged, and at 5 p. 1,0. when the ball was set in motion there would be about 2,000 spe3tatora present. Mr. Ashton of Oldham refi.,,,reed, and the teams lined up in the following order.</p>
        <p>Warrington : Hallam, full back; Fish, Isherwood, Jones. auu Elockenh4ll, threequarteis ; Bate and Duck- worth, half-backs • Taylor, llorrison, Boardman, Eden, Swift, Antrobus, gcholtza and MeTeweather for wards.</p>
        <p>, Wigan: A.ndertan, till hack ; Nichelson, Straughan, Wood, and White, threequarters ; Owen and Mideieton, half-backs; Blackburn, Yates, Hthon, Ball, Preacott, Ellison, Rigby and Brown forwards. Jones started for Werriagton and efter the usual ex- changes, the Wi,geners get afo )ting in the home half. Here they carried a number of scrimmages and attacked hotly for ten minutes the Warrington backs having plenty of work to do on the defensive. Oseeu, Wood, and White several times tried to break through, but Isherwood and Fish were watchful, te latter having once to send the ball dead. A mull by Wood was turned to Warriugton's account by Joues, who snapped up the ball and ran well into the Wigan half,where play settled. Fish punted beautifully to the goal where Wigan nad an anxious time, Duckworth elmest scoring. Warrington now attacked, ane their ruperiority in method began to tell. Several well-ju3ged points to the Wigan line were cleared only in the nick of time and it looked as if War- rington's attempts at sciring were going to he fruitless, ' especially after Fish bad made a great effort at the corner. At last Bate broke away in midfield and draw- ing the Wiganers on to Jones he passed to Isher- wood. The latter threw wide to Hockenhall who took the ball at full speed aed scored splen- didly. Fish failed at goal. Warrington continued to press the attack with vigour, An leieoa being grasied in his own quarter, while Hallam finely repelled an attempted invasion by the Wiganers soon after. Bate was instrumental in getting the next try, for he cleverly punted across the field,aud the ball hitting the cross-bar, Duckworth took it over near the posts. Jones kicked a goal. Wood by a good kick and follow up raised the Beige, and Hallam was held near the home line. The defence was sound however, and at half-time, Warring- ton led by 8 points to nil. The teams ceessed over immediately, Wigan re- starting without Brown, who was hurt. Nicholson was the first to get away and showed a clean pair of heels, but lost his chance by passing rather than face Hallam. After Jones had failed at a penalty kick, which were frequent hereabouts, Lherwood broke away from loose play in the centre and feeding Fish well, the latter was able to race over and ground the hall between the posts. Jones easily added another goal. A spell of desultory play followed in the Wigan half, but before the close, Duckworth and Isherwood passed out to Fise,who again outpaced all opposition and scored a brilliant try in the same position as before. Jones again doing the needful. Time came soon after, Warrington being easy wiaiiere by 3 goals 4 tries (18 points) to Wigan nil. Widnes v. Runcorn.</p>
        <p>On each side of the river this match ia looked upon as the Local 13,rby, and is regarded with the keeneet interest. The meeting on Saturday, however, was in- ves:ed with more than usual importance beeeuse of the position held by Runcorn. A victory fur the Cheshire team meant almost certain peseession of the cup, while a defeat would put them on a level with their old time Oldham ; and thousands crossed the bridge from Runcorn in crder to witness the encounter, and the Rail- way Company had a rare haul. There would be quite seven thousend people on the Widoee, greund when tbe teams lined up as follows : Widnes : Batty, full-bace ; Brennan Hewn, Rispao, and Hughes, three-quarters ; Murphy and Donelas, halves ; Aspey, Elampson, Dounelly, Leyeon. Power, Riley, and Emery, forwards. Runcorn : S Houghton full-hack; C Butterworth, J Butterworth,T Warder, andMe4seneer, three-quarters J Jolley and J Faulkner, halves; S Wsler, El Farmer, A Taylor, F Gayter, Reece, 0 Hughes, J Tomlinson, and J Langley, forwards. D Jones and F Darlineton reserves. , It will be obs-rved that the Runcorn team was con- siderably weakened in the back division by the absence of Wilkinson weeEe place was taken by Messenger. Widnee turned out as selected. Rencore won the toss and Aspey started for Widnes in face of a nice breeze. No return was made and Widnes were quickly busy in , Runcorn quar;ers. Jelley and lidughton resumed play to centre, where hostilitiee were confined for some time, neither eide being able to gale, an advantage. At length Runcern invathd Widnes quarters, but Meisenger kick- ing too hard sent the ball dad over the Widnes line. Some fine exchange kicking followed the drop out, and Breenan making a bad mull, Messenger had a chance but he failed to take it. Runcorn now mede 'netters warm for the home team, and from a scrimmage under the pests, Jolley obtained possession but failed te get away. Beam and Brennan combined prettily, but the latter was well tackled and then Hughes saved a Runcorn rush daringly. Runcorn continued to prese and C Buterworth was thrown into touch when dangerous. Widnes forwards by a combined dribble gained much ground but by clever passing Rulieorn batiks ageie pleeed Widnes on the tielenaive, and C Butterworth in attempting to kick across put the ball owrr the dead ball line. Widnes fur.</p>
        <p>VHOLEBILE AGFNTS.—F A. JUNKINS, 199 PADGA. tE, Warrington. J. BAINItS, 91, BUTTER-MARK .EET, Warrington.</p>
        <p>wards again made a combined rush and attacked, Batey dropping at: goal. Messenger, evticse play had been very defective was replaced by F Gayter from the scrim- magers. Houghton eased the pressure and the Runcorn forwards combining', well, placed the Widnes line in clanger, but Rispan saved. Warder securing pos3ession dropped a goal in fine style and his S9CCI3II put new life into the visiting team, who returning to thi attack indulged in a splendid passing movement that ended in C Butter- worth scoring at the corner. Jolley railed at goal, and the interval arrived wiqa Runcorn lea,ling by five points tAnothing. Farmer restarted and Aspey returned,WidneS forwards followed up well and Hou.hton mulling almost under the posts. Aspey obtained polsession' and planted over the line but a " Knock on' negatived th 2 ',lint. Whines maintained their footing in Runcorn quarters and Brennan was nearly over. Batey made a good attempt to erop a goal. Smart pas- sing by the Runcorn backs, in which the brothers Butterworth were conspicuous, put Runcorn in a good poaition and a determined rush by the forwards almost ended in a score. By kick and rush game which the wind aided Wi,lnes relieved their lines and became aggressive hut the defence of the green un's proved equal to all demands. Runcorn forwards once more removed the venue hut Bown saved splen- didly. By a series of kicks Widnes pressed strongly and Douglas got over under the posts but Batey failed to land the goal, but the last named when "time" was rapidly approaching, dropped a capital goal and the match ended in a draw of one goal one try each.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE JUNIOR LEAGUE. Runcorn Recreation v. Runcorn Hornets.</p>
        <p>As the Hornets only required three out of a possible six points to win the championship, great interest was evinced in this encounter by several hundred spectators. In the first half K nyon seored for the Recreation, aed Green for the Hornets. In The vcood half, Johnson dropped a goal. Final:—Horr,es, 1 goil, 1 try ; Recrea- tion, 1 try.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>Latchford Rangers v. St. Helens (AI</head>
        <p>Latchford Rangers v. St. Helens (AI</p>
        <p>_ At Warrington. Du!ing thu first half Latchford at- tacked, and Lowe early on dropped a goal, which proved the only tangible score during the initial half. In the second half, Chrimes was injured on tho Latchford side, but, despite being short handed, the Rangers were the aggressors, and Armitage aAded a further try, followei by one from Bramha, Armitage adding a third try. Firal : Latchford Rangri, 11 point; S. Helens (El), nil.</p>
        <p>OTHER MATCHES. Warrington A v. Fletcher's Engineers. This match was played at 40,We:spool on Saturday afternoon. Both sides placed song teams in the field, when Sudlow started on behalf of Warrington. Fletcher's pressed,and a paes!ng movement by the backs wae spoilt by Lawlestrknocking on. The visitors had a kick at goal, but failed. bailey intereepted a pass, but lack of speed told.Warrington forced the play in the tors' half. A dribble was t•peilt by Fc:rster being offside. Fletcher's again got away, pAgsing fieely, but Forster ran in touch. Warrington came with at rush, Kuight clearing grandly. Again the bemestera got away, and were only just kept out. Play was taken to centre, and after a few minutes Warrington were forced to concede a minor. Antrobus got deer, 130 Ratcliffe hauled hint into touch. Lawless scored for Fletcher's, Ashurat failing at goal. Half ticae :—Fietcher Rosell's one try ; Warrington A, nil. On resuming toe second half Fletcher's went away, but Malres kicked n;cely and re- lieved. Warriegton now took up the ptessure, and Knight repeatedly sent them back by forward rushes. Fletcher's brought play to centre, where the play was chifily confined. Final :--Fletcher's Engineers,one try ; Warrington (A), nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>ASSOCIATMN FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATMN FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. Earlestown Rovers v. Castner Kellner.</p>
        <p>, The return fixture ti,!trieen the above club took place at Earleatown on Saturrlay. The first match played at Runcorn molted in a win for the Rovers by one goal to nil. On Saturday the llovets had all the game and evetuaily ran out easy winntze by four goals to one. LANCASHIRE ALLIANCE. Adlington v. Warrington, _ _</p>
        <p>At Adlingt3n on Saturday. Adlinn were the first t 3 preas, Hargreaves scoring. Wartington were never dangerous in the first half, when the score was :—Ael- lington, two goals; Warrington, none. After the restart Heywood scored a thtrd for Adlington. Adling- ton, three goals ; Wari-ingten, nom.. Crewe Alexandra v. Earlestown. Lancashire League.</p>
        <p>These teams met at Crewe before 2,000 spectators. After Johnson had started tor Earlestown the Crewe for- wares at once made far the visitors' forrre6s, Lockett making two splendid efforts to open aa account for Crewe. Through this pressure ayea and Cattain were kept busy, and no progress oeao made. Good kicks by Thompson Ur midfield saw Crowe atte.cing vigor- ously. Accurate passing by Hoshana arid Brookfield result-2d in the latter inissiin7,f oy inchoo. Another good opening waQ spoiled by Beshain kickaig will cut3ide. Several abortive corneis now HI to Crewe, who hal the full measure of their opponents even at this early stage. After some neat passing by Fas;er and Lockett, the last- named centred acoiira,.ely to Eietha:n, who scored the first goal for Crewe after twenty minot,is' play. Earles- town now strove hard to equalise. hu,.; Cowntry proved a stumbling block to their tiortm. Further scoring took place, the visitors being now overpiayed. -.Foster scored a.gain tor Crewe. Final :—Crewe Alex-in:lra 4, Etrles- town 0.</p>
        <p>Northwich Victoria v. Newton-le-Willows. Warrintor: Ci.!7 S7.ni;</p>
        <p>tie was played Rt ‘v sides rnus Ltreti their full strength. Opening w;11, Nd evtoli proved the aggressors, bat had play lout a favourable opening. A corner kick c,ino O nothing, but clever kicking by Tomlinson and rirrath gave .ILirrocks trouble. was seen. by the N^rthwich forwards, and nth d La it tea de_ u t Capital Ashton Puce was fine, and Northwicti were sent back. Clever play ensued, and Senior kiching jutliciou:Jv passed eventually to muskett, who scored with an oblique shot, Elorrocks having little or no chanco with the shot. Northwich continued to aesurne the iirgressive, and shots by Birkenhead and Tom'inson almost took effect. Newton improved, and Butler and Ellis were prominent in effective work, which harrassed the Cheshire defence. Barber closred well, and the Northwich goal had several narrow escapes. No 'Viol.: continued to attack strongly without any luck. 14, um the free kick Newton shot behind. Newton atadn got within easy reach of gal, hut faikd unmistakeably at close quarters. Twice Newton penalised. tort wild shooting lost favourahlo openings. Dale beat off an at- tempt at invasion by Ellis,w hi's., an unproductive corner was forced by Morrisey. Northwich &amp;feuded splendidly, and several clearances toy Baaker were applauded to the echo. Newton utilised a free kck but poorly, but some el ver passing in front of goal lost a favouraNe opening. Northwich were next penalised, and B ‘rker again effected a fine clearance. Premature work by Tomlin- son lost his side ground. F,Juls were frequent, and once Tomlinson almost registered a second goal for the le card foe cr s ton Wright but and Dale were dooc Nf!-.rton continued their weak attack in front of goal. Fialf-time—Narth- pi N peewrsto°nnifiimedPiro u tarieLd,r wich, 2 goals ; ,Newton, nil. Etial—Northwich Victoria 3, Newton nil.</p>
