      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <p>T ;. E EX ANIt. N Eli.</p>
        <p>MARCH rTM, 1900.</p>
        <p>THE ease with which British Complacency this country is raising and Expenditure. the money required for the war is much admired on the Continent. At the sanae time, it is being said that we too lightly estimate the actual cost, and the sacrifices we shall have to make in future to maintain our mili- tary position. The idea is to destroy the com- placent feelings which prevail in official circles,and to remind us that money is not everything in war. These suggestions are likely to do us some service. , 'he prevailing tone of optimism in finaLcial circles seems to be hardly warranted. We shall Probably find that the war will cost more money than the CHANCELLOR of the EXCHEQUER has esti- mated. It is nearly always the case. The Abys- sinian War, as far as our memory serves, was the Only military undertaking covered by the esti- mate. It will be said that Lord KITCHENER did not present us with a very big bill for our Part in the Soudan Campaign, and that even the gYptian Government came out of the affair bet- ter than was expected. In each of these cases there was a collapse ot opposition more dramatic than could have been foreseen, and hence the ex- penditure was restricted in time, and therefore in amount. On the present occasion there has never been any sound anticipation of a collapse,though in war with an irregular and undisciplined body the Point of collapse may be reached in the most un- expected manner. The complacency of the British t_ax-Payer is due,probably tolthe general view he has be able able to take of the resources of the country and the CHAN- CELLOR artful way in which the CHAN- of the Exchequer has drawn his Picture of our prosperity. Of course, we can bear the cost of a big war; bot it is foolish to imagine that when we are taxing ourselves twelve millions More in one year, and borrowing large amounts as well, everything will be proceeding much the same as before. Thera mast be some disturbance to trade. There will be less money to spend if the Devenue is to reach the height assigned to it, and there will be less money to invest in commercial enterprise if we lend it to the Government to be wasted (as far as reproduction is con- cerned) in military operations. Hence in twelve months' time we shall be more Pinched than we are now. If rigid economy in the consumption of the commodities taxed for the war be 19rgely practised, the revenue antici- pated will not be earned, and the money will have to be found in some other way. We must also re- member that war haves behind it higher expendi- ture, apart from what is spent in actual fighting slid transport. The pul4lic complacency on this Point is most remarkable. Credulity is even reach- ing the ex:-,reme of foolishness. We fear the gold inines have something to do with the matter. We forget that the destruction of private property Will also bave to be paid few by somebody, and by the two Republics which have caused it. Hence it may he some years before the normal level is reached, and peace will warrant home Budgets of moderate propertions. Meanwhile, large social reforms will be hung up.</p>
        <p>Tuu advance of our troops The Progress iu South Africa is having of the War. some effect in bringing ehanges that are interesting, if they are not de- cisive. General Bur.r.F.R reports that the Boers have now evacuated Natal. Some have gone to the Free State by Van Reenan's Pass, others have taken the railway into the Transvaal to go to the tree State,and others would seem to have entered the Transvaal by a more westerly road, in order to escape the quicker. At Ladysmith there will be I,°lne changes. The civil residents have mostly 'eft the place. and some of the garrison have been conveyed to the Mooi Valley to recruit. The re- Pair of the railway will take time, and, in the Illanwhile,supplies are being pushed on as rapidly as Possible. New guns, fresh ammunition, and I.lll°re troops will be needed. General BULLER, 7 his advance to Van Reenan s, is al- eady in concert with General ROBERTS In the further prosecution of the campaign. n Cape Colony the Boers are slowly retiring, pressed by three of our columns, and Free Staters, and the Dutch who joined them, will have to cross the Orange River as best they can. The Dutch residents, who were much inclined to remain pas- Siva until they saw which side was going to win, have now, apparently, made up their minds that the Boer cause will not triumph. A few bafids of Boers are out in the neighbourhood of Britstown and further north and wes;, seeking more to obtain recruits than anything else. Lord Roberts has had another bout with the enemy and again came off best, utterly routing them from their fortified camp on the Modder River. There does I.:C't Seem much likelihood of his march To_ 1310erefontein being long or seriously delayed. "`-'I3IIItT is believed to be somewhere in the Free State, and the conference between Presidents I"rsma and STEYN has not lead to any overtures for peace. It looks as if an effort were being made to reach Colonel BADEN-POWELL, and to clear out</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <p>parties of disaffected residents between the Vaal and Maf eking. Colonel PLUMER appears to be making his way southward. Hence, at every point where the Boers and the British are in touch there are preparations for further fighting, which can hardly fail to be of more decisive character, than the engagements of the last few days. Our reinforcements of horse, foot, and artillery seem to be pouring in and to be despatched to varicus points with a promptness and secrecy that is most commendable. So complete has been the change in the progress of the war that even the critics on the Continent, military and political, begin to perceive that success on our side will soon become decisive and undeniable. The skill and energy of our generals are being evoked in no com- mon degree, and we are learning from the Boers the lessons which will enable us to act with more caution, with less loss, and with more hope of obtaining the advantage of numbers and discipline The Boers must now begin to feel the pressure of the five months war alike in the diminution of their numbers and the exhaustion of their resources. They are short of money, if their ammunition holds out. Their com mandos must be effected by the wasting effects of war, and th° new campaign is to decide the fate of the Orange Free State. We can trust Lord Roberts to act in his own way, and in his own time, and with quite as brilliant results as have hitherto followed his combination of numbers and strategy. We may have no hope of immediate and final battles. But the war is making very substantial and satisfac- tory progress.</p>
        <p>It is to be hoped that the weather conditions will be favourable during the Queen's visit to her capital. In no way except by demonstration and decoration can her Majesty's subjects show their deep appreciation of the personal sacrifice she has undergone for their benefit. It is no light matter for an aged lady accustomed to warm climates at such a time to brave the inclemency of an English spring, and at the same time add to the enormous amount of work attached to her exalted position. The London public will be eager to welcome the Queen among them. That there will be numbers of sightseers from the provinces during the short visit is also probable,and we may expect to hear of a reception to the most venerable crowned head in the world as genuine, if not in point of number as great, as that which attended the triumphal pro- gress on Jubilee Day. We all trust that no harm will accrue daring the Queen's exposure to the cold March winds. There is a certain risk aqached to the expedition, and her Majesty's medical advisers were only fulfilling their duty in pointing it out. But in affairs intimately connected with the nation the Queen has never hem one to consider her personal safety. Everyone will hope that the weather will be bright, and that the welcome afforded by London's teeming millions will give pleasure to the august recipient.</p>
        <p>Warrington has quickly followed in the wake of its near neighbours—Manchester anl Liverpool—. in opening a fund for the relief of our famine- stricken fellow croatures in India. It would in- deed have been regrettable if we had allowed the relief funds in connection with the South African War to completely dry up the springs of public charity. Of India's need at thepresent time there can be no possible doubt. The extent of the famine is unprecedented and the heartrending re- ports which have already reached us must have ; touched cords of sympathy in the hearts of every' Englishman -who has read them. At the present moment there are millions of our Indian subjects depending entirely upon the Government for their daily sustenance, and as was pointed out by the Rev. C. H. Hocken at the meeting in the Town Hall last week, they will require some financial aid when they leave the famine camps to enable them to make a fresh start and buy seed and bullocks. On the occasion of the last famine Warrington rosponded nobly to the call for assis- tance and contributed £1,160 to the relief fund. The town has now-a larger constituency and con- sidering the good trade which has prevailed there should be no difficulty in raising a like amount, despite the fact that we have contributed hand- somely to the war relief fund.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Board of Guardians does some very remarkable things at times. Last week the Board distinguished itself by refusing to grant a modest increase in the subscription to the War- rington District Nursing Association. It was shown that the subscription now paid to the Association was really not expenditure at all, seeing that the services of the nurses saved the Union from affording relief in cases which would otherwise re- quire assistance. But the Board began to quibble about an alleged grievance that the Nursing Association did not extend its operations through- out the whole union, and that it was therefore un- fair to call upon the outlying townships to contri- bute towards the precious ten pound note which is sent from the Wa,rrington Union. The fact that the whole union benefitted by the Associa- tion was entirely overlooked and the re- commendation to increase the subscription was rejected. This cheeseparing policy, it must be remembered, is the action of a Board which has been spending the ratepayers' money with both hands in the erection of a new hospital. By the time this Institution is ready for the recep- tion of patients it will be found that the expendi- ture has been nearly £40,000. A more typical case of straining at a gnat and swallowing a camel, we have not seen for some time.</p>
        <p>THIS DAY, SATURDAY! THIS DAY, SATURDAY! THIS DAY, SATURDAY! THIS DAY, SATURDAY!</p>
        <p>BOROUGH OF WARR,INGCON,</p>
        <p>ARTS AND GRAFTS EXEIIBITION.</p>
        <p>PARR HALL, AND SPECIAL ANNEXE, M&amp;RCH 3RD, TO 24TH.</p>
        <p>His worship the Br. iyor, Alderman H. Roberts. Req., J.P. will open the Exhibition at 3 o'clock, this day Saturday. -</p>
        <p>ARTS: INVENTIONS, MANUFACTURES,</p>
        <p>ENGINEERING TRADES.</p>
        <p>THE PROBYN LADIES' ORCHESTRA. GALLERY OF ARTS, COOKERY LECTURES, AMERICAN MIDGETS, MRS. GENERAL TOM TFILTMB, THE BROTHERS MAGRI, CINEMATO- GRAPH, MADAM ISIDORE (FROM BARNUM AND BAILEY S U.), AND NUMEROUS OTHER ATTRACTIoNS. SPECIAL INSTALLATION OF ELECTRIC LIGHT. OPEN DAME 2 Tru. 13. ADMISSION SIxPENCE.</p>
        <p>SEASON TICKETS 3s. 61. AT MOT'S BRIDGE STREET, WARRINGTON. N.B. 'BUSES LEA.VE THE{ EXHIBITION BUILDINGS, NIGHTLY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <p>T" appearance of that unique little" Diary and Corn panion " issued by co. G. BUSSHY &amp; 00 , for the use o: Cricketers (which may be obtained front ::.ports Dealers Booksellers, or gailway Eook,talls for 6d), r, minds us of the coming season, in anticipation of which Geo. G. Bussey &amp; Co., have Prepared a " record " stock of Bats. We are told that the timber they hold for Bat making alone exceeds 1.000 tens. " Glorious Cricket follows th,3 British Flag, every where."—Advt. (1)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>MR EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MARCH 9, 1900.</head>
        <p>MR EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MARCH 9, 1900.</p>
        <p>1 RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>PAROCHIAL ROOMS, WIDNES. Mr James Bough,presided over a moderate attend- ance at the meeting held in the above rooms on Saturday evening. Daring the evening the vicar, the Rev. C. E. Stocks, delivered an instructive gospel address, which was greatly e,nioyed. The programme which was an excellent one, was carried out by the secretaries, and proved very in- teresting. BETHESDA CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH. On Monday evening, a social evening, conducted entirely by gentlemen, was held in the tiohoolnorn under the presidency of Mr. J. J. Norman, .1 P. There was a very large attendance. Tae programme was of a novel and interesting character. The first part was contributed by the Runcorn Glee Party, who splendidly maintained their reputation for beautiful and effective singing. after a short address from Mr. Norman and an interval for re- freshments, the sec,ind part of the programme was rendered by the Milton Congregational Church Minstrel troupe. DUTTON CHOOL BOARD At the monthly meeting of the Dutton School Board, held in the Board-room,Dutton, on Saturday there being present—the Rev L D Lyon Bennett (chairman), Messrs John Kinsey, Z S Blllin&amp;iton,and ilerbert Potts, the Clerk reported the death of the School Attendance Officer, Mr. John Spender. After some discussion, Mr. Potts gave notice that he would at the next meeting move the appointment of a successor, with a salary at the rate of ..£5 per annum. NARROW ESCAPE FROM BURNING. Last Sunday afternoon, a woman observed smoke issuing from the kitchen of a house in Water-street, Widnes, occupied by Thomas Bird, labourer at Widnes Foundry. She immediately rushed across the road and into the house, where she observed Bird sitting in front of the fire with his clotning smouldering. The fire was extinguished and the unfortunate man was co., veyed to the Accident Hoepital, where he was attended by Dr. Hutchin- son.</p>
        <p>BURGLARS AT WIDNES STATION. About midnight on Thursday the Central Station was visited by burglars. At two am. on Friday P.C. Evans discovered that the place had been entered. The thief or thieves hid burst open the drawers,bat failed to find money,which it is natural to suppose they were in search of. They however, abstracted the money from the automatic machine. and also took toe sweetmeats and cigarettes. Tuis is the fourth time that the station has been broken into, and despite the vigorous enquiries made by the police up to the present they have been unable to trace the whereabouts of the thieves. A BOY Roes Hee FATHER. Leonard G. Sergeant, a young boy, of Welling- ton-street, was brought up on remand at Runeora Petty Sessions, last Monday, charged with the lar- ceny of a pair of boots valued 133 the property of his father.—The prisoner's mother, and Acting- Sergeant Gregory repeated the evidence given at the previous hearing.— It was also stated that the boy woald not have been proceeded against but for the fact that he would not tell his parents what he had done with the boots.—Prisoner stated he gave the boots to another boy. who had pawned them.— This was borne out by Mr. Caradic, pawnbroker, who said he had taken the boots produced in pledge bat hey were not pledged by the prisoner.—The Bench severely reprimanded the boy and ordered to receive six strokes of the birch. ORDER OF DRUIDS, RUNCORN.—NIL DESPERANDUM LODGE (WARRINGTON • ISTRICT BRANCH. A .` smoker" was held by the above lodge in their clubroom at Brunswick-street, Runcorn, on Satur- day, to celebrate the official visit of the district officers. The usual lodge business was got through very early to enable members and frienne being ad- mitted to the social, which was presided over by the District Grand Master, C Bsbbington, who was supported by tee Deputy Distvict Grand Master, E Allen, and other district officers. The Grand Master expreised the pleasure it gave him and his colleagues to see so many present, and trusted all would thoroughly enjoy the well prepared programme he had before him, and finally result in some of the youag men present joining the lodge. Songs were contributed by Messrs. Righey, Harrison. Wilson, Barlow, Thompson, Lamb, &amp;c. Bro Finch presided ' at the piano. The D.C.S. spoke of the enjoyable evening spent, and was sure that all were thoroughly satisfied with the manner the members had contributed to their amusement. The social side of the friendly secieties, when conducted upon lines of taste and forethought, conduced very r uch to the advancement of lodges and districts, and played an impertant part in advancing the whole of the interests that build up euch institutions of usefulness. In appealing for a new member a very hearty response was made. Votes of thanks were given to the vocalists, and to the landlady for the use of piano and large room. PRESENTATION AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The ugual fortnightly meeting of the "Rose of Sharon " G.U.O. was held in the Foresters' Hall, Runcorn, on Slturday evening, Bro George Parr in the chair. Alter the transaction of the routine busi- ness, an interesting presentation took place. The committee, in order to induce the younger members to take an intelligent interest in the working of the lodge decided to presAit to the brethren who pass through the chairs an illuminated and framed emblem, in recognition of their two years' services. Bro John Palin Parr has just completed two years' valuable services, and Bro Jones, on behalf of the lodge, in a speech full of recognition of the good qualities of Bro Parr, and expressing high appre- ciation of the members for his services and their best wishes for his future welfare, presented him with the emblem. The recipient suitably thanked the members for their present. Bro Peacock initi- ated an interesting discussion as to the relative powers of the lodge and district and Bro Davies replied. The sick experience of the lodge is at the present time heavier than it has ever been in the past, owing to the influPnri epidemic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>HARSH FATHER IMPRISONED.</head>
        <p>A HARSH FATHER IMPRISONED.</p>
        <p>Case of Cruelty to Children at Weston Point.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Bench of magistrates, at the weekly Sessions, on Monday last, dealt with a peculiarly dis trest,ing case of cruelty and neglect of children,in which the defendant was Joseph Peach, of 28, Sandy-lane, Wes- ton—From the evidence submitted by the pelice, (who conducted the case on behalf of the neglected children), it appeared that there were three children—Mary 15. John 11, and Joseph 6—whom the defendant had ill- treated. Some two years ago the defendant's wife died, and an elder daughter (aced 18) who was then in service came home to keep house for the father. His treatment of her was, howecer, such as to cause her to have and return to service. From thence onward Mary (15) had taken the part of mother. At the time she entered upon the responsibility she could not have been more than 13, so, naturally, it was net to be ex- pected that things would be kept up to standard. The father, who was of drunken habits, found fault with the girl, and oft-times thrashed her few what he termed " not doing what she was told." Then when short of money, he used to compel her to go and pawn things, or else "pick cinders" which the children used to dispose of far 3J to 51 a bag t the neighbours. When foot was brought into the house, the man used to take the lion's share and give the children but a scanty allow- ance, so that they continually complained to the neighbours of having no food, or at any rate not suflicient. 'lbis treatment whilst there was food in the house %P. 2 cruel ; but the height of cruelty was reached when the poor childrea " stole "as the defendant termed it " a crust from the cupboard," for which offence they got " lickel by him " with a cruel thin strap.—John, tho 12 year old bey,who gave his evidence, clearly and fearlessly, said he and his brother were often thrashed by his father for not getting up at six o'clock to go and " pick cinders ;" and he had been often sent to school without breakfast as a further punishment.—Defendant to the Bench: Yes,he has been sent to school without bretkfast; but there was a reason for that —The Chairman : You ought to be ashamed of yourself, standing there, and owning to sending the little fellow to school without breelefast. You should be ashamed of yourself !—Further evidence was given by Mrs. Ann Sutton, and Mrs. Mary Bosteck, neighbours, of the complaints of the children, and of hear- I ing them scream when th melted be the defect- (hut, with the leather thoeg ---Cheestables Wylie and Bencroft also confirmed the a 4 character given to defendant by the previous witneetes, and the latter added that some 8 months peevieusly he had in company with the N.S.P.C.C. leepector cautioned the defendant about neglecting his children. The edest girl had come to him and asked could not s-imeehing be done to her father who WWI SO ootinoally illtrea,ing them —Defend- ant elected to be summarily dealt with, hut offered no defence beyond stating that he had only " licked the children for the perpeseof correcting them when they did not do what they were told." The Bench said they had given the case thebest cenAderation they could,and thought it a very had one. In the hp, however, that the defendant would reform aed .peor.n his duties as a parent that ought to do in the future, they would not inflict the full term of imprisonmeot. Defendant would be sent to gaol for six vve,ks with hard labour ; though be was liable to slx months imprieonmeht and a heavy I fine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>The umoue is pretty current, in well informed circles, that the result of the Electrical Engineer's report, submitted re..:ently to the Runcorn Urban District Council, will be the shel dug of the project for at lea.,3t a time. Some of the members of the Council think the time for the report to be made public is not quite opportune; but when they can see that the public are being fed by such rumours as: " The Council being afraid to face a £3OO de- ficit, on the first year's working of the scheme " surely, they will see it is time to let the public fully into their confidence. I give the rumour for what it is worth.</p>
        <p>How are our local affairs progressing? is a query that comes surprisingly smooth from my pen. But to find expressive words to athquately and cun- eisely reply to myself is a much more difficult matter. It waii, perhaps, a workingman friend whom I met the other day who reminded me of the fact that Runcorn is still a long way behind as a Municipal body; but, whether it was he, or some- one else, matters little. Sufficient be it, that I was reminded, and asked too to reminu our Councillors. I was also asked to state that the election of Urban District Councillors takes place on Saturday March 31st.</p>
        <p>The latest Runcorn creation "rhe Ratepayers' As- sociation " ; have now the opportunity of exercis- ing their limbs as it were. Tne opportunity, the Association has, for testing its Mrength, is, without doubt, a golden one; and three or four candidates, pledged to them and thrown out now would settle the question "Shall Runcorn have a Rateoayers' As- sociation ?" far more satisfactorily than it has been settled hitherto. With a good policy and goad can- didates—(pledging some of the retiring gentlemen would suffice)—much might be accomplished. Have they courage enough to try.</p>
        <p>The explosion caused by burning naphna, petro- leum, and benzoline, at Runcorn. last Wednesday brought to light a most reprehmsible practice which is also a public danger and m.mace ;—That of burning wuste paper in open spaces.—The lesson we have had should teach us as a community to be more careful in future and take immediate steps for our protection. We are faced with the great diffi- culty of tracing these strange happenings to their source; therefore the inhabitants of the neighbour- hood where such practices are common, should keep a watchful eye on each other if life and property are to be safe.</p>
        <p>Dr. McDougall has figured in a good many of the thrilling incidents that go to make up Runcorn's history. But, perhaps, the most novel experience he has yet had was that of Wednesday's when be notibly,if somewhat forcibly, took temporary com- mand of the operations for the extinguishing of the fire. He it was who caused soil and rubbish to be heaped upon the burning barrels with such good eff ct; and with his sound commonsense he directed operations in such a manner as won admiration fro -a everyone.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>WESTON POINT TO WARRINGTON BY ELECTRICITY.</head>
        <p>WESTON POINT TO WARRINGTON BY ELECTRICITY.</p>
        <p>The Electric Lighting of Runcorn.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Public Will doubtless be pleased ta learn, that the investment on their behalf, of the Urban District Council, in the Provisional Order for Lighting the District with Electricity, is likely after all to prove a great boon. The represeutaAve of the proposed Electric Rtilway from Warring- ton to Weston Point has recently had an interview with the Urban District Council, the result of which has been to put a far brighter aspect upon the lighting question. From what I can glean, the Railway Company make very handsome off irs to the Runcorn Public, and are prepared t take over their Provisional Order, and work the lighting of the district for the Council iii con- junction with the proposed railway. The Company also give the District Council the option of laying its own mains, erecting lamps, standards, etc., the company supplying them with the necessary cur- rent at so much per trade unit. The figures quoted are said to be most advantageous, and the oppor- tunity of accepting the company's offer is in all probability going to be grasped.—The interview coming so immediately upon the expnrt's report which, by the way, showed the Council could only work a scheme themselves at a considerable loss— has fully proved the value of expert evidence, for the Council as a body now fully realise the marketable value of their Provisional Order and can be trusted to do the best deal for the town. The plans of the proposed railway will be deposited by the Cimpany with the Council in a few days,in the meantime they distinctly want the public to understand that the proposed line terminates at Weston Point, and not at Weston. This latter fact is by far the mot interesting to Runcorn, as the opening out of that growing centre of industry is a difficulty they have been trying to solve for a considerable time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>ALARMING EXPLOSION AT RUNCORN. A Sewer Blown Up With Naphtha.</head>
        <p>ALARMING EXPLOSION AT RUNCORN. A Sewer Blown Up With Naphtha.</p>
        <p>Exciting Scenes. About one o'clock, on Wednesday, the inhabitants of , Runcorn, especially in the vicinity of High-street, were thrown into a state of consternation, upon hearing a terrific explosion. Investigation, showed that an alarming outbreak of fire was raging in a lean-to-ahsd, at the back of Messrs. Lea's and Son's oil shop, High-street, in vslactt several barrels of petroleum ; three of naphtha. ; and a can of beazeline were stored ; and that the naplr.he getting into a sewer in the vicinity had exploded with disastr- ous results. From what we can glean, the fire orieinateel in a peculiar manner illustrative of the dangers of burning waste paper in open epaces. It appears that a boy, (William Warren), employed at the oil-shop, went to the shed, at about quarter to (me o'clock, for the purpose of drawing som banzaline from a can. Whits measuring the oil, he saw a lighted piece of paper wafted over the wall, from the direction of an adjoining yard. He was frightened by the paper alighting upon his cap, and in his excitement brushed it rapidly off, and was terrified to behold the fiery missive fall into the benzeline. The flames from this alarmed him still more, and he tried to remove the burning vessel further from the oil-barrels with his foot; but, in doing so, kicked it over. Thus matora were made worse. Then, in his ignorance, but with the best of intentions, the brave little fellow got a bucketful of water, and dashed it upon the flames, adding to their fury. He then ran off and spread the alarm. Whilst he was away the fire spread with alarming rapidity, and the fiery liquid percolate I into a large sewer, which connects Haztehursee scapery with the town's main sewer. The result was that the naphtha, rapidly decomposing in so confined a space, went off with a fearful expl Ain's. The sewer was cracked for a considerable distance; a large gap about four feet square was formed in it at the seat f concussion; and the back yard of Messrs. D 11 Je ies and Sons, drapers. (which adjoins that of Lea's), was literally wrecked. That the explosion was no little one, may be gathered from the fact that the sewer was about eight feet below the surface of the ground, and the large flagstones with which the yard was paved were upheaved and bored hiebelp into the air, and smashed into atoms upon their again reaching the ground ; a large rain water butt was also lifted bodily, and completely overturned. The hack window of Lea's premises was shattered inwards, and many panes of glass splintered. The general a ppearaneo of tnat section of the premises was somewhat appading, and out of the gap in the ground belched forth vivid forked flames of fire. Assistance soon arrivsd, the first oe the scene being Geerge Smith (assistant to Messrs Lea's, the proprietors of the shop). The heroic young fellow at once rushed into the blazing shed with shirt sleeves turn ,0 up, and manfully wheeled out of the reach of the ft mes, as many barrels as remained intact. Then hs rapidly set to -vork demolishing the woo lon shed. Messrs Jones, Dr McDougall, Meeirs James Lea (Temperance Hetet), W B Britnelow ; the Fold-eta (under Superintendent D Pearson) ' • the Runcorn litre &amp;egad() (under Superie- tendent Robert Wright) ; a large body ef Mr Lea's work- men, and a large number of civeiees h ii pined Soil and rubbish were rapid,y h ape e u oa the flames ; the hoes-pipe was run through Mr J eses' seep ready to cope with continsencieethou d they ar ss.; an I,in about halt an hour, 90 well w as the w irk carried out, the fire had been completely sub-bed. The prompt action taken alone averted much more serioes con,equencPs. was, 3 !!arrnts of p-troleutn, 1 of naphtha, and half a can of beneetine were com- pletely lost,besides the sitar:etc by the explosion, and the les; of the shed. • What wAuld have happened had anyoae of Mr. Jones' family 13,en in the yard at the time is dilfisult to iearinies but, of a eurWy, had Mrs Jones, who had Pest reterned to the hou-e in the yard to see the caollwra- tion, she wc:uld uncloutycdly have-been eery seziou3ly injured. The origion of the.fire, is, of course, traceable to the cause stated above ; end it would ,1)e very intereatila to know from whence " that bit of paper" eves wafted.</p>
        <p>Upon close inquiry from neightours our reporter !carnt that the practice of burning waste paper and such like produce at the back of the several business premises in the vicinity of the fie is pretty general, and they told him ol: more than one fire supposed to have originated in the same way. oir representative himself Law the charred remains of pieces of recently burnt paper in several parts of the AA -meat premises. This shows that in all likelibood th.t little boy's story is perfectly true. If so, starkly, for the prl- tection of the community at large, steps should be taken te prevent a recurrence of such an event.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>NEWTON URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>NEWTON URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Council was held in Vac Town Hall, Earlestown, on Monday afternoon. Mr CBF Brron, J.P., C.C. presided and there were also present Dilessrs W Collme;wooi, J W Emmett, J.P., J Simpson, W Thompson, E T Evans, W Beattey, r Ormand, with Mr \V W Snirley (clerk), Mr A BOWEIi (surveyor), and Mr D fi Lloyd (gas engineer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>WAR IN SOUTH AFRICA.</head>
        <p>THE WAR IN SOUTH AFRICA.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said hs thought ic was his duty to con- gratulate them on the good news they received last week from Lila seat of war. The news was received with great acclamation by the coun:ry and he thought they too showed their appreciation of the efforts of Lord Roo:3rt.s and General Buller in their successful work. He trust: d that it was the precursor of peace and that they woulil soon see South Africa a prosperous country again. ABSENCE OF MR. STONE</p>
        <p>The Chairman said he had retnived a telegram from Mr. Stone at Bournemouth regretting hi i inability to be present.</p>
        <p>GAS AND WATER COMMITITE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of this committee it was reiolved that the engineer be empowored to examine and repair, and replace on the guides the No. 1 gasholder at the gas- works ; and that the engineer be authorised to give the undertaking required by Mr. Leslie with regard to the C)uncil laying gas mains across the book bridge near to Mr. Boardman's Mill, i e., to make good any damage and maintain in repair for six months afterwards. Letters from Messrs R Evans an,l Co Jolla Stee and Co., A W Anderton, and Messrs R Walton and Co., with reeard to the purchase of coke at the gasworks were sub- mitted,and it was aftirwards resolved that no further cu- tracts for the sale of ceke be entered into at the present time. A letter dated the 3rd February, was read from the Yulcen Foundry, Limited, with regard to the vaticy of gas, and it was ordered that the matter be referred to the Gasworks Extension Committee. It was resolved that it be a recommendation to the C mucil to appoint Mr. J. T. Eloggard, of H,lifax, to the position of gas- works foreman, at a wage of £2 23 per week, with house, coal, gas, water, and rates free. A lezter dot the 25tn January, was read from Mr T Mather, of S tad- Robb Farm, Lewton, asking for compansation for a watercourse made by the Council on land in hie occupa- tion adjoining the site for waterw.alta. It wis ordered that the Chairman and the Clerk be empowerA to s:ittle the matter. The Chairman moved and Mr. Ormand secluded tlvi adoption of the minutes which were agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>HIGHWAYS AND BUILDINGS toIiNIITTEE.</head>
        <p>HIGHWAYS AND BUILDINGS toIiNIITTEE.</p>
        <p>This committee resolved ttiat the following huildine plans be approved and passel, viz. William Melling, two houses, Market-street,Eerlestown; Vaicen Foundry, Limited, alterations to shop, No. I, Maucheeter Row, The Surveyor submitted his estimitee of toe cost ce: making the new public road from Crow-lane to War- grave-road, with setts, the stern of £3.1.81. 95 81., and with macadam, the sum of of £2,320 121- It was re- solved that the Council be recommended to make the said road with macadam, arid kerbed and channelled on each side and one cindered footpath, with the necessary fences, as per the Surveyor's specifilation and estimate of £2,320 12.5. The Clerk read lettels from Messrs. Houseman and Co., solicitors to the Right Hon. Lord Newton, stating that they proposed to insert in the agreement with the Council for the proposed new public road, th it the work shmli be completed within two years. Tee Clerk read his replies thereto, from which it app2ared that he had endeavoured to inenc- them to agree that the completion should b 3 withhe nee year of tle receipt of the sanction of the Leese GOV.'Cil- zneut Board to the Isere) wing by the Distriet Geoncil of the amount required ; whereapon it was ordered the'. the Clerk again endeavour to induce Massre Housemen to agree to his suggestion,aud failing this,the Chaim in end kise Clerk to interview Ildr.Leslie.—A le ter da el the 13-h Feb:uary. was read from Mr. A. C. Leslie, in answer to the Cierk'e letter, stating that he had no power to aen'e to the deviation of the heel of sewer from Mill-lane to the Central Oatfall S.twera Wo-ks unless a new deed was executed an I freda piens prepare 1. It was resolved that the chairm an, the vice-eheirm en and Mr. Stone tale, an early opportunity of intervinwieg Mr. Leslie with referenee to this matter. With regard to the objection lodged by Mr. Parties-, to the makine of that part of Flaydock-street which is unbuilt upon and which had been deterred from the 'net meeting f farther consideration, it was resolved that no further steps be taken with reference thereto. The surveyor submitted his estimate for the continuation of Vevluct-1 street from Legh-street to Market-street, i e., fencine off e. strip of land 36 feet wide,and forming a clndered path six feet wide on one side. Eatireete, £9O. It was re selved that tendera be invited for the same. A further letter, dated 11th February, was read from the Local Government Beard with reference to the salary of the Inspector of Nuisances, and rrglesting the Council to sonsider the matter, and stating that the Board were of epinioa that the initial salary snonld not be less than 11100 per annum. It was resolve I that the Couecil be Heomtnended that the salary of the Ftsp eater of Nuia- ances, be £lOO per annum, and that the Local Govern- ment Beard be informed accordingly. A letter dated the 24± January, was read from Mr. John Gre4orv,thn then toll cellecterireporting Mr. 1 homes Burke,haker,of Peckers Hill. Sutton, her refusing to pay toll for hawk- ing bread in this district. This was cnrasiderei to be a clear case of a breach of the local act and it was resolved that the matter be referred to Messrs. Peace and Ellis, solicitors. with instructions to take legal proceedings if thee-•_consider the Council have a good case.</p>
        <p>Atla meeting of the committee on Mondar, February, 26, a letter was read from the Luncasoire County Coun- cil enclosing an extract from the report of the County Medical Officer of Health with regard to the effluent at the Newton Sewage Works on the oecasion of his visit in December, 1899, and asking the District Council to, very carefully consider the question with a view to steps being taken to meetthe obj-ction raised by the County Medi3al OfEcer.—lt was ordered that the Clerk reply thereto on the lines now suggested by the cemmittee.— A letter was read from Messrs. Houeeman and Co.. solicitors to Lord Newton, with reference to the period of completion of the proposed new pubic road. The matter was referred to Councillors Borron, J.P., Colling- wood and Stone, J.P. rhe minutes were approved on the motion of Mr. Collingwood. GENERAL PURPOSE 3 COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The sub-olmmittee apoointr.d for the °urn's() reported with regard to the Bill of the Lancashire Electric Power Gmapany, that they desired instructions to engage (if, after further consideration, it was deemed advisable) Messrs. Peace and Ellie, solicitors, to oppose the Bill. It was resolved that the sub-committee be empowered ac- cordingly. The questiln of reline' ef ground values was again brought forward, and, after a discussion, it was deferred to tha next ordinary meeting of this cornruittse.</p>
        <p>ThP Chairman moved the confi-rn ttioa of the mill,es Mr. Evane geeendel and the minutea were approvel</p>
        <p>REPRESENTATIVE GOVERNORS</p>
        <p>Mr Simpson moved the appointment of Mr Enamel Mr Ormand, Dr Watkins and Mr Borrun, as repreAenta- tire governors on the governinE.i., body of the Win wick Granamar &amp;hoot and Dean School Exhibition. Mr o;mand said a mietaka had been made. It was the appointment of governors for the Winwick Occa- sional Foundation, which incluled the Winwick Grammar School, Dean School Exhibition and the annual grant from tt4e Lowton endowed school. Mr Ecans se londei and the proposition was agreml to. This was all the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Local Doctor's Windfall.</head>
        <p>A Local Doctor's Windfall.</p>
        <p>Charles Ernest Richmond. a medical man who has practised at Warrington and at Chorlton-on- Medlock, Greenheys, King-street and Lime Grove Manchester, came up for public examination in bankruptcy before Mr. Registrar Atkinson at the Nlanchester Bankrupcy Court on Monday. In answer to Ar. C. J. Dibb, the Official Receiver, the debtor said that he had £12.000 left to him by his uncle about 13 years ago, and had expended that money in household and personal disbursements: Re had also lost money in ,hares. on a secret pro- cess for waterproofing and for strengthening cotton goods, and on an automatic timing machine. Re had disclosed unseeureri liabilities amounting to .2391,and assets to realised £9O, or a deficiency. Re attributed his insolvency to the matters he had mentioned and to the extravagance of his late wife, his expenditure on relatives, loss of profits owing to his own and his wife's illness, and losses on pro- perty in Chorlton-road, Flulme. When he filed his petition there were nine actions pending against him, and also jurlmnents, but he was now hoping to meet his creditors' demands by a scheme under which they would receive 7s. 6 in the pound.— The examination was adjourned with the view to the debtor's scheme being proceeded with.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. BOOTIIROYD. of 131, Bridge-street, Warrington is now off'-u.inzhitre scv of pianos and organs at reltly re.:nced prices during hi i clearance sato for one month oalv and tits on vie+ ve the latest automatic electric p'ano (penny in the, slot). This elerric piano has been r.alv!nu hy it of ,the leading, hltel prc:prietors theoug!ymtth cluntry. Evervon-1 is invited to dal awl hltr this marvellous inatrument. a most ingenious and sensational invention, Mr. Boothrovd is Rise show in a large virie.y of penny hi the slot polypons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>DOCK LABOURERS AND THEIR HOURS.</head>
        <p>THE DOCK LABOURERS AND THEIR HOURS.</p>
        <p>No Dispute at Runcorn Docks</p>
        <p>Upon inquiry at the D ,cks ow Thursday mornin..7, our representative interrogated Mr. Digges (the manager) as to the report of a dispute about the men's bourn. Mr. Digges informed him tnere was no dispute. The men had asked the company to consider the question of shortening their hours from 6 p.m. to 5.30 p.m. ; and the company were doing so. Our representative further leant that the piece- workers, had taken matters into their own hands, and eiven notice they would ou.pend work every daV at 530; and had done so since Monday last.—As the Dock Company. bay to consider the intere4s, of the ship owners, the cargo owners, and the wharf owners in co4junction with their own, the men huld bear with them patiently. and in all probability their application will be favourably entertained. Meanwhile work at the Docks goes en as usual.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Death of Mr. Joseph Garner.</head>
        <p>Death of Mr. Joseph Garner.</p>
        <p>We,announoe with regret rho doath of a well known and highly respected Warringtonian, Mr Joseph. Garner, which took place at his residence Marshhoufe-Inne, at obout six o'clock on Sunday, evening,at the age of 72 years. The deceased gentle- man had been troubled for some years with anfemia, but he took to his bed, only three weeks ago. He was attended up to the time of his death by Or, Bennett, of Padgate and Dr. White. The deceased who was a Liberal in politics, was elected a representative of the St. John's Ward in the Town Council in November, 1888, find retired in Novem- ber, 1897. The funeral took place at tbe Cemetery on Thursday afternoon.</p>
