      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>INFLUENZA INCREASING.</head>
        <p>INFLUENZA INCREASING.</p>
        <p>--- The Influenza Epidemic is again spreading over the Country. The visitation is of a very prostrating nature, and the disease is certainly on the ine,rease. The origin of the disease or rather the circumstances which bring it about is as much a mystery as ever. You cannot trifle With Influenza. It undermines the Nervous System to a greater extent than almost any other disease, and itsis Pneumonia that kills, and Pneumonia strikes the man who imagines he had only a mild form of Influenza, and can go about the street just as usual. It can hardly be I doubted that the prevalence of Chest and Lung Com- plaints must be ascribed to the baneful infleence of In- I fluenza. whose after effects so frequently take this form. ' It was a bad day for this Country when this malignant scourge first visited our shores. Year after year it has never since failed to revive at it due time, and to extort its tribute of human life. Haring observed its ravages and its dreadful effects upon the constitution on the occasion of its former visite, we cannot but regard its recurrence with much fear, as a great many have not yet, if they ever will, regain their former health. As a Preventative, we know of nothing equal to Gwitym Ivens' Quinine Bitters. A few strong doses taken ins time have often proved effec'esal in warding off attackr of this Malady, and always succeed in mitigating thei 'Severi ty when a patient is under their influence. Do you suffer from Chills, alternating with Sudden Flushes, Severe Headache, accompanied by difficulty of breath- ing, and symptoms of Catarrh, Pains in the Chest under arid bstween the Shoulders, and a stiffness and soreness of the Muscles, Loss of Appetite and Nervousness. If you suffer from any of these symptoms, know that they are the forerunners of Influenza and it behoves you to resort at once to a course of Gwaym Evans' Quinine Bit- ters. By common consent Patients everywhere admit that they feel more depressed and miserable after an at- tack of Influerze than while uader its influence, and more than they feel after an attack of any other illness. The hest way to banish these miserable feelings is to take Gwilem Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic. We can strongly and confidently recommend thi. Preparation, AR it has treated severe cesss of lefluenza, Heavy Cold, Extreme Wealtneee and Lan- goes, with greater success than any o'her known Bernede. We may aho add that as a remedy for Indiges- tion. Sleeplessness, Lew Spirits, Nervousness, and Loss of Apoetite. G i'ym Evans' Quinine Bitters, outdo un- equalled. It is sold in bettlrs 24 91 amid Cs 61., by all Chemists, and Patent Meeieiee Vendors, or direct car- riage free' I e the ahnye pries, by the - earnprietors : Quinine. Bit Prs Manufacturing Cemnany, , Limited, Llanelly, South %Vibe&amp; Avoil Imitations. See the name Gwilyrn Evaes "on Label Stamp, and Bottle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>E3III3ZZLEMENT AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>On Saturday at the Warrington Police Court before Me,srs. T. Birchen (in the chair) and R. T. Faircleugh, Stanley Collier, of Werrineton,was charged with havine on the 23rd January, being a servant to the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Company Limited, feloniously and freudulently emlanzled the sum of £2O lls 21 received by him on account of his masters. Mr H Greenell prosecuted on behalf of the company, and Mr WR J Jenkins appeared for the prisoner, and on his behalf pleaded guilty to this charge as well as two other charees of embezzlement. the sums being respectively £l6 us 61 and £9 54 6d.—Mr. Graenall said that up to a recent data the prisoner was in the employ of the firm at a coal wharf in Tanners-lane. He was in charge of the books. This was one of those C 3385 of embnzle- =tent in which he believed prisener had taken to betting to enable him to put matteis right, His instructions were to deal 'Pith three specific cases. In one me Mr. Dixon a coal aeent at Lvnem, paid over to prisoner .the sum of £2O lls 2d ; he could not tell whether it was simely in money or cash and cheques. The money was paid and the receipt given and signed by the prisoner. The money, however. was never entered into the beoks at all. The only cheek Was that in the counterfoil in the receipt hoek ;it was never entered either in the cash book Or ledger until qeite recently, when the prisoner knew that shortly an audit was t ) take place; and then he gave up the amounts, or a great number of them. The amount was paid on January 23rd.—The o'her case was that of Peacock and Barlow. £l6 lie 81 ; on October 6th. The same thing was done again &amp;nether account was from Mr. Dixon, of Lymm, for £9 5s 61. There were, Mr Greene!l otat3d. several other cases, hut as the prisoner had pi-aded yulty he did not propose to enter into them; he would confine himself to the three cases —Fred Dixon, Lernm, and Mr. Peacock having given evidence in sup- port of the above statement,—Mr. Jenkins addressed the Beech for the defence. He said peisorer had been in the employ of the company for ahent 15 years, and hi v industry was sunh that two years and a half ago was appointed the mana4er of the coal department which Was attached to the Liverpool office and Wigan. The Warrington offie ale had no reason to reproach themselves of any neglect in checking the young man" accounts, but-- Mr Greenalh—The checking is done by the LiverW)ol People.—Mr Jenkins.—We put the saddle on the right hove. The books are supposed to have been regularly checked and a,ulited. and as a matter of fact the defal- cations ow/ht to have been discovered.—Mr Feirclough. —A. goad deal depend* upon the prisoner if he is a sharp naara—Mr Gresnall—There is no doubt the books were entirely in the prisoner's control.—Mr Jenkins, continu- ing. said that some t,rn since a friend of the prisoner got into difficulties and he frequently advanced sums to him amounting in the aggregate to £220. Having got into difficulties, he commenced to misappropriate his employer's money in the hope as they found in all cases of she kind, of getting it hatqr so that he would be able to turn the corner an, repay his employers. There WAR a rteleemine feature in the matter, namely, that the moment the discovery was made that things were going wrong, he at the instigation of his sister, at once went to the firm and be bad rendered all the assistance he could in the meter. The prisoner's char- acter was now ruined ; indeed, the greatest punishment he would have to bear was the the knov.dedge of the fact that he had ruined his own personal character —Mr. Greenall said it wal not quite correct to saa that. the accounts had been professionally audited. The prisoner knew the hooks were to be so audited, and that was why the whole matter came out. Perhapa the system of checking adopted was not the best. What Mr Jenkins had said was Perfectly true—that the prisoner bad since elven the accountant every assistance in his power, —The B inch decide:) to send the prisoner to gaol for three months, without hard labour the Chairman remarking that the case was a very painful one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>MINOR CASES. Warrington Borough Court.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES. Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>SATITSDAY.—Bfore Meears,T. Bireh%ll (In the chair) and</p>
        <p>R. T. Fairclongh</p>
        <p>AFFRAY IN &amp; LODGraG Ifousz —Peter Daley was charged with assaulting and heating Thomas Blair at about midnight on Priday•—lf e pleaded not gulltv.—Corn- plainant, whose fsee was much distignred. stated that he lodged at the "Model Lodging-house, Clothall:Yard, of which the prisoner was manager. On Friday night ha went to bed and came downstairs again at about midnight. When he came down the prisoner "kicked him in the jaw "'and struck him with a roker without the slightest provocation. He (complainant) was sober.—John Patterson, who legeel at the same house stated that 'he prison ar and he went to bed early end came tiownstairs to get some beer at 11 o'clock. he prisoner kicked complainant between the eyes, but he (witness) did not see him use the poker. The complainant when he was in drink "had a habit of sparring" with anyone who was in the house.—The Chairman : Does he do it viciously ? Witness : No sir.—The Chairman : Was Blair sober ?—Witness • No. How could he be sober when we had been driitking together all day ? —The prisoner said that when complainant came downstairs he toted like a madman. He denied using the nokee.—The Bench thought the eom- plainant's conduct had been aggravating but the prisoner had no right to kick him. He wonld be fined £5 inclu4ing costs wi',ll the alternative of a month's imprisonment.</p>
        <p>IVElrfr.sp un--SO...fore Mr. fled,le, (in the elnir), Alderman FRi,•elonvh and Mr. Rirehall</p>
        <p>AR'S FLIRS r t&amp;TTrANc.—WiiHam Ifonehton w‘s charged on a warrant with not haying £3 as Sd. arrears in snalntenance due to his wife.—Prisoner adroit' ed not havillhi paid the money.—Prisoner's wife said her husband had not paid anything sines the mile was rnade.—The Cie.* : The order W 43 Iniad,, on the 24th November to nay lOs a week.— Prisoner : I can't affard ta pay 101 a week.—The Clerk : you have not nail ar.viting at %IL—Prisoner said he coild only earn 15s 54 a w?ek. —He was committal to gaol fora month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Warrington County Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Warrington County Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Befort, Adte3Rrs. :Lynes Tinsley (In the chair),</p>
        <p>and S. R. Armitaze,</p>
        <p>TRRYT AT RURTOTWOOD.—Jaraes Harrison pleaded grimy to stealing a horse e'nth and harness vsioel at £1 from a atahle at "The Farm in the Fields," Burtonwood, the urn- p srty of Harry Bridle—Marry Bridge. said the horse elOth and haroess Dindos.el be•orured to him. He saw them safe in the stable at 12 °Wolk noon on Saturday, and missed the horse cloth at 7,30 that night and the harness on Sunday aftsrnoon. He was warking all Satnrday Yfternonn in the fields. and at about flr3 o'clock he beard his dogs bark. Shortly afterwards the prisoner came to hiei with a meek on his back. Retod witness that he had some rabhits in the big and asked him if he would buy some Witness said he al not want any and the man went an. He said he had gat the 'mishits from Knoweley.—Sunerlntendent Mnneghan of the Warrington Psliee Vireo said that at 7 o'clock en Saturday night be was in Thitterroarket-street and 59. W the wisoner go to Percival's Vold. followe + him to (11eosnn's lodging-house and saw he had get the harness and horse cloth In a seek. He asked him how he came into nosaession of them and he said "'they are mine I bonght them" W;tnees then told him he would have to come to the Pollee Station and he immed'atelv threw the Iyag down and said he (witness) would have to carry it. With assistance he took him to the Police Station and charged him with being in tiossession of the articles and not being ablo to give a satis- factory account of them. He replied"! fouud them on the road eide and put them in the bag. ' On Monday he was brought before the megietrates and handed over to the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <p>County Police. He gave the name of Johnson but the fol- lowing day that of Harrison. He at first said he lived at St. Helens, and after wards ht Bedford Leigh and Haydock.— Prisoner said be did not know how he came into possession of the articles. He must have been drunk when he took them —He was sent to gaol for 14 days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>B(ONDLY.—Before Messrs. James_ flactil -,(p:•3 41 1: ;) t t</p>
        <p>W. Timmins.</p>
        <p>LICENSING TB iNSFERS —The following transft,rs were granted : —Royal OA, Weston, from Archibald Haining to John Henry A ckerley.—Prospect Inn, Weston, from Ellen Vase, to James Robinson Hall.—Royal Hotel, Runcorn ; from Mary Ellen Ind, to Archibald Haining.—Bridge Inn. Run- corn, from George Riley. to Henry Treadwell.—Fox Tavern, Runcorn, from Joseph Horigkinson to John Leach. —Village Inn, Weston, from the late William rtnlse, to Mary Ann Rules. _ _</p>
        <p>MAINTEN INCE APPLICATION.—The police made application against Thomas Hughes, Briscoe-street, for an order to con- tribute towards the maintenance of his son, Robert, who was in the Industrial School at Macclesfield.—Defendant, said he was already contributing 2s 6d a week towards the maintenance of one of his boys at a Blind Schoel.—Superin- tendent D. Pearson. said the boy in question was sent to the Macclesfield Industrial Schools, some short time age, by the Runcorn Bench, and ordered to be detained there until 16 years of age.—An order for is. a week was made. Tim BLACK LIST WI. 3 this week but a small one : William Tsvigg; 77, Halton-road. being fined ss. for being drank and disorderly, and John Riff, 8, Duke-street, labourer, fined 10s. for using obscene language. NEGLECT Or BDITCATION,—The following parents were fined 6d. and 4s 6d costs, for neglecting to send their ehil- dren regularly to school :--Albert Ravenscroft, Pool-lane, Runcorn; Philip Handley, Irwell-lane ; Thomas Blackburn, Stanley-street; Peter Cawley, Taylor's-row; Bdward Wain- wright. Stonehills (2 cases) ; William Martin, Gilbert-street, (2 cases).—Bvidence was given by School Attendance Officers Bowser and Howard. of the Runcorn School Board.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>AN OBJECT-LESSON IN GRATITUDE.</head>
        <p>AN OBJECT-LESSON IN GRATITUDE.</p>
        <p>Mae. ANN Baoww, .ef 20, Freer-street, Attleborough, Warwickshire, is the heroine of a remarkable report in the local newspaper. "Sincerity ani gratitude "(says this reporter) " were the predominant notes of Mrs. Brown's story, and the tning she most impressed upon me was her earnest desire for the full facts she related to be published in the iaterest of the pablic." The wife of a rural postman, who has traversed the country around Nuneaton tor thirty-seven years without inter- mission, Mrs. Brown is a type of woman, happily not uncommon in this country, in whom great kindliness of disposition is allied with a shrewd outlook on the facts of life and a large fund of plain, practical cam- mon-sense. About two years ago she experienced very grave cause for alarm in the appearance of a large tumorous growth in the abdomen. She underwent ex- amination at Ike hands of several doctors of high repute, and with one consent they informed her that an opera- tion would result in certain death. To add to the poor woman's agony of mind, she was confined to her bed- room by a severe attaok of bronchitis, and, as she in- formed a representative of the "Nuneaton Observer," life became one long spell of indescribable suffering. "In addition to the unsuccessful treatment by doc- tors," said she, "I tried numberless reneldies suggested by friends and neighbours, but I could get no rest night nor day. In spite of a steam kettle, used continuously in my bedroom, I was subject to a dry, hacking cough which often kept me awake the whole night through." "But you appear hale and hearty enough now, Mrs. Brown," said the reporter. "Will ymi tell me the cause of such wonderful improvement ?" " With pleasure. And all the neighbours round here can bear out wnat I say. One day, when I was at my worst, a pamphlet was left at my door, and as the re- sult of my reading it I asked my husband to get a b3x of Dr. Williams' pink pills. I took two or three the same day, and that night I experienced the beat night's rest I had had for months past. The benefit I derived from the pills naturally induced me to persevere with the remedy, and in a short time I was almost entirely free from pain ; in fact, I was as well as I am now." "And so you left off taking the pills ?" "No I still eirry a box in my pocket," and as she spoke Mrs. Brown, produced, truly enough, the familiar box for the reporter's inspection. "But if you are better, why do you continue to pur- chase the pills 2' asked the reporter. "1 keep them for my own use in case of emergency, and I give a great many away." "And have you found any of you friends benefited by them ?"</p>
        <p>" Fre:inently. Only the other day a neighblur corn- plaiqed to me of pains at the heart, and I advised him to try Dr. Williams' pink pills to pale people. He said they woald do him no good ; but I persuaded him to try them, and the next day he came to me an.l said : Mrs. Brown, you can scarcely credit what redef I got frcen those pins you gave me. lam going to get a b3x for rzrysell.' " .</p>
        <p>Mr Brown also furnished the reporter with the de- tails of other remarkable cures, includieg that of her own niece," wh,se lips and ears wore liks wax," but who was completely restored to health after taking two boxes of Dr. Williams' pink pills. Mrs. Brown's history, and the numerous similar experiences she was able to refer to, on the part of per- sons well known to her, account for the confidence with whinh Dr. Williamepink pills are regarded by all classes of pelple, from the lowest to tba highest in the land. 74ey have cured paralysis, locomotor ataxy, rheuma- tism. and sciatica ; also all diseases arising from impoverishment of the blood, scrofula, rickets, chronic erysipelas, consumption of the bowels and blurs, arsernia, pale and sallow complexion, general muscular weakness, indigestion, loss of aopetite, palpitations, pins in the b tck, nervous headache and neuralgia, early decay, all forms of female weakness, and hysteria. These pills are a tonie, not a purgative. They are genuine only with the full name, Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people, and are sold by chemists, and by Dr. Mediciue Company, Holborn Viaduct, London, at 2s. 91. a box, or six boxes for 134. 91. Pills sold loose or from glass jars are not Dr. Williams' ; they should be accepted only in the piuk :closed wrapper as above described.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>NEWTON MEDICAL OFFICER'S ANNUAL REPORT.</head>
        <p>NEWTON MEDICAL OFFICER'S ANNUAL REPORT.</p>
        <p>Dr. Watkins, the Medical Officer of Health for the Newton-in-Makerfield district ha 3 presented his annual report far the year 1899.</p>
        <p>THE BIRTH AND DE&amp;TH RATE.</p>
        <p>It states that there were.registered : Births, 279 males, 271 females ; deaths, 143 males, 137 females ; excess of births, 270. The birth-rite was 36.3, and the dereth•rate 19 8 per thousand of the estimated population. The infantile death-rate (that is, the number of deaths under 1 year of age, relatively to the number of children born in 1899) was exactly 20 per cent. The deaths of children under 5 years of age were 52 8 per cent, of the total number of deaths. To death-rate from the seven principal zyrnotic diseases was 31 per thousand of the estimated population. It is a remarkable fact nays the Medical Officer, that not one of the C 9593 of "choleraic diarrhaa " or I " cholera nostras " was notified to him before the death of the patient. All the eighteen etees occurred in the month of August, when there was a very abnormally high earth temperature. As sixteen Casf.s9 were in chil- dren under live years of age, he was g,reatly inclined to the belief that cases of acute gastro-enteritis and gastro- enteric catarrh were in some instances certified as " cholaraic•" There was a heavy tale of pulmonary disease, namely, phthieis, 13; bronchitis, pneumonia and pleurisy, 52; indueeza, 12. There were 171 cases of infectious sickness notified to him 11'3 per thousand of the estimated population. The following were the diseases : scarlatina, 130 ; diphtheria 1 ; enteric fever, 20 ; puerperal fever, 2 ; erysipelas, 18; tqtal, 1.71. -The enumher of Ctleeß in the second quarter rose to 78, of which 72 were cases of ssarlatina, and this very marked increase was coincident with the closing of the isolation hospital to our c sirs, only 11 of them having been admitted in the second quarter of the year. The distributien of the 171 cause were R 9 follows :—Town Hall, 28 ; Viaduct, 36 ; Crew-lane, 26 ; Newton, 30; Wargrave, 51 Thirty-two ef these cases were removed te the isolation hospital. Dislafectants were freely and gratuitously supplied ,rand houses and articles carefully disinfeeted after the ter- mination of the cases. The work of the sanitary staff was very greatly increased by reason of so ceneptratively few cases having been sent to the isolation hospital, and he had to record how cheerfully and well they performed the extra amount of work thus imposed on them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>WATER SUPPLY</head>
        <p>WATER SUPPLY</p>
        <p>The quality 4:ontinuPs to be good,hut the quantity and constancy of supply is at times defectivo. Certainly the economy that had to be so rigidly exercised last summer prevented the watering of the streets and the flushing of sewers as much as was really necessary. The truth is, the population has outgrown the available sup- ply, and the active measures in progress to secure an abundant, and, he hoped, permanent supply, were now* too soon. Removal of night soil had been efficiently done. Scavanging of the streets hsd improved, and the work of completing several new streets was gradually progressing.</p>
        <p>SEWERAGE AND SEWAGE TREATMENT.</p>
        <p>1 New sewerage has been, and is being, mado to meet ' the needs of localities newly built upon. The treatment of the sewage is in a transitional state. The alumino- , ferric treatment, with sabsequent precipitation, is being rep:aced by the bacterial process—the success of which is a consummation devoutly to be wished. The condi- tion of the Newton Lake, and, consequently of the NI w- ton Brook, is lostter than it was a year ago. This has been brought about in two ways ; less pollution is brought down the Millingtord Brook above Golborne ; and extended sewerage and improved sewage treatment at Gollsorne. There 'esti!), however, room for improve- ment. The domiciliary visitation, entailtd by the new Vaccination Act necessitated my goinz, as the Public Vaccinator. into every nook and corner of the township where children are horn. Thereby, my inspection was constant and comprehensive. My experienco it that the majmity of householders are reavonable, and sometimes even s,,ratetu!, in acne pting and following advice abou sanitary defects.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>fr ,</head>
        <p>fr ,</p>
        <p>r</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MARCq 9, 1900.</p>
        <p>induced to spend many pleasant afternoons in those buildings during the next few weeks. (Applause.) The motion was heartily cairied. The Maxon briefly replied, and the opening ceremony was brought to a conclusion: The Exhibits.</p>
        <p>ANOLO-A3IERIW7 BOILER SOLVENT COMPANY RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Eis the name of tho firm implies the company make a speciality Gf boiler fluid, and the exhibit which will be found at the far end of the annexe, includes samples of the company's productions. The company has been granted awards for the excellence of their fluid. The preparation is manufactured spe3ially for users of steam power—marine, land and locomotive. 'rho firm also makes condenser detergent and disinfecting and sanitary cleanser. MERSEY BEDSTEAD COMPANY LIMITED.</p>
        <p>This Warrington firm again show some excellent speci- mens of their work. The bedsteads wbich are on view are neat in appearance and the workmanship is of the best. As.exhibitors the firm have been very successful in gaining awards. At the Auckland Industrial and Mining Exhibition they obtained a first class award for brass and iron bedsteads and at the second building trades and decorative arts exhibition hold in Manchester in 1898 secured a diploma for the excellence of their metallic bedsteads. This speaks well for the future of the firm and under the prtAsent able management a fur- ther enlarviement of this industry may be anticipated. CROSFIELD'S, LIMITED.</p>
        <p>One of the most attractive exhibits is certainly that of Messrs Joseph Croifield and Sons, Limited. The stand, which is situated in the centre of the annexe is draped in delicately tinted material and here and there imitation polyanthuses and chrysanthemums are inter- mingled. Messrs. Crosfield'a ErViltliC specialities are deservedly given a prominent place in the exhibit and the peculiar scent associated with their increasinzly popular toilet requiiites pervades the air for some dis- tance beyond the stall. The household soaps manufac- tured by the firm are also exhibited in great variety. In o cc corner a milling machine and stamper are to be seen in operation. The work of decoration has been entrusted to Mrs Massey and Mr Woodgates and they have cer- tainly succeeded in making the exhibit attractive and interesting. _ EXAMINER."—JOIIN WALKER AND CO. _</p>
        <p>One of the first rands in the annexe to catch the eye —and the ear ton—is that of John Walker and Co.. " Examiner " Offiv, Warrington, where two printing machines are-continually at work. The task of teen ferring the Wharfedale rotary machine from the " Etaminer " works WAS no easy one, but it was safely accomplished, and the somewhat complicated machine was erected satisfactorily on its temporary foundation. Since then it has been connected with an electric motor, which contains 3-horse power in its small compass, and which runs the machine rapidly and steadily during the whole time that the Exhibition is open. The large num- ber of people whe stand and watch the process of print- ing going on before their eyes i conclusive proof that the exhibit forms one of the principal attractions in the' building. A small" Pearl" platen machine also comes in for a good deal of attentign—its easy manipulation being generally admired. On it, visitors may have visit lug cards printed while they wait, at most reasonable prices, while orders for all claes3s of printing are taken and, where possible, execute' at the exhibitien.</p>
        <p>first machine of the kind that has been Made, and the tests have proved very satisfactory. Mr Melbourne makes a speciality of tanners' machinery which he is supplying to an ever increasing circle of cus- tomers throughout the country. He is also a manufac- turer of steam engines up to 100 i.h.p, and his other manufactures include lifts, cranes, hoists, mill gearing, grinding sad cruihing machinery. Mr Melbourne has received some excellent testimonials from his customers who speak in high terms of the quality and durability of his manufactures,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>MR .R. R. PEARSONT, ROLD-STRERT.</head>
        <p>MR .R. R. PEARSONT, ROLD-STRERT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Parson has a most attractive stand in the hall and shows a selection of handsome pianos, organs, and harmoniuntis,&amp;.,by BPchstein, Brinstnead,Justin Brown Board, Eitey, and Christopher. _A great attraction is an angelus, which is an instrument which can be attached to a piano, and on which anyone can play the best of music. Ir, is also an organ in itself, and when played together with the piano gives a wonderful combination of really beautiful music.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>F. PENDLEBURT AND CO., WINWICK-STRENT.</head>
        <p>F. PENDLEBURT AND CO., WINWICK-STRENT.</p>
        <p>In the main building Messrs. F. Pendiebury and Co., house furnishers, 10, IVinwick-street, are showing some beautiful drawing room fornitan in Chippendalp, mahogany, and rosewood. They have also a large num- ber of occasional chairs covered with silk, velvet and tapestry. A notable exhibit is a "cosy corner "of solid ma.hogany covered with silk. This piece of furniture can be moved into various positions and made into two separate seats if desired. An interesting book case is shown which is an American invention recently intro- duced into this country, and has already found a large number of customers. It is known as the Wernicke elastic book case. It is made of a series of cases with glass fronts. Each case is separate and by adding these units the book case can to extended either in height or in width to meet the requirt ments of the smallest or most extensive library. Messrs. Pendlebury intend to change their exhibits during the exhibition at various timos. Their goods are of reliable quality and up to date in style and appearance.</p>
        <p>TEE MISSES. TURNER, SANICEY-STRFAT</p>
        <p>Visitors interested in decorative art should not fail to examine the work shown hy the Missei Turner, of Sankey-street, and Bold-ltreet, which is of a high-class character.</p>
        <p>JAMES OLDVIELD, CHESTER. , Some ingeniously contrived but very simple pate%t looking-glass adjusters are shown by Mr. Oldfield, whose busieess premises are at Chester.</p>
        <p>C H. PIGOT, „lIILIDGR-11TREZT. Two interesting exhibits are shown by Mr. C. H. Pigot, of 75, Bridgc-atreet, who displays a number of high-class pianos, organs and musical instruments. An automatic organ known as the Kimball self-playing organ is included among the exhibits,and is well worthy 4f notice. Mr. Pigot makes a speciality of a twenty guinea cottage piano which is an instrument of excellent tone and well calculated to meet the wants of the work- ing-classes. A variety of musical instruments are also shown, inclu 'ing one or two splcialities in mandolines and banjoes. Mr. Charles Pigot exhibits on his own aecount some of O'Brien's celebratellshock tills which are guaranteed tl prevent mistakes and save their value In time.</p>
        <p>=WAS PIERPOINT LED COMPANY BUTTBEIMARICIAT STREET. At this firm's exhibit which is le the main ball are to be foetid samples of their superior farm and garden seeds, including a sample of their fiasst dwarf evergreen lawn seed which is a special study of theirs. The grass and clover seeds in which they do a very heavy trads appear wall grown and perfectly clealed. There are fully priced descriptive catalogues of all requisites for the farm and garden.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>A. 11. AND C. LAWLESS•</head>
        <p>A. 11. AND C. LAWLESS•</p>
        <p>This Warrington firm of pickle !manufacturers, gold medal sugar boilrs, and wholesale confectioners, have again an attractive stand, extending nearly the whole length of the principal corridor. A feature is an ex- hibition of toffs and weet-rnaking,at which a couple of men are employed from two each afternoon until ten. In one small portion of the stand is exhibited I,ooolb. weight of sweets, packed in 81b. glass jars. There are also shown naany hundreds of jars of jam and mar- malade. In another part is shown a very large assort- ment of Cadbury's chocolate; while cocoa is made and sample cups distributed free. Visitors should especially see the process of the making of the well-known mint humbug, a most interesting feature of a show which is generally excellent. Free 5331201 PS of this sweet are given away. Magas. Liwlfteg's retail and wholesale stores are at 5, MarkFt-place,and their steam works ore at MerslY Mount, in one portion of which is made four tons of toffee every week.</p>
        <p>F. C. TTON, implement agent, Market-street, has a stand at the far end of the annexe on which he exhibits numerous agri- cultural and dairy implements, including chaff cutters, turnip cutters, ploughs, lawn mowers, cream separators, and churns. A speciality sold by Mr. Dutton is the " Alexandra " turbine separating plant. Its special advantages aro that it requires no fixing or special foundation, is simple, easy to clean and manage, and has no loose or complicatei parts ; requires little power, and the cost of fuel is nominal ; and it will separate effizieutly and produce thick and thin cream as desired.</p>
        <p>W. BISHOP &amp; COMPANY, LIMITED, DIAL MILLI The manufacture of 1311-x is one of the more recent industries of VVarrington and it is pleasing to record that it is in a 113ur1sh1ng condition. Se ue tine simples of Borax crystal era on view at the stall of Messrs. W. Bishop and Co., Ltd., and an attractive feature of their SEhibit 13 a large peratnid of borax. rha firm make a sp,cislitv of their "Harmony" pavrier 13,rax which is now lydnE,, offensively used for prlserying meat and for toilet and sanitary purposes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>S. BISHOP BRISTOL.</head>
        <p>S. BISHOP BRISTOL.</p>
        <p>This firm who have a hiih—r-e-t;u—tatien for model yachts an .1 schooner 4, show some beautiful specimens of their work in th. maim hall. The yachts with all their necessary fittinr,,s hlve attracted considerable at- tention and have been erntly admired. LICEENSDE RFS ft TYPE s; BITER C?., LIVE RPIOL : Tbis firm's trpewritirs aro shown in operation. A number of the maohinos have been introduced into Warrington sod have given satisfaction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>W. BRIIWARD, HORSIMARICHT-STREET</head>
        <p>W. BRIIWARD, HORSIMARICHT-STREET</p>
        <p>The enlebratel Doti(ton china ware is sho mn in great variety at this and. Some of the productions are most artistic and cannot fail to please the eye. There is ale a fine affection of Brettley art ware.</p>
        <p>CADET/NY AND COMPANY, LIMITED. It ig perhaps hardly nec.ssa.ry to state that the pro., ducts of this firm are cocoa and chocolate. A splendid assortment of their productions are sho km and a peep at tigatantl is enoulh to set the children's hearts longing for a taste of the sweetmeats so temptingly displayed.</p>
        <p>VULCAN FOUNDRY, LIMITED NIP7TON-LE-WILLOW3</p>
        <p>This firm w.iich was established so far btek as 1832 has a very practical exhibit. By means of photographs they illustrate some of the types of locomotive tank engines of which they are makers, for use in connscLion with collieries, contractors and docks. The firm do a very extensive trade in locomotives and at this moment are busily engaged on a large contract for engines for Indian railways. some ef the most important features of their work are displayed, and altogether, a re.mt pleasing exhibit is produced.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>/OHM MELBOURNM,</head>
        <p>/OHM MELBOURNM,</p>
        <p>An excellent exhibit is shown by Mr. Jobn Mellmrne, of the Wilderspoal iron works, who for a great number of years bag carried on an ext,msive blsiness in the town as an engineer, and iron and bras; founder. A 10-torse power engine is shown slationAry. Near to, a motor drives a revolving brush. This machine which is Mr. Melbourne's patent,is used for p3iiihing leather. It is the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>CARTER_ AND SON, BRIDGE-MERV</head>
        <p>CARTER_ AND SON, BRIDGE-MERV</p>
        <p>Immediately in front of th'e platform, Messrs. Carter and SM.), show samples of elm, )ur, .rain, and mat There is also an assorcmlnt of bir-1 arid other sm,ed exhi bited in a glass case. The articles do not lend them- selves to effective display, but an effort has been male with some success to make tha exhibit an attractive one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>MR. _IC ._ M. ASHTON, SANKEY STREET,</head>
        <p>MR. _IC ._ M. ASHTON, SANKEY STREET,</p>
        <p>Pharmacew-Acal products in graat variety are shown at Mr H M Ashton' s stall wilch is situated in the main hall. Prominent amongst the exhibits are some of Mr Ashtoh's specialities, such as his welt-known, Drinkstone embrocation which is now ex:eusively used in the district, his carbolic taitin no Arder, elixir of raspberries, for coughs and colds, and tic and neuralgia mixture, all of which are gaining a high reputation. Mr Ashton is +went for the Idris table waters, and "Frs.; in the thr_iat," large quantities of which are shown. }DIMS. I'. ALLENTAND SONS.</p>
        <p>The stanol of this local firm of plumbers and decora- tors is arranged as a naedid bath-room, the ceiling and walls having been tastefully decora ed. The bath,lava. tory, and w.e. are a Model wattage arrangement, with inexpensive sanitary appliances. A special feature is the exhibition of a soil pipe arrangement with branches, veutilator, and vent pipe to prevent syphonage. rho work In connection with this has been executed by Mr. J.Allen, and is without doubt excellent. There is also an exhibition of joint-ipping on copper piping, as used in breweries. In addition the firm showed a bossed lead for lead finial, and an arrangement of globes and incan- descent lighting.</p>
        <p>2ICHMIND_ AND COMPANY, LIMITED.</p>
        <p>Gas apparatus of all descriptions are shown by this enterprising firm at one of the most attractive and in- teresting stands in the exhibition. The firm's" model" coekers are now so well-known and have found a place in such a great number of homes as to need no commen- dation here. Suffice it to say that those who are still without ono can hire them at very reasonable rates from the Warrington Corporation Gas Department, and have them fixed free of !cost. Prominent amongst the specialities shown is the Mansion House cooker, which is designed to meet the require- ments of a large establishment. It is built in three sections and stands six feet high. In the centre of the stand was a realistic imitation of a yule log fire fixed on a stand with ornamental dogs. It is a very bright, cheerful fire, strongly resembling a burning wool-fire, and throws off a powerful heat. Gas fires are shown in great variety, prominent among them being the" Royal Sovereign" in bold and handsome design. As a background to the exhibit several of the firm's show bills are displayed. One of them is distinctly up-t'-date, and may be de3cribed as a political hit. Standing imperiously beside a Richmond cooker is a cook with a long spoon "in his hand whose visage bears a streng resemblance to that of the Colonial Secretary. In t:3 e distance " Oorn Paul" is seen approaching with a fine fat eoo4e in his pessession. "Bringing his goose to he cooked "is the apt title given to the illustration. The stand is a great attraction to visitors, and Mr: Harborne, who is iu charge, showed great assiduity in booking orders.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>ARTHUR CLARE, LNUTSFORD-ROAD</head>
        <p>ARTHUR CLARE, LNUTSFORD-ROAD</p>
        <p>This exhibitor is an 'engineer, brass founder and finisher, coppersmith, etc. carrying on business at the Knutsford-road Brass Works. A number of hs specialities are shown, including some useful enginc•ers' fittings.</p>
        <p>PETER CL&amp;RE, BRICE-STRERT.</p>
        <p>Mr. P. Clue ha e a nicely arrangedlitall in the annexe, showing specimens of his productions. He is a manu- facturer of ropes, twines, cart and waggon sheets, rick and boat covers, sacks, hags, woollen I; nd waterproof horse covers, etc., and his exhibit is a most creditable one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>W. LAWSON &amp; CO., NEWTON-LE-WILLOWS.</head>
        <p>W. LAWSON &amp; CO., NEWTON-LE-WILLOWS.</p>
        <p>This firm of engineers who do an extensive business as brass founders and brass finishers to the engineering, plumbing and shipbuilding trade, al manufacturers or sanitary flushing cisterns, saturAtors, hydraulic brass work and other kinds of general brass founding,show a collection of their specialities. Amooggt them are flue samples of hat and cold water taps and a single syphon cistern.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>MCDOWILL'S LIMITED.</head>
        <p>MCDOWILL'S LIMITED.</p>
        <p>At the far end of the annexe Messrs, McDawtil's Limited, of Rylands-street, coach and carriage builders, have on view the horse ambulance which has been specially constructed by the firm for the Watch Com- mittee of the Warrington Corporation. Accommoda- tion is provided for two bezls provide/ with wire mat- tresses and also seating accommodation for those in attendance. It is provided with india-rubber tyres. The vehicle will undoubtedly serve a useful purpose, and it is a satisfaction to know that a local firm have bsen found equal t-) the work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>MESSRS. 7. H. PARKINSON AND SON.</head>
        <p>MESSRS. 7. H. PARKINSON AND SON.</p>
        <p>Two exhibits are shown by this local firm of brush manufacturers. In the annexe,mat making is shown in Iperation Sinaett 6r chain mats are made under the eye the;visitor with remarkable rapidity. On a stand in the far corridor an excellent display of fancy goods and general knick-kaacks is made. They also show several carpet sweepers, the special machanisrn of which is claimed to make them • superior to others in the market. Bicycles are shown from Smith and Sons, Saltnev, and others with the Edie free wheel and brake. Another exhibit worthy of notice is a chemical chimney cleaner.</p>
        <p>W. DOZDINSON, WINWICK-9TRZET, has catered very succeasfully tor nearly all the exhinitors In the matter of his speciality in signs and banner makine. He was specially appointed by the Committee, and his energy and tact have won for him high encomiums.</p>
        <p>I. H. TARE. ICING-STREET,</p>
        <p>shows a caso. containing soma excellent specimens of his work in book-binding.</p>
        <p>LONIIIPORD WIRE COMPANY.</p>
        <p>This firm is again to the fore with an attractive ex- hibit. Wools' patent wire mattresses form, of course the chief feature of the exhibit, but there are other specialitias which claim attention. Having inspected the brass French bedstead with wire mattress attached, the hospital bed and the Longford foldingbed,a specimen of the firm's settees is brought under notice. This is now in large demant_fo; „vraitbg rooms and hotels. Other portions of the exhibit include a single wire carry- ing a weight of 6501193. This is used in the making of springs for railway carria,:e seats. Another thread of wire from which the famous wire sa,ddlPs are made is shown, carrying a weight of 4521b5. Several of the wire saddles are on exhibition. An interesting feature is a sire mattress, which supports a ton weight. The weight is removed oceasilnally, and the wire springs back to a horizontal -position, apparently unaffected by the severe t.st to which it is put.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>WILLIAMS AND CLAY, PANY_ BUILDINGS.</head>
        <p>WILLIAMS AND CLAY, PANY_ BUILDINGS.</p>
        <p>A prominent feature of this firm's exhibit in the annexe is the" Swinton" hitches range which is shown in actual use. A large number of these ranges are in daily use in Warrington and district and the large num- ber of testimonials which Messrs. Williams and Clay have been favoured with bear evidence that the ranges are an unqualified success. There is a perfect simplicity in their construction and any cook can use them. An advantage is that they can be used with either close or open fires and by means of a bottom grate the size of the fire can be reduced. It is claimed that it saves 50 per cent in fuel and it is a certain cure for a smoky chimney. At a second exhibit in the far corridor some very servire- able fire-pla.cem and mantles are shown. Some of the cheap lines reach-the low figure of els 3d. Both exhibits are well wortn inspection.</p>
        <p>N. WRAGIS, WICILN.</p>
        <p>Photographs of the size ot postage stamps are taken by this esnilitor and can be obtained at a reasonable price. "srurntsg" PUBLISHING COMPANY.</p>
        <p>This firm show pictures of Warrington in all ages and specinvns cf Warriogten printing from the time of the celebrated Byres. Al) the publication,' of the Company are on sale anti photographs of the Volunteers now on their way ta the front are exhibited.</p>
        <p>W. ROBERTSON, LTD., LATCHFORD. This firm which has recently added t our local indus tries and a hose works are at Morris Brook. Latchford show a case of polished res. Their splcialities include Robertson's patent cold compressed steel rods,rods.bars, etc. They are also polishers of tuhe3 and rids in any metal.</p>
        <p>STANNIA.Y. BUTTERMAIMET-STENST. Lover g of the f tvourite weed should not fail to inspect Mr Stanway's stand. Here are to be sera two young ladies d4tly making cigars and cigarettes. This:is an entirely new industry in Warrington and Mr Stanway has decided to take up the business of making cigarettes. He hopes to engage a number of workpeeple within a short period, Gaving to the large demand existing for cigarettes. His speciality in the cigarette line is "Golden Virginia "and in the cigar line, the " Leon do Cuba ' awl the "Victoria Royal." His exhibitionalso includes a number of articles which cannot fail to be of interest to smokers.</p>
        <p>JOHN NICOL, MITTERELLRYET-STRBRT.</p>
        <p>At this exhibitor's stand au endles3 variety of clocks and wAtches, silver plate std jewellery, is shown, Prominent amongst the exhibits is a grandfathlr's clack which is in an excellent state of preservatien, consider- ing that it has " stood on the shelf" for 150 years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>JUSTIN AND COMPANY, HALEY,</head>
        <p>JUSTIN AND COMPANY, HALEY,</p>
        <p>This firm makeq a speciality of thlir Quillaia glove Bv., cleaner, and their exhibit has attracted a large num- ber of visitors.</p>
        <p>DkVID OWEN, GOLEORNE-STREET.</p>
        <p>Hanging in the coriidor near to the focal artists' work, M. Divid Oxen has a handsome case showinz various styles of picture fraaaes both of simple and elaborate designs. Mr Owen is a gilder and carver of repute, and undertakes regildinK in all its branches.</p>
        <p>MEWEY ITIITE LEID COMPANY, SANKEY.</p>
        <p>It woudb9 difficult indeed to make a very attractive exhibit of such a commodity as white lead, but the</p>
        <p>Mersey White ][e‘ad Company have done their best to, make their exhibit both interesting aril useful. White lead is shown in its various stages of manufacture, and also the finished product ,Qhich is of excellent quality.</p>
        <p>BERTRAM GARSIDE, 22, GOLBORNE-STREET.</p>
        <p>This exhibitor shows a case et Enudel boiler fittings wbicshnw clever workmanship and minute attention to details. WARRINGTON ELECTRIC LIGHT AND POWER COMPANY.</p>
        <p>This company haa supplied the electric iigbtiug for the buildings and also the power by which the machines are driven. N &gt;ar tlieir niltors at the end of the annexe are shown electric plnnt 2nd electrelicrs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>TrFE EXHI3ITION. A SUCCESSFUL VENTURE.</head>
        <p>TrFE EXHI3ITION. A SUCCESSFUL VENTURE.</p>
        <p>Parr Hall and the special annexe have been a centre of attraction since the opening of the Exhibi- tion on Saturday afternoon last. There is a consen- sus of opinion that this year's exhibition is the best which has yet been held in the town. The number of exhibits exceeds that of any previous exhibition and the spacious annexe gives accommodation which was greatly needel on the previous occasions. Many new features have been introduced Which add greatly to the interost of the exhibition. Apart from the increased number of exhibits, a gallery of art has been provided in the main corridor. Here are paintings, photographs and other works of art by local artistes, arranged by Mr. Aylward, the art master at the School of Art. In the main hall, the Probyn Ladies' Orchestra, which has gained a high reputation in Warrington, plays selections at inter- vals. As a side show, the famous American mid- gets have attracted large numbers, and many have been pleased to renew their acquaintance with Mrs. General Tom Thumb.</p>
        <p>The exhibition has been entirely promoted by local tradesmen and their efforts will no doubt be rewarded by a substantial return. The attendance during the weAr has justified all expecta- tions and there is little doubt but that the venture will be a great Success. The attendance on the opening day was estimated at about 3,000 and large numbers have visited the exhibition during the week. Mr Enlph Johnson is the chairman of the committee which has had the arrangements in hand, Messrs C T King and W II Pigot, secretaries, and Mr Wm Roberts, treasurer, and they have been ably seconded in their efforts by Messrs F Pendlebury, W Parkinson, W H Pod- more. G Thompson, E 8 Garlanl, F Knowles and C H Ingot. There was an encouraging attendance at the opening ceremony on Saturday afternoon. Mr. Ralph Johnson presided, and on the platform there were the Mayor (Alderman Roberts), the Mayoress and Miss Roberts, the Revs. Canon Willis, A S Cameron, A F White, G W Dickiaeon. Morley Stevens in, EC E Carleton, E R Tarbeck, John Youge, Theo lore Bishop, OH Hocken,El G Roberts, .1 Warnock, J F Matthews, C Finch, Fathers Whittle and Wilson, Aldermen Hutchinson and Francemb, Councillors Smethurst, Tinnion, Bennett, Baird, Ellison ; the Town Clerk (Mr J Lyon Whittie,) Dr Gomel!, and Messrs Robert Gfruett, John Faiiclough, J W Hayward, William Roberts, HG flensball, George Thompson, ES Garland, Messrs C E Sing and W H Pigot, (secretaries.) a number of ladies, and Mrs Oeneral Tom Thumb and. the American Lilliputian Company. The Probyu Laelies Orchestra, were also on the platform, and they opened the proceedings by playing the first verse of" God save the Queen." Mr. RALPH JOHNSON, in asking the Mayor to open the exhibition said he snould like to give a few reasons why that exhibition had been got together. It was considered by a number of gentlemen in the town—manufacturers and tradesmen—that if an exhibition was held it should be under the rnaeagemeet of local peeple. (Hear, hear, and applause.) Of course, it was a serious un- dertaking, e• but after a good deal of coesidera- tion they 4'canee to the conclusion that with such a 'large number of industries as there were in War- rington, no difficulty should be experienced iu providing an exhibition, not on a small scale, but one which would be worthy of the town. (Hear, hear.) They had hoped to receive more support from local manufacturers. Some of them could not see why they should show,ae they did not think it would benefit their firms ; no doubt that was a perfectly legitimace reason. Others thought that this was purely a money-making concern' and they were afraid the promoters were going to make money out of it. (Laughter.) Well, he could assure them that they lead not the slightest intention or wish to do so. If they could make something they would be glad, if they could make a £5 note moles() they would be delighted. (Renewed laughter.) There- fore, their sole reason in getting up the exhibition was in the interests of local manufacturers and trades- men. They were told in some cases that if they would cart the goods to the hall, fix the stands and assure the exhibitors that they would have a gold medal they would exhibit, but not otherwise. (Laughter). Others, on the contrary, were extremely good, Messrs. Richmond and Co., having not oraly taken VOry great interest in the exhibition, but assisted them in very many ways. They had also undertaken t) give cookery lectures every day at their own expense,and they would no doubt prove to be very interetting to the ladies of Warrington. He should like, also to mention the Vulcan Foundev Com- pany, who had erected a very handsome and pleasant exhibition, an inspeetion of which would well repay all who visited those buildings. Then as to the side shows, they had been most careful to select those of the very highest possible standard, Se that parents might bring their children to see them and be perfectly pleased and satisfied. Tnoy also decided to arrange for ale art gallery to encourage the younger branches to take up the study of art, and in this connection they had coins very good exhibits, Mr Aylward, headmaster of the School of Art, having kindly undertaken the work of hanging the pictures. He was now going to let them into a secret. Toe Mayor was not the first gentleman mentioned as being suitable to open the exhibition, as come of the members of the enthusiastic committee thought they might have asked H.R.H. the Prince of Wales to do so. (Laughter.) But they thought they might climb down a little and then they were all unanimous in feeling that there was no one they maid ask who was more entitled to itch an honour or who would b 3 more acceptable to them and to the people of Werrington than the Mayor. (Hear, hear, and applause.) Speaking personally he was delighted to ask him to open the exhibition. (Applause.) The MLYOR assured them that it gave him very great pleasure to be present, and he thoroughly appreciated the honour as well as the privilege of opening the exbibition- He did not mind on an occi Mon of this kind even playing second fiddle to the Prince of Wales. (Laughter.) He dare say in some respects his Royal Highness would have performed the task better than he could but he did not think he mild have done it very much better. (Elear.bear and laughter.) He would only have occupied the same position he did—that of her Majesty's representative. He was very glad to occupy this pos;tien for another reason, and that was that the exhibition had been entirely promoted by Warrington men and managed throughout by their fellovv-t swnsmen. (Applause.) He hoped they would not consider this a political sug- gestion, but they in Warrington were rather conserva- tive, not in the political sense—in matters infinitely more locally important ; they were a little wanting in the power of initiation, and on two previous occasions stranger had come amongst them and had done very well for himself and t some extent,too, for the town. But if they were not particularly strong in the power of initia- tion, they were strong in the power of imitation, and in the ability to improve when a suggestion had been made to them. He was perfectly justified in making that statement when he informed thein that in 1898 the num- ber of exhibits, or firms exhibiting, was practically only 50 ; to-day at the third exhibition promoted by their local men and manufacturers the number was more than double. (Applause.) He was glad that an occasion like that gave them an op- portunity of having their attention distracted for the moment from what had been occupying everyone's at- Caution seriously, and of late jeyously, although mixed with pad splashes of sorrow and regret. He was glad that an occasion like that offered them an opportunity of devoting their attention to something which was well worthy of the occasion and was ellculated to be a per- manent benefit to the town and its varied industries. Years ago Warrington was celebrated for about four or five principal trades. It always had a nornber of smaller ones, but he should .bo very unwise if he were now to say which, oot of almost a dersn, or even more, was the most important industry. The town was growing rapidly and the industries they had, remained with them and had enlarged considerably ; new industries were con- tinually coming amongst them arid an occasion of this kind was a valuable opportanity for comparison of ideas and produces and those who came there, buyers and sellers, would receive benefit, and the town would be considerably benefited, by toe results which would be obtained. (Applause.) At this stage, Miss Faulina Johnson, eldest daughter of Mr. Ralph Johnson, presented the Mayoress with a basket of beautiful flowers amid loud applause. Mr. GEOROS THomr3oN proposed a vote of thanks to his Worship for opening the exhibition, and to the Mayoress for her presence. He sail that when Mr Jahr: son mentioned the suggestion which had been made to invite the Prince of Wales.of course they took it for what it was worth,but there was a nobleman wha would have been very proud to have been present had he been alive, namely, the Doke of Westminster, whom they had tha !honour of seeing in that town not very long ago. Mr. ; Thompson remarked that tee annexo had been erected 'at considerable expense. Mr Johnson had said the pro- meters would he very glad if they made a £5 note out of the exhibition, but if it did any good to Warringtoneue, persenally, did not cars if he lost £5. (Applause.) Mr. GLELLND in seconding the motion said it was only I due to the Mayor to say that when he was asked to attend he consented without a moment's hesitatitm, notwithstanding the overwhelmine number of engage- ments he had had ti fulfil doting the past few months, and his ready acquiescence Co the wishes of the commit- tee proved that he wan deeply intereited in and had the welfare of the exhibition* at heart. (Applause.) Cmtinuing, he thought that if there. was one thing the committee ought to be thankful for more than another it was the good news which Lord Roberts and General Buller had bleu the means of conveying to them during the past few days. He had no hesitation in saying that if they had had a repetition of the bad news they received some few weeks ago it would have greatly interfered with their attendance, and therefore, with the success of the exhibition. However,as the news was all they could wish for they were in biighter spirits, anti he trusted that the publle of Warrington and the district would be</p>
      </div>
