      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>W. H. PODMORE, MARKET PLACE.</head>
        <p>W. H. PODMORE, MARKET PLACE.</p>
        <p>Fancy stationery ot tL Ciescriptions is shown at Mr. Poclmore's stand. Special lines are faocy photo frames, and metal brooches containing the photos of the Generals whose names have become household words during the last few weeks. A number of instcards,bcar - ing different views ii Warrington and district are also on view.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>PENERTH TANNERY COMPANY LIMITED</head>
        <p>PENERTH TANNERY COMPANY LIMITED</p>
        <p>This is the only local firm exhibiting leather which is such an important industry in the town and diitrict. Their exhibit has been arranged with considerable taste and skit and shows boot leather of all descriptions. The firm are to be congratulated upon the excellence of their exhibit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>J. CROOKB</head>
        <p>J. CROOKB</p>
        <p>This exhibitor has two stalls, no which silver and electro plate is attractively diiplayed, A feature of the exhibit is the electric engravin4 and the manufac- ture of jewellery in gold wire which is carried out in view of the visitors. V. A. NETTBERT, SANKEY-ST BEET.</p>
        <p>Mr. Frank A. Nmbert, woo oas recently occuiei the premises at 72, Sankey-street, has air) n establishment in Scarisbrick Avnnue, Slothport. His exhibit com- prises optical instruments and deals specially with spectacle work. The comparison is shown between the old and now maks of spectacles and the visitor is able to trace the development of the glass from its raw state until it reaches the finished article.</p>
        <p>GEORGR MOULT, XING-STREET.</p>
        <p>At this stand a lavatory and bath of first-class quality are shown and special attention is called to a new patens seat for baths which can be fixed to any bath and to arranged as not to injure the bath in any way. Several specimens of fine workmanship ik the plumbing line and an excellent collecticin of chandeliers are also on view.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>M. WALLED AND FON FROGNALL BRIDGE</head>
        <p>M. WALLED AND FON FROGNALL BRIDGE</p>
        <p>This firm supply the refreshment bar with mineral waters, and a large etcck of their errcellent beverages is kept. The bar is in charge of Mrs. Baker. JONES' SEWING. MACHINE COMPANY,</p>
        <p>This firm, whose branch establishruent.is in Sankey- street, have a large and attractive stand in the hall. The company are the largest manufacturers in England exclusively engaged in making first-class sewing machines for domestic, manufacturiwg and art sewing. The superior quality and high-class finish of these machines is acknowledged throughout the trade. The comp‘ny now issue every machine so that in the event of tke hirer's death the machine becomes the praperty of the widow or next-of-kin without further payment. The excellence of the compans's machines and the conditions of their business are responsilsle for the large trade they do in this district. The company's factory is at Guide Bridge, Manchester.</p>
        <p>MILS. DALEY. IBLACKPOOL, has a stand oa which sne shows sous° beautiful speci- mens of art embroidery.</p>
        <p>B. DAVIES, LIVERPOOL</p>
        <p>B. Davies, of Liverpool,fias a number of scientific and mechanical toys on view as well as a nurnba of school requisites.</p>
        <p>ZORN_ JACQURS, BRISTOL</p>
        <p>This exhibitor displays souv, creditaole samples of art carving in bog oak, connemara marble, rock and crystals, itc.</p>
        <p>JAMES STOTT AND CO. p MANCHE ITER.</p>
        <p>The exhibit of this firm demonstratEs the utility of the " Stott " gas governor, and should receive the attention of all gas consumers. Tan object is to prevent over- pressure of gas and the consequent waste therefrom. The governor acts as a self-acting gas tap fixed to the meter or service pipe, and closes with every increase and opens with every decrease ofspressure from the street mins. A large saving in 4as bills is thus effected.</p>
        <p>lIIGSR, SYNDICATE, ELLESMERE-STREET,</p>
        <p>One of the oldest of our local industries is that of fustian cutting and an exhibit which has proved a great attraoloo has been that of the Roger Syndicate. This firm shows a: patent velvet cutting machine in action which has been patented by Mr. Roger, of Warrington, and whose name is connected with the syndicate. The euccess which has attended this invention since it was placed on the market three 'years ago has been most marked. At the present time it is being used in Ger- miny, , France. and America as well as in this country and the work which it is doing is superior to that which was formerly done by hand. Close to Mr. Roger's machine the old method of cutting by hand is demonstrated and the improvement which' has been effected is made apparent. The opera- tor no longer needs to walk to and fro along the frame for by simply turning a wheel the knife rues along the material which is being cut and by an ingen- husly contrived carrier is brought back again as another kr.ife ascends. The ,work of fustian cutting is now a nice clean employment and thp introduction of Mr. Roger's patent has done much tOreighten the public esti- mation of the industi y. By means of the machine an operater is now enabled to earn as much wages as a four loom weaver‘at e faotory. As a back ground to the exhibit, samples of the material in its various stages of manufacture are shown, from the raw material to the finished piece of velvet. •</p>
        <p>JOEt ECCIAS, WIIITRBIRX, BLACKEI7RN. This oil and grease manufacturer makes a speciality of "velvet oils" of which samples are shown. These are special classes of lubricating oils, made from pure hydrocarbon and neatsfopt onty. They are of high class quality, and can be supplied at the samo prices as most dealers charge for mineral and other cimmoi-claas (AK The fire test being so much higher (498 Fh) and viscosity greater at any given tempnrature (200' fh. 4 degrees more than castor nil), tltcy are specially adapted for heavy work. They can be usAd for engines, shafting machinery, spindles, lo4ms, &amp;c., and are stainless to machinery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>M. MASSRT, ACTON GRANGE</head>
        <p>M. MASSRT, ACTON GRANGE</p>
        <p>M. Massey, the vrell-knowa fruiterer and florist of the Market Ball aed keton Grange has a very nice exhibit in the corridor, showing a choice collection of rare cut 11)wers aLd vegetables. There is also on view a fine stock of honey gathered from the owner's hives at Acton Grange. Another fetture of the exhibit is a cage of singing canaries. The stand is in charge of Mr. Birch. W. ASHTON AND COMPANY.</p>
        <p>This firm whose lo4sbinette works are in Boamont-st., have on view snm of the bee, samplca of their work. There are baqiioette; in groat varier.y all of which have been made at the Company's works and the workman- ship and general get-up are all that can be desired.</p>
        <p>TUE ORCHESTRA.</p>
        <p>The Committee made an excellent choice in engaging the Probyn Ladies' Orchestra, which has become very popular in Warrington. The conductress is Miss Edith Probyn' and her orchestra comprises three first violins, a s?eond violin, violincello, double bass, cornet, flute, Piccolo, organ, piano, drums, bells and effects. A pro- gramme of marches, waitze-I, overtures, descriptive fantasias, Isc., haø been presented in a manner which has greatly pless,,d the laree audiences who have had the opportunity of listening to the music. The soles by several of the artistes have been a distinct feature. Miss Edith Probys, the talented conductress, cleverly executes violin solos, and the cornet solos by Miss Carrie Probyn have been very highly spoken of. The same remark applies to the piccolo solos by Mice Mabel Probyu, and the 'cello solos by Mi4s Dora Probyn. Mae Theresa Foote has been most successful in her rendition of songs and ballads.</p>
        <p>SIDI SHOWS</p>
        <p>The place of honour must be given to the famous American Midgets. Large numbers of visitors have been anxious to witness the performances of Mrs. General Tom; Thumb, her husband Count Magri and bis brother Baron:Magri, and those who have taken the opportunity of doing so have been well repaid. Performances have been given at intervals in the main corrider, adjoining the art gallery and the programme consisrs of songs, dances, stretches, duets, fencing, boxing and an illusion- ary act entitled "The enchantoa statue." The sole manager for the Company is Mr. F. J. Stuzncke who nowt he congratulated co providing such an interesting entertainment. _ -</p>
        <p>In a tent near Cho entrance of the annexe Maeavae Isidore, phrenologist, clairvoyant. palmist and character</p>
