      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <p>RICHMOND AND COMPANY,</p>
        <p>LI M ITED.</p>
        <p>Annual General Meeting.</p>
        <p>The annual general meeting of the shareholders of Richmond Roe C.). Lee, Gas Srove M anutacturers of London, Wsrrington, Stratfor I, Plymouth, Dublin and Bournemeutn, wee hell last Teursday at the Company's works, G.:rpenters'-read, Stratford, E. Mr. E W Richmoee, the Chaiten ae and Managing Director of the Company presided. Before c enmencing he business Mr. Iltetimond referred to the great lass the Board had su3taiued by the death of Mr. Charles H Vessey of Sheffield, and a resolution of the greatest sympathe w as accorded to Mts. Vessey and the members of the family. Rercrring to the year's working, the Ceairmen said he was sure they would all agree it had been a very satisfactory one, and no better proof of the rxcrllent condition of things could exist than was shown by the attendance of shareholders at that meeting. The shareholders were satisfisd with the progress of the Cempeuy, and did net trouble to at- tend its meetings. The balance standing to the credit of profit and less account at the end of last year was £12,125 18s 2,1. which the directors proposed to deal with as follows :—Dividend on nreference share capital at 6 per cent per annum, £1,574 104 id. Dividend (free of in- come tax) at the rate of 10 oor cent per annum on the ordiesry share capital, £2,733 is 3d. £1,500 was voted to the employees' profit haring fund, Mr Richmond re- marking tear the directors considered the money very well invested by givine the ernpleyees an interest in the concern. They had given it a fair trial, and were satis fied it was the proper thine to do. £Bl4 33 was placed to the reserve, bringing it up to £lO,OOO whilst £l,BOO was written off as further eh preciation from patterns. p!ant and showroom fixtures, leaving a balance to be carriee forward (subject to the vote for Directors' remeneration) of £1,204 3s 103. He was glad to be able to inform the shareholders that the sales for 1899 were SO per cent. over and above those of 1898. Last year he had the pleasure of announcing that the Plymouth and Stone - house Gas Light Company had entered into a contract with them, and be bad now the additional pleasure to inform them that last year they secured a large contract at Dublin, and the premises were open there andeloing an increasing business. He had only that morning come up from Beurnemouth, where they had obtained another imp ntant contract. Satisfactory progress was being made with the new premiers, and he hoped that at their next meeting he would be able to announce that the working of the business there had been satisfactory to all concerned. Tne increase in thoir bu mess had been something p'nenomeeal, and he knew of no business in the whole of the gas industry—with the single exception of the Welsbach Incandescent Company,—which had shown such solid progress as their Company had done in the short ten years of its existence. Ten years ago they had a very am.!! works, and now they were paying over £50,000 a year in wages and salaries. The reports and accounts were unanimously adopted, and the dividend, of 6 per cent on the preference shares and 10 per cent on the ordinary shares were declared. rin extraordinare meting was afterwards held at which a further increase of share capital by the creation of 2,000 £5 preference shares (£10,000) and 10,000 ordinary shares of £3. (lace was passed unanimously and power was also taken for a further increase of capital which it was considered would be necessary during the year for the development of the business at the Com- pany's new Branches. Tue Second Extraordinary General Meetine will be held at the Company's Warrington Works at 12 o'clock on Tdursday March 15th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>WARRINGTON ASSESSMENT COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON ASSESSMENT COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>A meeting of thie committee was held in the Board Room, at the Workhouse on Friday. Mr. George Pilliog presided awl there were also present : Messrs A J Jolley, J Clarke, J Billinee, William Houghton, with J C Satton (clerk), William Owen (valuer), W El Woodcock, E C Cooper, and A Wright (aireistant overseers).—The valuer in his report stated he had male the following valuations and insnectione:—Messrs. McCerquedale and Co., Limited, additions to Printing Works, Newton, since last valuation in 1888, gross, £3lO 10e ; rateable £248 Ws:, which will make a total of gross, £912 ; rateable £709. Tee overseers have from time to time since the last valuation added to the assessment stends now at gross, £749; rateable, £562. In the townehip of Warrington,Mesers. Greenall,Whitley and Co , additions to brewery, buillings, and land. bottling store s and yards, Sec, grow £2BB, rateable £237 103. The alteratiens taking place here are not completed. He had givea the additions here without taking into account any credits for buildings taken down and re- moved, intending to take these fully into consideral ion when the alterations now taking place are finished and in occupation. Mr. Joseph Wainwright appealed egainst the assessment on land at Arpley, Warrineton, 9 acres, 0 rood, 12 poles. gross £25, rateable £23. He (Mr. Owen) had ascertained that the total rent paid by Mr. Wainwright for all the land he occupied, which belonged to the Hon. Misqee Wilsen Patten, for the ye.ar ending Februery 2nd, 190 d, was £l7 si. A new adjustment of this rent and the area would be made in the current year, owing to the new (Eversion of road made by the L. and N.W. Railway Company. Toe assPssment should be reduced to gros, £l7 i 0 ; niteable, £l6 102.—The report was adopted. Mr. Kirkham, solicitor of Minchcster,with whom was Mr. Davies the secretary to the Haydock Race Course Company Limited, appealed against the assessment nf the race conree which was fixed at £3,000 gross, £2,670 ratable ; Mr. Kirkham contended th a the assessment was too high and a fair assesiment would he £1,200. After legal argument between Mr. Kirkham and Mr Sutton, it was ultimately decided to reduce the assess- ment to £3,000 gross and £l,BOO ratable.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>EMT BOOTS Fo ALL, UNDIRt TN&amp; OLOJK. -110</head>
        <p>EMT BOOTS Fo ALL, UNDIRt TN&amp; OLOJK. -110</p>
        <p>THB MEDICI, PROFESSION are ext,nsively rneom- mending the to Aerate use of Port Wine in all cases o elfbility, eq. ,rially after Influenza. INVAI.I PORT of exeeptional quality, tlieronghly metered i guaranteei by W. 1 A. GILBEY, can be olstaine,,, of their 3,030 Aget.:s at 2s. 83. per bottle.—Advt.</p>
        <p>UNDER THE °LOOK FOE GOOD BOOTS.—Advt</p>
        <p>A DAMAGED BROUGHAM.</p>
        <p>Claim at the Warrington County . Court. The Judge Visits Bridge Foot.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court last Thursday before his Honour Judge Gwynne James an action was heard in which Mr. rtidward Connor sought to recover £22 17s 61 from Mr. William Russell for damages to a brougham on the 21st July alleged to have been caused by the negligence of the defend- ant's servant.-31r Porshaw represented the plain- tiff and Mr Browne, the defendant.</p>
        <p>Mr. Forshaw, in stating the case, informed his honour that both parties were car proprietors at Warrington. On the 21,4 of July last, the plaintiff's driver named Richard Dennett was returning from Paddington with the Rev. H. G. Roberts in a brougham and when in the act of crossing from Mersey-street to Arpley-road, the defendant's driver with a pair of horses attached toe brake came down Warrington Bridge at a great rate and dashed into the brougham. Toe defendant's driver was on the wrong side of the road and was travelling at the rate of ten miles an hour. The pole of the brake entered the front part of the brougham and did considerable damage, the repairs costing Z.T2 17s. 6d.</p>
        <p>Plans were handed to his Honour showing the position of the streets. Mr. Browne suggested that his Honour would probably be able to shorten the case if he visitad he scene of tbe accident.</p>
        <p>Mr. Porshaw said he had no objection to sucha course being taken. After a short consultation. his Honour said he would visit the place in company with Police-constable Edwards, who witnessed the accident, and the Rev. H. G Roberts. On his return his Honour said he thought his visit would assist him a great deal in the case, and perhaps shorten the hearing of it. Both the gentlemen who accompanied him were agreed as to the exact spot where the accident happened. Richard Dennett, the driver for the plaintiff,was Ulm called and gave his version of the case. When he reached the end of ICrsev-street he saw the defendant's brake coming over the bridge at the rate of eight or nine miles an hour and on the wrong side of the road. Witness proceeded across, and tha defendant's driver tried ts pot the brake on but he could not hold his vehicle back, and the pole entered the front part of the I-rougham. Witness was thrown on to the horse and considerably shaken.—Cross•examined He could not say why he didn't turn his horse down Bridge street instead of go- ing across the street when he saw the defendant's brake coming. He knew it was the rule for those in Fide streets to give way to the traffic on the main streets. The Rev H d Roberts, MA., said in the first place the defendant's driver was on the off-side of the road, in the second place he ',vas goina at a good speed and in the third place, the man lost Jails head.—Cross-examined : It was an accident. To dr vPr of the Urougham did try to turn down Bridge-street, but a little cart was in the way.</p>
        <p>rith Honour severely testsl both thi (Liver and the last mentioned witness by getting them to mark on a plan the exact spot where they saw the defendant's brake.</p>
        <p>Morris Mulcahy, who was driving a hansom at the tiLne also gave evidlnce on 13,half of the plaintiff. Richard Matthews and Jatues Rooney stated that the cost of the repairs was £22 As. This was all the evidence called on behalf of the plain- tiff.</p>
        <p>For the defence, Mr Browne contended that it was the duty of the plaintiff's driver to have turned to the right down Bridge-street when he saw the defeedant's 'bus being driven down the hiidge. Instead of making way for the 'bus, the plainfig's driver, as stated by Mr Spann, whipped up his horse and dashed across Bridge- street with the object of reaching Arpley-road. The consequence was that there was a collision and the brougham seriously damaged. No evidence of negligence on the part of the defendant's driver had been shown. He had a perfect right to use any part of the road so !wig as no one was coming in the opposite direction. Even if there was some evidence of negligence which he did not admit. Denoett, the driver of the plaintiff's brougham was guilty of contributory negligence. When the police examined the braves afterwards, the defen- dant's horses were quite cool while that in the brougham was sweating. As to the amount of the damage, there was no independent testimony as to that, seeing that the plaintiff bad himself done the work. James Green. 33, Bostock-street, cab driver for the defendant, said he went to Grappenhall with a party on the night in question, and on returning about eleven o'clock the oecurrence too place! He was in the middle of Warrington Bride when he saw the plaintiff's cab rush across from Mersey-street in the direction of Arpley- road. He applied the brake and tiuned his horses towards Arpley-road. If he had not done so the pole of the brake would have struck the middle of the brougham.—Cross--ercamined : Flo had been arrested for furious driving. William Travis, butcher's assistant, gave evidence as to seeing the accident. The defendant's driver was not driving furiously. P.C. Edwards said he was about 50 yards fronl the scene of the collision. He heard Green apply the brake to the 'bus. The plaintiff's cab was travelling much faster and the horse was sweating. Mr. Forshaw having addressed the Court his Honour summed up, and in giving a veriics for the defendant, held that there was nothing to prevent the plaintiff's driver from turning down Bridge-street until the brake had passed. He believed the witnesses had endeavoured to tell the truth, but it was not improbable that in a case of this kind there would be divergencies of opinion. c He placed considerable weight upon certain independent witnesses, and be believed what Mr Spann had said,that the plaintiff's driver whipped up his horse in order to get across bafore ttr. passed.</p>
