      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <p>PLEASANT PAIAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>--i, HARD TO BEAT. —A wet carpet. _ THE LAP OF LI:MUHL—A eat enjoying her milk.</p>
        <p>WHERE TO GET CHEAP DIPS.—The public baths and wash-houses.</p>
        <p>IT disgusts us to see others doing the foolish things we do ourselves DIGGS : "Where 'do _y, . think is the finest soenery in this country?" Griggs: "Don't know. No two railway gulde books agree on that point." WE learn to talk during the first few years of our existence, but it takes us all the rest of our Lives to learn to keep silent. EASY.—" How did you manage to reel off so many thousand miles on your cyclometer?" "I attached it to my gas-meter." HELPED.—" Did the doctor do anything to help your rheumatism?" "I guess so. Anyway it has gained on me steadily ever since." HUSBAND (reading the paper) : "What donkeys some men will make of themselves I" Wife: "Now, John, what have you done this time?" SHE (hesitatingly) "Now we are engaged, James, I—l ought to tell you that my—my grand- father was hanged." He: "That's all right; my grandfather hanged him." CONSTITUTIONALLY DOUBTFUL—Browne : "We ire a cynical lot." Towne: "Why do you say hat?" Browne: "When a Klondiker says it is folly to attempt to get out there, we think he wants it all himself; and when another tells how rich the finds are, we think he wants to sell a claim."</p>
        <p>SHE: "Do you remember, Frank, the night you proposed to me, I hung. my head and said nothing ? " He: "Do I remember it? Well, I should rather say I did! It was the last time I saw you act so ! ' MISS VANDERWHACK : "Now, will you listen to me while I tell you the plain truth, Cholly ?" Cholly Fiveo'cloek : "I'm all ears, Miss Vander. whack." Miss Vandervrhack : "That's just what I was going to say, only I was going to put it differently." HAD MANY PUNCTURES.—" There's a tyre," said the disgusted bicyclist, "that seems to have a false idea of its raisßion." "How's that?" "It seems to think that it is a tacks,collector." ARGIMIENTCM AD HOMINEM.—" Paw, it is true there's nothing new under the sun?" "That's what a wise man used to say." "Well, you buy me a bicycle of the latest pattern, and there'll be something new under yours."</p>
        <p>SHE: 'Why is it, wonder, that little men so often marry large women ?" He: "I don't know, unless it is that the little fellows are afraid to back cut of the engagements". CONVINCING.—Mrs. Bingo: "The next is the last act, isn't it?" Bingo: " How do I know?" Mtg. Bingo: "You've been out twice, haven't you ? " DAWSON (to stranger at Mrs. De Noo's recep- tion) : " Who is this Mrs. De. Nc o?" Stranger: "She is Mr. Da Noo'w wife." Dawson: "And who on earth is De Noo ? " Stranger "I am." BROWN: "I thought you were a vegetarian, -but I hear you eat mutton. Robinson: " lam not a bigoted vegetarian. I oniy eat the meat of such animals as live on vegetable food." "MARRIAGE," said the old bachelor, "is but a lottery after all." "There's where you're off," replied Henry Peck. "If you draw blank in a lottery you can tear it up, and that's the end of it.</p>
        <p>UP IN FIGURES. —Teacher : "Come, Dick. What comes after ten ? " Dick: Eight, nine, ten—er —I dunno." Teacher: "Bobby, can you tell Dick what comes after ten ? " Bobby: " Yes'm—jack, queen, and king." A MATTER OF OrmoN.—"Your wife is financially interested in your business, isn't she ? " "Yes." "A silent partner, I suppose?" " Well—er— that's what she calls herself."</p>
        <p>"1 DON'T see why you should sneer at my en- gagement ring," said the fair girl, as she faced the belle of the neighbouring town; it's a great deal prettier than the one you wore three years ago, and haven't worn since 1" "No, dear," re- plied her friend, with a cool, faraway look in her voice, "not prettier, but quite as pretty. It's the same ring." ON THE OTHER Sinn.—Magistrate: "What is the charge?" Plaintiff: "She ran me down with her bicycle, broke my arm, cut my head, sprained my ankle, and bruised—" Defendant: "Yes; and you broke six of my spokes, bent my sprocket wheel, broke my chain, and punctured my tyre." OFFICE Boy; There's a man outside, sir, says won't you please give him ten cents for a bed?" Slobson : "Tell him to bring it in and I'll take a look at it." PLACING THE BLAME.—Mrs. BTOWII "So Mrs. Jones is nearly dead from insomnia? What is the cause of it ? " Mrs. Smith (indignantly) : "Why, her husband is the cause of it. He talks in his sleep, you know, and she has to lie awake all night trying to find out what he is saying, poor thing ! ' THE LAST DIVIDEND.—First Klondike miner: "Our claim is plumb worked-out now, and all the gold cleaned up, pard—yer couldn't find another cent's worth uv gold in it with a magnify- ing glass." Second Klondike miner (sadly) : " Yes ; there hain't nothing left fer to do now but capitalise it." NEVER TAKEN OFF. —Sunset Sims (drowsily): " Dey say de Prince uv Wales never wears a suit uv clothes more dan once." Weary Willie: "Well, needer do we; only it's a longer once." THE HeNs's WORRIES. —" Yes, said the old hen, as she picked her feathers a little, "everything is changing." "What's the trouble?" asked the pea-fowl. "These incubators; seventy-five chickens came walking out last night, and I can't tell which are mine and which are the incubator's. Maternity is truly an awful responsibility. AN old Highland serjeant in one of the Scottish regiments was going his rounds one night to see that all lights were out in the barrack-rooms. Coming to a room where he thought he saw a light shining, he roared out, "Put oot that light there !" One of the men shouted back, "It's the mune, serjeant !" Not hearing very well, the serjeant cried in return, "I dinna care a tacket what it is! Put it out !"</p>
        <p>A PARIS BOHEMIAN, in very low water, on re- turning home one evening, surprised a burglar in the act of examining his cupboard, which he had broken open. The thief was struck dumb at the unexpected apparition of the occupier of the pre- mises; but tire latter pacified him with a gesture, and said, "You May go on, my man. Take your time, and good luck to you! If you find anything, we'll go halves." WITH NO DONNYBROOK, SOMETHING HAD TO BB DONE !—When the feeling between Great Britain and America was highly strained recently, three companies of Irish-Americans were raised by one Mike in New York to tight against England. When the scare abated a little, the colonel of the battalion in which them) warriors were enrolled suggested to Mike that it was time to disband his men. " Sorra a bit of it, Colonel !" was his answer. "Sure they are in throuble with the German Emperor now.' The American promptly "judged " that Mike was stilllonging to strike a blow at Ireland's enemy. But it was not so, for the son of Erin explained that, in case of war between England and Germany, he should range himself and his merry men on England's side. -</p>
        <p>NOTHING BUT THE TRUTH.—A gentleman had a cow which he wished to sell on account of her being unruly at milking time. So he sent her to the fair with his man Pat, strictly charging Pat to tell no lies, but to speak the truth about the cow. Pat returned in the evening with nearly double the 'sum expected for the cow. Master: "Pat," said the master, "you've been telling lies about her." Pat: "The devil of a lie, sorr." "What did you say, then?" "They asked me was she a good milker, and I says, says 1, It's tired milking her you'd be before you'd done, sorr." FOR several minutes the young • man did not speak. His heart was too full. It was enough for him to know that this glorious creature loved him; that she bad promised to share his fate. With a new awl delightful sense of ownership he feasted his eyes once more upon her beauty, and as he realised that henceforth it would be his privilege to provide for her welfare and happiness, he could have almost wept with joy. His good fortune seemed almost incredible. Finally ha whispered tenderly—" How did it ever happen, darling, that such a bright, shining angel as your self fell in love with a dull, stupid fellow likf me?" "Goodness knows," she murmured ab raptly; "1 mufi.t have a screw loose somewhere." ALL COLITLII3E PHOPLB should real Ine Rural Wold d. weekly, which contains articles en LIG. ses, Cattle, thie Dairy, Poultry, Bees, Rabbits, Pigs. Gardening, Naturai History, Botany of the Farm and Garden, the Nome, Foreign and Colonial Intelligrnce. A.nwers to Correspondents. etc. It IS profusely illustrated and is altogether, a most inteyest- ing and instructive farmiytg and country paper. it good Tneoir, for servants requiring situations. The VidnagPr efers to send a specimen tree. The address is 110 and 114, STRAND, Londf,n, W.—Advt. LINUM CATHARTICIIII PILLS, digestive, correc- tive, and agreeably aperient, 90., 13,1 d. Of all Cheinsts. Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
