      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>tetttrs to the OVitor,</head>
        <p>tetttrs to the OVitor,</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of !etters intended for publlcatitn must in at caEes send their names and addretse3 to the Editor. MattPrs intend6d for publicalon mils% b,3 written on 9ne side only of tve paper. Corresponctents are strongly advised to write briefly. A short letter bas much greater chance of insertion than a long one.</p>
        <p>War, and the call to Prayer. Sir.—Your correspondent e I ma" heel openel a question which every citizen of our greater Britain etsould speedily answer for hineself.—Should the present war be carded on to a finish or be stopped? " boa" appears to think he has given reasons -why it should be stopped, or, if I gather his meaning rightly, never have been entered upon. I will now ask leave to offer what I hold to be good reasons for its prosecution to com- pletior. assume even " Irma" will admit that if we are to live in this world at all, we must lhe in it as it is. Whatever Utopian doctrinaires may think and preach we who have to live in the world as at present con- stituted must so order our conduct as best befits present conditions and not in accordance with conditioos which, we hope and trust, may obtain in the luure. At present the lion does not lie down with the lamb—at leest not until the latter has an interior relationship with the foriner—then and then only. Now, this world consists roughly speaking of two chiles, the eaters and the eaten. Lock where you will this is so; the weak ere devoured by the strong; naturally,netionally,financially, and commercially. And the advocates of passive "Peace at any price" would quickly find themselves assimilated, like their prototype the lamb. Therefore I hold our best maxim is -" Be strong to resist evil and to do sight." "And does "Icna" think Mr. Kruger to be very lamb- like? Is he a good ex, mple of Christian forbearance and passive non-resistance? Were those tracts, he has been so busy, importing in the cases marked "Biscuits," or missives of a more telling kind ? It is true this exemplar of Christian passivity has for the last 18 years been striving for the convertion of the British and en- deavouring to bring them to their knees. But this, des- pite his prayers, his texts—all from the old or fighting Scriptures mind—and his pious attitudes is as far from realization as ever. Well,well ! I should dearly like to be able to introduce Oom Paul to our" lona," cod to hear the very candid opinions which might pass between the pair. I think the icterview might be good for beth; for it is good to be able to" see ourselves as others see us." Now though I have of old in these pages, deprozated jingoism, and t he prosecution of aggressive wars I should indeed, greatly deplore the taking of our bands from the task we are at present engaged in. To do this .would be tantamount to the virtual sacrifice of the valuable lives —the blood and tre, sure already expended. The blind- ness of the Exeter Hall people is something amazing. They do not see that in crying out against the further prose- cution of this war, they are acting as Devil's advocate. The Boer Government has proved itself a brWal one to the natives—liars and falsifiers of the truth. The splen- did response made to the call by our cousin Colonials should, alone, prevent so pusillanimous a termination to this arduous struggle.—Yours, Sec., JOHN PEERS, March sth, 1900.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>DEATH OF COUNCILLOR DR.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF COUNCILLOR DR.</p>
        <p>CANNELL.</p>
        <p>-- It is with regret that we announce the death of Councillor Dr. Cannell, who passed away at his re- sidence, Reynard House, Academy-street, Warring- ton, 'at 825 on Sunday morning. The deceased gentleman had for some time been in a precarious state of health, having only recently recovered from a very serious illness, during which he had been attended by Dr. Harris, of Manchester. and Dr. Nuttall, of Warrington. He went out on Thursday evening to see a patient, but owing to sudden indisposition had to return at once, Drs. Langsdale and Bowden being sent for, and acting on their advice he at once took to his bed. Al- though his condition was known to be eri'ical the end came much sooner than was expected. The deceased, who was the youngest son ef the late Mr. Thomas Cannel), was born at School-brow on the 9th January, 1852, and the first school he at- tended was that kept at one time at Waterloo House, Wilderspool. For several months the de- ceased was an art student at Glasgow, but be 0- sequently decided to take up the medical profes- sion, and his qualifications and career are thus briefly described in "The Medical Directory :" " L.R.C.P. Edinburgh and L M., 1875; L.F.P.S. Glasgow and L.M., 1875 (Anderson's Universi;y, Glasgow) ; surgeon to the Shepherds, Gar- deners, Oddfellowe, Stubs's file works, and medical referee to several insurance societies. Dr Carmen had been in practice for about 25 years, He was very kind to the poor, by whom he will be much missed. He had besin a member of the Town Council for about eleven years, having been con- tinuously in office since 1889, and at the time of his death was a member of the Watch, Cemetery, and General Purposes Committees, and deputy-chairman of the Town Hall, Park, and Baths Committee. He was first elected a member of the Council in July, 1889, to supply a casual vacancy, and was re-elected on the Ist November of the same year; and when the borough was _extended and the wards re- arranged and increased in number, he was assigned to Howley Ward. The deceased who leaves three sisters, was an ardent Conservative in politics. At one time he occupied the post of president of the Warrington Swimming Club in which he took much Interest. • The Funeral. The remains of the deceased were interred in the Cemetery at noon on Tuesday. The funeral was attended by the Mayor and members of the Town Council, and as a mark of respect, a large number of blinds were lowered in the streets through which the cortege passed. The procession was headed by a contingent of police under the direction of Inspector Phillips and amongst those who attended the funeral were the Mayor (Alderman Roberts, wearing his chain of office, which was draped), the Town Clerk (Mr J L Whittle), Mr T Longclin (borough surveyor). Mr George F F White (deputy clerk to the magistrates),Dr Reginald Smith (police surgeon), Mr Frank Turner (superintendent of the Fire Brigade), Mr. W S Haddock (gas engineer), Mr. Fred Taylor (secretary of the Gas Department), Aldermen Sutton, Fran- comb, and Bolton; Councillors Heaton F R Roberts, Baird, Lee, John Wilkinson, Bennett, Jolley, Smethurst, Ellison, Plumpton, A J Jolley, John Evans, Peacock and Rogers; the following mourners (who started from the house), first carriage, Messrs W C Baines, James Ulnae% J Rimmer and E S Rimmer ; second carriage, Messrs Arthur Browne (deceased's solicitor), Samuel J Hewitt; third carriage, Messrs Robert Chadwick M Gibbons, Isaac Parker, and John Cannel) ; Drs Langdale and Bowden (deceased's medical attend- ants), Drs Peacocke, Braide, Nuttall, Geary; Mr homes Mason (representing the ConservatixeClub Committee, and also the Howley Ward, of which he is secretary), Mr George Watkins (the club,) Mr William Holbrook and Mr Herbert Hayes (the Con- servative Association). Mr John Clayton (Liberal agent), Mr.Bramball and Mr John Burgess (Friend- ship Lodge of Oddfellows), and Messrs S Davies, William Taylor, T Boscow, James Rushworth, S Moss, W Ashton (park keeper), J Waldron, J Last- ton, Cross, and John Sampson ; others representing various friendly societies, and tha following bearers —Messrs. G Billington, H Warburton D Harries, W Pemberton, F Hawley and G Taylor (all members of the Shepherds' Lodge). The officiat- ing clergyman was the Rev A S Cameron (senior curate of the Parish Church). The funeral arrangements were carried out by Messrs. J Moun- field and Sens, of Buttermarket-street, who elm supplied the coffin, which was of brown cak with brass mountings, the plate containing the following inscription ;—J a cues Cannel), born January 9th, 1852, died March 4th, 1900' The following is a list of the ladies and gentle- men who had forwarded wreaths ;—Aldertnan and Mrs Bolton. Mr and Mrs Chadwick and family, Mr and Mrs Arthur Browne,Me and Mrs A E Board- man, Mr and Mrs Whittle and family, Mr and Mrs Francomb, Mrs Hewitt and family, Mr and Mrs Cannell, Mr Baines, Mr and Mrs Rushworth,Mr and Mrs Wakefield, Dr Bowdon, Dr Peacocke, emplosaSs of Park and Baths Committee, Missei Mather, Stephen James Lyons, Gertrude Lyons, Miss Strettle,jlr Taylor, Mr and Mrs Watkins.</p>
        <p>r CADBURY'S COCOA, on the testimony of t e Lancet " represents the standard of highest purity." It is entirely free from all foreign substances, such as kola, malt, hops. Ac., nor is alkali used to darken the colour (and so deceive the eye). Dr. Andrew Wilson in a recent article in the 111ustratel London News writes: " Cocoa is in itself a perfect food, and requires no addition o 5 drugs whatever CADBURY's Cocos is absolutely pure, and should be taken by old sud young, at all times and in all seasons ;for Children it is an ideal beverage, promoting healthy growth and development in a remarkable degree.'l2lnsist on having CADBURY's. as other Cocoas are often substituted for the sake of extra profit, Sold only in Packßts And Tins— Advt. 3</p>
        <p>NEW SPEING' jACICRTS, Capes, Manintosheg and Im- perial Cloaks. Choice assortment of White Sailor Hats. Just receivei.—W. T. WILLIAMS, 74, Sankey-street. Warriniz,ton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS.</p>
        <p>BOERS ROUTED BY ROBERTS. The Victory Complete.</p>
        <p>The following further despatch from Lord Roberts wag issued late on Wednes day night. POPLAR GROVE, WEDNESDAY, 7.35 P.M.</p>
        <p>We have had a very slice essful day, and com- pletely routed the enemy, who are in full retreat. The position they occu pied is extremely strong and cunningly arranged, with a second line of en- lrenchments, which would have caused us heavy oss had a direct attack been made.</p>
        <p>The turning movement was necessarily wide owing to the nature of the ground, and the cavalry and horse artillery horses are much done up.</p>
        <p>The fighting was practically confined to the Cavalry Division, which, as usual, did exceedingly well, and French reports that the horse artillery batteries did a great deal of executi'on amongst th e enemy.</p>
        <p>Out casualties number about 50. I regret to say that Lieutenant Keswick 12th Lancers was killed an 4 Lieutenant Bailey, of the ' same regiment, severely wounded. Lieutenant De Critipigny, 2nd Life Guards, also severely wounded. The remaining casualities will be telegraphed tc- morrow. Genera's De Wet and Delarey were in command of the Boer forces.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>CAPE COLONY,</head>
        <p>CAPE COLONY,</p>
        <p>British Occupy Burghersdorp. MOLTEN°, THURSDAY, 7 A.M. Burghersdorp was occupied by our troops un- opposed last night.</p>
        <p>Cronje and his Army to be</p>
        <p>Sent to St. Helena.</p>
        <p>It has been resolved, writes "H. W. L." in the " Daily News" from the House of Commons lobby, that General Cronje and his army, interne-4 at Capetown and en route,shall forthwith be despatched to St. Helena. They will remain there till the ter- minatiOn of the war. The Commander-in-Chief in south Africa has selected Lord Bathurst to com- mand the escort conducing the Beer prisoners of war to St. Helena. Lord Bathurst, who is son-in- law of Lord Glenesk, i 3 at present at the front in command of a militia regiment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>Squadron of Kitchener's Horse Captured. CAPE TOWN, WEDNESDAY.</head>
        <p>Squadron of Kitchener's Horse Captured. CAPE TOWN, WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>A and B Squadrons of Kitchener's Horse, which were reported missing, have returned to camp at Paardeberg.. They were cut off with the E Squadron on February 13, but escaped, and after- wards lost their way on the veldt. E squadron con- sisting of 75 officers and men was captured, and are now prisoners in Pretoria.</p>
        <p>The Queen's Appeal to Old Soldiers.</p>
        <p>Tha response to the Queen's appeal to old soldiers to nijoin the colours to form a reserve for one year's home service has been very satisfactory at Orford Barracks,uo fewer than 150 having already volunteered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>On Board the" Gascon." " Bacca " at a Premium.</head>
        <p>On Board the" Gascon." " Bacca " at a Premium.</p>
        <p>Private A. C. l'immins, one of the six volunteers attached to the special service company of the 2n,1 Cheshire Re4iment now en route for South Africa, writes as follows to his parents at Rockfield, Runcorn :</p>
        <p>--" S.S. Gascon, Bay of Biscay, 24th February, 1900. We had a terribly rough voyage across the Bay of Bis- cay, and on the first Sunday out nearly every man on board was laid full length on the deck, half dead with sea-sickness; but what a beautiful sight it was to see the big rolling waves! I could not eat anything myself for three days only a few dry biscuits, but I am all right now, and making up for lost time. Speakman has been very poorly indeed. I hope you received the photo of the ship I s3nt from Southampton. We expoct to arrive at Teneriffe on Friday. The food we get on board is very fair, but we have to rough it. We sleep in ham- mocks slung up on hooks to the roof. On Monday we were caught in a storm, but the sunset after was really exquisite. The fellows on board are just beginning to come out of their shell. There is no doubt that the Sots beat the English for sport. One of their games is to get their comrade down on his back and cut his moustache off. I will forward you my address in the Field Forces as soon as I know."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>Lance-Sergeant Speakman Quite Well.</head>
        <p>Lance-Sergeant Speakman Quite Well.</p>
        <p>Brook House, Halton, near Ruacorn, March sth, 1900. Dear sir,—l think it woeld be we I,in answer to the very many kind inqair ies respecting my son Eric, to insert in your paper. " Ru acorn Examiner," that Lhave receive.! a !otter from him, of which the following is an extract : —Twin ec rew H.M.S. Gascon. February 22nd, 1900. Dear father, mother, and all,—You will be wondering how your wandering san is getting on. Well, I am pleased to say that I am all right,and in grand condition. How is me tiler keeping ? Tell her to cheer up and trust me to God's care, tne same at I hive done myself. Tell her lam quite happy, and looking forward to landing in South Africa. We did not leave Sauthamptoa till Saturday morning. We went down the Solent in grand form, but when we got into the Channel, oh, dear then the fun commenced (if you can call it such). In a short time after the ship began to pitch, first one and then another began to be sick, so to keep in fashion I followed suit. We are all right now. The food is splendid. We were three days in crossing the Bay of Biscay. It is, one ef the sailors told us, one of the roughest voyages she, the S.S. Gascon, ha 3 had yet. We have not seen the sun yet, although we are close to the Canaries. It has b 0 m dull every day since we left the Bay.' The sea has been quite calm, just a gentle swell on the surface. The as. Gascon sails ver y steadily. We have all:kinds of drill on board for develop- ing the muscle. They are not allowing us ashen at Teneriffe, were we coli and got a supply of fresh water. If you cluld see us on o ri now you would frit know us, our hair is cut short; we look like a shipload of eonvict?, nat. soldiers." Bath Mrs. Spealmail, myself; and family thank the public very much for their kind incpiriea repeating our son, and abeut whom such wild rumours were alloat.—Yours, J. J. SPZAIMAN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>Hardships of the Campaign.</head>
        <p>The Hardships of the Campaign.</p>
        <p>The following lee extracts from letters received fro-n Private G W Johnsnn (No. 2922) of the 2al Devon, by his brother Mr. S Johnsons Dover-street, Runcorn :- Bth January 1900. Cape Town,. Dear Brother ani Sisters.—Just a few lines to let you know we have arrived at the Cape. We had a very crowded passage across, there being hardly any room te stir. In fact,we had hardly room to wash or do anything else . . . . . . I hope you spent a good Christmas and a Happy New Year. lam sorry to say that we spent a very peer Christmas, as we had nothing at all, and were only in one another's road. Officers are all gay here. They get what they want, but the like of us get nothing. If we want any extra br, ad, we pay 4d.a pound for it. It is a regular fraud I can assure you. And not only that.very often, we cannot get a drink of water. I have sailed across the pond before,but this" caps all." and to make things worse we have no tobacco. Again I must say we have not room to have a comfortable sleep. There are ten men' in our mess and only room to sling six hammocks. Someone has to sleep on the table, one on each form and the other on the floor. So you see what a soldier's life is on board ship. It is a pity that the. public could not see for themselves how we are treateds After tenderly written remembrances to members of hi family, the writer goes on to say he is in good health, and hopes for a safe return home after the war." "Feb. 6th :.—Dear brothers and sisters.—l am sorry to inform you that I have been wounded. I reeeived a bullet wound through the shoulder, but it is easier to- day. We were under a very heavy fire yesterday. It was from a cross fire that: I got shot as I was crossing over the Tugela river. Our Naval guns, and artillery were bernbarding the Bor trenches all day. They had three good positions, hut were routed out of two of them and I believe we have cap`ured the o;ber now. We have been only three mil's from La iysmith, and exeect to be in at six o'clock to-night, which will be a great relief to all for our first Battalion is hemmed in there. Dear brother we are morel back to " Spear Hill" on account of the Boers firing on the hospital—the cowards. But we gave there what they wanted yesterday, for I know they meet have had a heavy loss. Our caseali- eies were atm reieh guees : Killcd and woaaded about 130 to liO, which it very light considerine the fin we Iv( re under."</p>
        <p>The Late Colonel McCarthy O'Leary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>Colonel of the South Lancashires. Killed at Pieters Hill.</head>
        <p>Colonel of the South Lancashires. Killed at Pieters Hill.</p>
        <p>The above is taken from a vartrait of the late Lieut. Colonel W. M'Carthy O'L3ary. commanding the Ist Battalion South. Lancashire Regiment who was killed in the desperate engagement at Pieter's Hill preceding the relief of Ladysmith. It will be remembered that in his dispatch referring to the capture of the hill, General Buller said the enemy's main position was" magnificently carried by the South Lanceshire Regiment about sunset," and it ia most regrettable that the victory should have eost the regiment the life of their respPcted Colonel. It appears that on Monday, the day before the fight, Mrs. McCarthy O'Leary, received a cablegram from her husband stating that he was ." never better."</p>
        <p>The deceased Colonel was born at the village of Mill street, County Cork, Ireland, in 1849. He first became connected with the Army 20 years ago, when he was gazetted ensign of the 82nd Foot,then styled the "Prince of Wales's toluateers" and now the 2nd Battalion of the South Lancashire Regi- ment. He received his lieutenancy two years later, and for four years, 1874 to 1878, was instructor of musketry. In the latter year he was promoted captain in the South Lancashire Regiment. The rank of major came in 1883, and from June the same year until 1888 Major McCarthy O'Leary acted as adjutant to the Volunteer Regiment at Waraington. He was appointed colonel in Novem- ber, 1E96. He had completed 30 years of service in the Army in April last, and his time would have expired in November, 1900. He was a Justice of the Peace for the County of Cork, one of the undeir- sheriffs of the city, and sat on the last Grand Jury in 1893. This was the Colonel's first experience of active service. He was very well known and ex- ceedingly popular in Wasrington, espnially in Volunteer circles and ha 4 won the respect and esteem of all with whom he was associated. Colonel O'Leary was a man of commanding stature and his figure was very conspicuous in the regis merit. Besides being au excellent officer he was a perfect gentleman in social life. In religion he was a Roman Catholic. The deceased Colonel leaves a widow and several sons and daughters with whom much sympathy is exoreised.:</p>
        <p>The Fermoy Urban District Council have unanim- ously passed a resolution expressing sorrow at the death of Colonel O'Leary and condolence with his widow and family. Colonel Studholme Brownrigg,in command of the 35th and 47th regimental District, on Saturday RotifiA the Preston garrison of the death of Colonel M'Carthy O'Leary,and hoped all officers would weir mourning in uniform for a week from that date in respect to the memory of a gallant officer. Colone! J. W. Ridgway, the commanding officer of the Ist Volunteer Battalion (P.W.V.) South Lancashire Regiment has i4sued the fllowitvr order:—"Warrington March 3r,1, 1900 It is with rle.rrst regret, which is shared by the whole of this battalion, that the Com- manding Officer has to record the death of Lieutenant Co'onel W MacCarthy O'Leary Commanding the Ist Battalion of the Regiment, in the engagement on the 27th February, to relieve Ladysmith. First associ tel with this Battalion as its Adjutant, from 1883 to 188, when he won the sincerest regard and re- spect of all ranks, he remained until his death its firm frien!; many acts of kindness and friendliest encourage- ment from time to time, testifying to his continued in- te7est ia it. By his death the whole of the South Lanca- shire Regiment loses a loved and revered leader, and her Maj,ity one of bee most gallant soldiers, and a high souled ~env.lernan. The officers of this Battalion will wear mourning, when in uniform, for one month from this date."</p>
        <p>On Tuesday at the English Martyr's Church, Preston, a service was held in memory of the late Colonel. The troops stcioned at the barracks, including detachments of the Loyal North, South and East. Lancashire Regiments and Kings (Liver- pool) Regiment, marched to the church, headed by the band of the South Lancashire Regiment, with instruments draped, playing the Dead March. Colonel St-udholtne Brownrigg, commanding the 30,,h and 47th Regimental Districts, was present, along with the officers of the garrison in mourning. Major Adam and a detachmf at from Orford Bar- racks, Major Buckton and Major Roome (adju- tant) representing the Warrington Volunteer Battalion rilgo attended the service. The church was draped with black. His Successor.</p>
        <p>Major R. H. Hall, who has since April, 1898. been seconl in command of the Ist Battalion South Lan- cashire Regiment, becomes Lieutenant-Colonel in command of tha corps in succession to Lieutenant- 041onel W. McCarthy•O'L,ary killed in action in South Africa. Major Hall joined the South Lancashire Regiment from the militia in Novenab-r 1873 ; became Captain in 1883 and Major ten years afterwards. He was adjqtant of the 2n4 Battalion from 1883 to 1833 and prior to going to South Africa had seen no war service.</p>
        <p>The Late Captain birch: The Queen's Sympathy.</p>
        <p>The Queen has srmt to Mrs. Birch, of Lympatnne, Granqe, South Devan, a mesgage of heartfelt sympathy on the death of her only son, Captain Birch. which occ wed but a few m-mtbs after toat of her husba..d. Caot tin Birch, who was in the Ist South Lancashire Reziment, wa+ killed on Solon ?Cop with hie subaltern, LiPiatenant Raphael, while they were gallantly trying to rescue a wounded sollier who was lying in the line of fire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>Warrington Men Killed and Wounded</head>
        <p>Warrington Men Killed and Wounded</p>
        <p>7ln th list of late I during th-) final operations for the relief of Ladysmith, appear the names of Sergeant (2502)iir. L tnce-Sergeant (4558) H. Monks of ton 1q Snuth Lmetshire Regiment. B-i:h were War- rington men, tho former residin4 at 14, Napier-3treet and the latter in Shin Yard. lamon7st thnse of the same regiment wound. d were Corporal (2461) T Worrall. of 3, Chapel-street, and Private (2625) P Edmunds. of Tilley-street, War*ington,and Privates (2484) E MoDowell, of O'dham and 1826 C Ralphson of St. Helens. who for- merly lived at Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>Late Sergeant Wheatley.</head>
        <p>The Late Sergeant Wheatley.</p>
        <p>Sertnant W. Whoatley who fel! in the engagornent at Piete,'s fiiii,was the on of cx-Paliee Cinstahle WheatlFT WOO, owing to ifilispisiti3n rctired from the force thre3</p>
        <p>or four years ago. The deceased joined the army at the age et eighteen years, and having served seven years returned to Wa, rington. He was first engaged as a bar- man in one of Me •srs Walker's houses, but eventually obtained a situation with the Bishop's Wharf Company, Bridge-foot. He was much respected, not only by the firm, but by his fellow employees, and when he was called un with the Army Reserves in November last to go to the Front they decided to pay his wife Oa per week during his absence. The news of his death has been received with the deepest regret by all who knew him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>Runcorn Soldier Killed.</head>
        <p>A Runcorn Soldier Killed.</p>
        <p>Of the forty odi Runcorn saldiers who are doing their duty, manfully, in the War against the Transvaal, Colour-Sergeant John Whitehead, (3607), of the 2nd Royal Lancaster Regiment,Nouth Africa field force, is the first called upon to lay down his life in his Country's cluse. Colout-Sergeant John Whitehead was but 27 years of age. He was born in Suffollostreet, Duke's Field, Runcorn, and even when.a boy was well-known, respected, and looked up to by his chums of •‘ The Fields.' He was educated at the Church Schools,Runcorn under the late respected Mr. Jordan ; and, as a echolar won the regard of his school fellows and teachers. At the age of 14 he left Runcorn for Manchester; and, sub- sequently joined the army following the ex- ample of his brother William, who by the way is now a Sergeant in the same regiment with which the deceased served. That young Whitehead made a good soldier is apparent from the fact, that, at the age of 25 years, he attained the full rank of Colour-Sergeant, and was made instructor of the gymnasium. Deceased was proud of the town of nis nativity ; and the town was also justly proud of him, which is fully evidenced bY the heartfelt grief pressed on all sides at the cetting short of so bright and Promising a career. That he fell doing his duty ; lace to the foe; a martyr to his country's cause ; is,however, the one gratifying feature in a very sad event. Deceased was engaged to a Runcorn young lady, and was to have been married upon his return from the war. Deceased leaves no parents to mourn his loss, as they preceded him, and went over to the great majority many years ago.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>Thrilling Incident.</head>
        <p>A Thrilling Incident.</p>
        <p>It is reported, that at the Spion Kop disaster, there were eight sergeants connected with tho variows com- panies dct tiled for the work who entered the actiDn. Of that number, only two returned to camp, and by a Strange cAucidenes upon reforming at the bottom of the hill the two brothers (Whitehead), found themselves Ancg again side by side. Overcome with emotion they shook hands, and fervently thanks' God for their safety, In face of this pathetic incident, the sad end of Colour-Sergeant Whitehead's career, comes; as a severe blow to him who is still left in the field.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>: Interesting to Soldiers' Friends.</head>
        <p>: Interesting to Soldiers' Friends.</p>
        <p>We learn that Mr. Shaw, tobacconist, Top Locks, Runcorn. h throughout the campaign in South Africa' acted as a" So!dier's friend," by supplying them with tobacco, almost at cost price, delivered carriage free and free of duty at the depots of the various regiments from whence they are forwarded !direct to the encampments. As Mr Shaw is about to forward another consignment in a few days. Me would advise our other soldiers' friends desirous of treating them to tobacco to forward their ()Agra at once to him, as the effect of the present Bud- get may result in Mr Slaw, being obliged to withdr aw his generous offer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>Local Red Cross Knights.</head>
        <p>Local Red Cross Knights.</p>
        <p>Hearty "Send-on" to Warrington Ambulance</p>
        <p>M. On Saturder night, the members of the Warrington Corps of the St. John Ambulance Brieade did honour to two of their slumber, first officer, H. T. Williams and second effacer C. J. Mansseur, who left Warrington on Sunday for London from whence they will proceed to the Cape as first-class supernumeraries. Assembling at the headquarters, the Gymnasium, Palmyra-square at 8 o'clock on Saturday, they proceeded by means of an omnibus:supplied by Mr. E. Connor, to Latchford and Stocktoe Heath, accompanied by Messrs. Cresfiele's band, playing apprepriate selections. On returning they dismounted at Bridge Foot and formed in procession, headed by the band. A number of men and boys carried flaming torches, and the band played patriotic airs. The proceesion marched through the principal streets, the" men of the hour" being carried shoulder high, to the Pelican Inn. On arrival at the Inn, a smoking concert was held and a number of interesting presentations took place. Captain Waring, the chief superintendent presided and the attendance included Dr. Adams, the chief surgeon to the Corps, Mr. Da.rbyshire, the stationmaster at the Central Station, and a numbsr of the workmates of the men who were going out. First officer Williams was employed by the Cheshire Lines Cemmittee,and 2nd officer Mansseur worked at the Win- wick Aseeum. After the toast of "The Ceeeen "had been musically honoured, Mr.Th,mpson on behalf of Mansseer's fellow workmen precented him with £3 03 61 in cash and said that the amount would probably have been larger had the notice of his going away not been so short. Second-officer MANSSEHR briefly responded and expressed his sincere thanks for the gilt. The CHAIRMAN then read the following letter which he had received from the Mayor :—" Dear Captain Waring. Please wish your men on my behalf, God speed, good health, and a speedy and 3afe return to old Wariington. Kinily excuse my absence. You will I am sure excuse me as I have had a very heavy week'" Mr J Charlton Parr wrote requesting Captain Waring to present each man with 10s on his behalf, that being the amount which he had sent the other members of the Corps who had gee; en the front. He said they had every reason to be nreed of the Corps. The Mayor sent each man Rl. Mrs Clare forwarded singlets and socks, and note paper, and Mrs Broadbent, Balaclava helmets, cholera belts, pocket handketchiefs, ve.seline,tobacco, and notepaper. Mr Chalmers sent tobacco and matches. Captain Waring in presentiug the gifts on behalf of the donors, said he felt a very happy man that day. The Corps had now sent out eleven men, and he was in the proud position of commanding officer without any officers. (Laughter.) With regard to the position of aff sirs in Seuth Afr ca, he had never despaired from the first. He was sure they all felt very proud cf Lords Roberts and Kitchener, General Buller, and Sir George White. Speaking of the manner in which the garrison at Ladysmith had held out. the Captain said that as an old sailor he was proud of these few sailors who got into Ladysmith and saved the situation. (Applause.) Dr. ADAMS in asking no assembly to drink the health of " The departing guests" also referred to the state of a'ceirs. The war was not over yet, but, as he said be- fore "We should worry throeeh." We were worrying thro seh and we had got our biti• ("teeth on. (Laughter). Bites left wounds and that was the reason their friends were szeine net. lie was sure that the men would do their duly. It was a very deep personal regret to hen, that ter eente Lancashire Regiment had lost its Colt.nel. He d air the thrining of Pieters Bill and Major Laseh who had run up morn than ono. for the Q wee's cue at Wimbledon, was wounded. They heped teat their frirmcin won!cl not be near anything liee leat danger, but they could not eis-</p>
      </div>
