      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>FATAL ACODENT AT MR. R. A NAYLOR'S WORKS.</head>
        <p>THE FATAL ACODENT AT MR. R. A NAYLOR'S WORKS.</p>
        <p>Mr Brizhouso, cr.rorier, conducted an inquiry on Monday morning at the Parade Room relative to the death of Walter Lunt, 18 years, who died under the following circumstances :—The deceased was in the employ of Mr. H. A. Naylor, timber merchant, and at about 3 p.m. on Monday, February 28th, he was in the act of throwing a bath off a pulley which drives a wood planing machine. The deceased used a piece of wood which, it is supposed, caught in the joint of the belc, knocking the end of the wood against the lower part of the deceased's stomach. He was removed to his home; and subsequently to the Infirm..ry, where he died from severe abdominal injuries and peritonitis. Mr. Browne appeared for the relatives of the deceased, and Mr. Henshall for Mr. R. A. Naylor, while Mr. Smith her Majesty's Inspector of Workshops, was present on behalf ot the Government.</p>
        <p>Dr. Thorp, junior house surgeon at the Infirmary said a severe blow with the stick produced would cause the injury which had been refetrel to. Fred Howard, also a maehinisc, said the deceased bad told him he was going to knock the belt off. It had turned over and he wanted to put it right. It, was wit- ness's duty to put it right, but he wanted the deceased to learn all he could himself. He believed the deceased wanted to put the matter right himselt.—By Mr Smith Re thought the deceased was cape ble of throwing the: belt off ; he _did not consider it dangerous to do so.</p>
        <p>Mr. Aenshall stated that the fast witness was the responsible overlook for three or four machines. BY Mr. Browne; If he had stopped the machine the whole of the works would have been stopped. 'I he Coroner: It would only have been a stoppage for a minute or two.</p>
        <p>Thomas James, also employed at the works, gave evidence as to seeing the accident,and said the stick used was strong enough but not long enough. Mr Smith remarked that the practice which was adop- ted was dangerous, and he thought it would be better to stop the engines while throwing a belt off a pulley. He thought an experienced man should be told off to put belts off, and that the engines should be either stopped or slowed down in the meantime.</p>
        <p>Mr. flenshall : The engines are always stopped when a belt is put on. The Coroner having summed up and commended the Government Inspector's suggestions, a verdict of" Acci- dental Death" was returned, and the jurors made a presentment" that it is advisable to slow down the engines when belts are being put on or taken off, and that there ought to be a special man appointed to do the work."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Meeting t)f the Parish Council.</p>
        <p>A meeting of-the Stockton Heath Parish Council was helcLat the National Schools on Tileslay evening. Jr.D Pearson presided and there were also present :—Messrs. J Whitley, Pemberton, Mason, Mclntyre, Clarke and E. Bradshaw (clerk).</p>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON AND NORTHWICH LIGHT RAILWAY.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that he wrote to the clerk of the County Council,,enclosing a copy of the conditions asked for by the Council with reference to the proposed Light Railway from Warring,ton to Northwich. He received a letter simply ackoowledging the receipt of the corn- ranuication, and wrote a second time. The following letter was sent in reply :—" Cheshire County Council, Clerk's Offices, Che 9 ater March 51h, 1900. Rs the War- ringt ea and NorthwlchLight Railway. Parish of Steck- ton Heath. Dear eir,—l am in receipt of your letter ot the 3rd inst. I submitted your letter of the 24th Janulry last together with the enclosure transmitted therewith to the Parliamentary Committee- of this Council, and I thave, acting upon their instructions pre- pared amendments to the order which are con" siiered to be sufficient to provide for all the reasonable n quirements of the parishes through which the propeaed ligut railway is to be laid. With reference to the special point raised by your parish council, namely, that the roads in the parish to be tra- versed by the railway should not be less than forty feet from kerb to keib, that ten fee footpaths should be pro- vided and that the passeagec line should run dowa the centre of ;he road, I have amended the order in this par- ticular to erovide that railwaye, one two and three, should not he constructed until the company aquire the necess try land to widen the roads to a width through- out between the fences of not less than 33 feet where there ie no feotps,th or only one footpath and to net leas than 39 feet where them are two footpaths. I understand from Messrs R Davies and Co., Selicitcre Warrine,enn whose represen- tative I saw on Saturday last that they were Instructed by your Parish Council to protect their interests in eon. neetion with the order and I have informed them that I would let them see the proposed amendments of this Council so that if there are any special matters which we have not provided for they may take such acticn as they may consider advisable on behalf of your Parish Council, I am, yours truly. Reeinald Potts, Clerk." Mr. Masora moved that the Council protest against the width of the road mentioeed in the letter as thee con- sidered that the main nada should he at least 60 feet in width from fence to fence,ad London-road being a con- tinuation of the main road from Warrington which is now being made 60 feet wide. Mr. Pemberton seconded and the motion was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>PROPOSED LIGMT RAILWAY TO RIINC"RN.</head>
        <p>A PROPOSED LIGMT RAILWAY TO RIINC"RN.</p>
        <p>The engineers of the Northwich and Warrington Hatt railway submitted a scheme of light railways propcsed to be run from Stockton Reath to Runa,nn on a scale similar to that proposed from Warrington to Northwicb. Tbe line of route of the Runcorn line was from Ellesmere- road, Stqckton Heath, through Waltsn, Moore and along ;.he main road to Runcorn. The promoters asked the Council to pais a resolution approving of the scheme but as thy had not submitted it in a complete form showin, the levels and th, proposed deviations, the Council deferred the consideration of it until that had been thne.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Parochial Committee.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Committee was held in the National Schoolroom on Thursday evening. Mr C W Davenport vesided, Pnd there were also present :—Mess's Pearson, Mclntyre, Clarke, Pemberton, Whit ev; Fairhurst, Mason, with G F Ashton (clerk), J Swinton (surveyor), and J Farrington (n7.isahce inspector). 66 KIMBERLEY DRIVE AND DUNDONALD AVENUE."</p>
        <p>Plans of new buildings submirted-ii;-141r7W;;ITurton, and Mr. 53,113 U 1 Pearson, were approved by the committee. An estate plan was also submitted by Mr. F W Wright for two new thoroughfares off VValton-read, to be respectively named, Kimberley drive and Dondouald Avenoe. Tise plan was approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-09_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>TFIE PAVING OP FAIRFIELD • ROAD.</head>
        <p>TFIE PAVING OP FAIRFIELD • ROAD.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pemberton moved a resolution that the District Council e askod to include in their next estimates the paving of the footpath in Fairfield-road so far as the buildings run. Mr Clarke seconded. Mr. Peirson said it the Cout,cil proposnd to put down tho ch-q ered tiles h would prefer a cindered footpath. Mr. Mason said 11, s:iould move an amendment that the footpath be paved with concrete or granolithic flags. Mr. Pearson seconded.</p>
        <p>The amendment wal carried. Mr. Clarke slid be obj,cted to prooorty owners tring put to the expense by the County Council of /pacing chequered tiles with fl )gs whilst at the same time the County Council were putting down the tiles themselves.</p>
