        <p>OUR FOOTBALL COMPETITION (COUPON* ON PAGE 7.) £5O Offered Weekly—too Aggregate Prize. RESULT OF LAST WEEK'S COMPETITION. ONE COMPETITOR WINS £5O. The Burnity v. Notts. Forest match included in the snatches for Football Competition No. 27 was a misprint. and therefore has been ignored in the examieation of , armoire. The following were the results of the other nine Imatches :-Glossop N. 8., drew with Wackbura R., Liverpool I beat Manchester City, Aston Villa drew with Sheffield 11.. Middlesbrough beat Bursleno P. V., Gravesend beat Bed- minster, Portsmouth beat Tottenham IL. Bristol beat Devon- port A., Bradford drew with Bit;ey, Broughton beat Swin- ton. Mr. A. Arthur, 5, York-road. Tunbridge Wells, is the only competitor who gave nine correct results, and therefore wins the £5O prize. The following competitors gave eight ee-rect results and therefore share the £lO :-G Drake. Nottingham; J Bruce, Scarborough; B Little, Leicester ; J Green, Kettering; P 11 Elolyoak, Newpor. Pagnell • II Lee. Hastings • H Lewis, Stratford-an-Avon ; F Beswick ; Mann, Chelmsford ; A Marshall, Leeds • D Allen, Keswick; B Chamberlain, Yarmouth • N Marlow. Hertford; H B Pittim, London; T Lewin. ihefford ; C Wilson, Stamford; T James. Melton Mowbray ;F3 T Jones, Surbiton ; S Holman. Leicester ; (3 Stuart, Doncaster; D Ball, Ilfracombe ; G Campbell, alas- goer; L Brooks. Brighton; H Webb, Lutterworth ; IT Thompson, Holloway; A S Pyewell. Middlesborough; Smith, Leytonstone ; B Maple, Bedford; W Cotton,Oxford ; C Bond Isleworth ; T Lawton, Bristol; J French. Oswald. twistle ; T Munroe, Chesterfield; B N Barber, Wolverhamp- ton. THE COUPON PRIZE. The £3 is rupon prize goes to Mr B Maple, Oaklands, Bush- mead Avenue, Bedfo,d, with 6541 coupons. THE AGGREGATE PRIZE. Mr Arthur has scored 33 punts for the Aggregate Prize ; Mr Maple, 31; Mr Proctor, 29; Mr Mann 20; Mr Pittam (CB.) 19; Mr Hutchings 14; Messrs Pridgeon and Pittsm (W.). 13 each ; Messrs Mason, Chaplin and Cook, 11 each ; Mr Wood. 10; Messrs Owen and Salt, 9 each; Messrs Ro igers, Brooks, Price and Funnel. 2 each • Messrs Astles, Crit,:hley, Twining, %like, Thompson and Gilett, 7 each. NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.. Rugby Football. [By "Ovalist.l There was another chance last week-end for Warring- ton to gain four points and make their record the best they have had under the Northern Union.but they failed to de what was expicted of them. Ssturday'e match was with Milken, and the Cumberland men presented such a strong defence to the Warrington attack that they kept their lines intact and won by a try to a goal. Warrington were by no means weakly represented and it is by not at all to their creditthat they could only sore a penalty goal all the afternoon. Time after time it looked probable that they would win, but taere was a lack of finish about the combined movements which pre- vented them being fruitful. !Willow thus got a welcome two points and spoiled Warrington's chance of making this season the best they have had since the Northern Union started. Monday saw the last League match of the season played at Wilder peel, Wigan being the visitors. Both sides were wed represented aud a good game was the result. The Wiean men got a severe trwuncing and Warrington gave about the best exhibition of back play tily have treated their supporters to at Wilderspool. There was a pour gate, considering that the game was not started until 5 o'clock, but those who were present enj eyed the rapid scoring which took place. Wigan were assisted by the redoubtable Charlie Wood, who receivea a welcome cheer from the Warrin2ton crowd on entering once more the Wilderspool ground.a. looked very fit, and plase 1 as well as any of his com- rades, but it was abundantly proved that he was not in the same class al Iseereveoi and Fish, the men opposed to him. Still he was quite as useful as Smith is at pre- sent. Wiean commenced by shocking the feelings of the home team for th.l, made the most desperate attempt, t 3 score. During almost the whole of the first quarter Warringten had to defend continuously, and it neeiel a little eye-rubbing to determine whether the foes were Oldham men or not roere was a similarity in jersey, and attack too. Like Olelham, Wigan had to fell hick and were soon subj .ctecl to a determined attack from the homesters. For a time play on both siclNs was rather loose. but War- rington improved as time went on and gave some exhi- bitiecs of clever passing. Hockenhall opened the scoeng in flue style, Duckworth followed suit, Jones kicked a goal, and by half-time Warringten were eight points ahead. Tee scani half opened with Wigan plaling seven for wards, for Brown had had to retire owing to injuries Warrington bean to pile points on rapidly. and Fish raccd rued the posts twice in superb style from open- ings made by leherw -Kid. Jones kicked a goal each time, and eightzen point 3to nil was the to' al score in favour of Warrington at the close. Wigan had not a look in behind the scrimmage, the backs being non-plussed by the more ,peedy Warrington men. • Taken all round, the display of the borne team was highly satisfactory, and augurs well for the Cup-ties, though S. Helene provide a formidable hurdle for the first round. Still, after the cropper the Saints came at Oldham on Saturday, we can fear them a little less. It is really remarkable that Warrington should have lost se many matches this season by small margins. The team has, generally, been good enough to win almost every match and vet the players have acquitted themselves so badly at times that in .re matches nave been lost than won. It has been difficult to see where the fault lay,for just 'when one fancied it was discovered,the team would go and play an excellent all-round game and satisfy everybody. The record below shows that the scoridg average has inprovM very considerably since 'ast season, and 174 points to 128 is far from had. But only 25 points out of 26 matches is where the rub comas in. Several matches have been played during th week, and tbe League table now stands as follows For. Against. P. W. L D. G. T. P. G. T. P. P. Runcorn ......25...21... 2.. 2...35.., 53-229.., 6... 7... 33...44 Oldham 25 21.., 3... 1...32... 93-343.- 9...18... 72...43 Swinton 25...18... 6... 49-191.-24.-20-146.-37 St. Helens 25...16... 6... 3...24... 53-207...12...29...111...35 Widnes 25 ..11...10... 4...22... 36-152.-19-35...143-26 Warrington 26...12 ..13... 1...24... 42-174 -16-34_123-25 Brought'n 1...20... 30 - 140...24...30...138...23 Salford 25 ..11...14.- 0...25... 46-188-31.-38...176.-22 Stockport 25...10...13... 2...21... 28-126...19...33...137...22 Leigh 25... 8...12... 5-.22- 21-116...24-47...189-21 Rochdale 111:24... 9...14... 18- 90...17...42...160 ..19 Itillom 25... 6...18... 1...19... 24 -110-30-58-234-15 Wigan 25... 6...18... 1... 7... 19- 71 . 28.-56.-231-13 Tyldesley: 25... 2...22... L.. 9... 16- 66 -41-84-.334- 5 The championship of the competition will be settled to diy, between Oliharn and Runcorn, on the ground of the latter. Runcorn have everything in their favonr for a draw or win will secure them the trophy, while Old- ham have to win the match to get ahead of the men in green. I hope the Cheshire lads will succeed—they fully deserve the honour—and! think they wili,notwithstand- ing that Oldham have shown the better form of late. It should be a very exciting game, and I hope to be there to see the fun. In the matches played since Saturday, Waeriegton beat Wigan. Reehdale Hornets beat Widnes, Lnigh b&gt;at Broughton Rangers, and Swinton beat Milloco, the hntne club winning in each case.</p>
