        <p>guise the fact that men who went out to do their duty for their Queen and country were sometimes amid a hail of bullets. Tlure was no freedom, and no safety in action, in rno ?ern conditioos of warfare. Considering this, to wish them a safe return meant more than a parting friendly nrssage. It we a dieinr.': hope that they would have a safe return. With regard to the men who were going out it was unnecessary to ray anything of their virtues. In any case they were good inen and true and they would ever reflect credit on the Warring- ton Corps of the St. John Ambulance Brigade. (Hear, hear.) They wished them good health, a safe voyage, successful work and a safe return. (Applause.) Th.. toast was enthusiastically drunk, the company sineing "For they are jolly good fellows," and cheering. .7eThe CHAIRMAN here mentioned the fact that the Belief C)ramittee was preeenting each man with a life insure ance policy for £lOO. They sincerely hoped it would not be required. etMr. DARZYSIIIRE also said a few words. He remarked that he had known Wiiiiame for the lest 18 years. He was under his charge at Chester 18 years ago, and he was then a very smart boy with good prospects. During hie connection with the Cheshire Liner he had fulfilled his duties as faithfully as coand be desired. He had every confidence in him that he would do his duty, and wished him a safe return. (Applause).. First-officer Weeetems briefly returned thanks. He said that the staff at the Central Station had that night kindly presented him with 22 103 6d. He understood that that amount had been collected within a trifle from the staff in the goods office and the passenger department. He could assure them that he was sorry in one sense that he was leaving those with whom he had been associated for nine years, but he was going fotward to do what he hoped would be a service of mercy to those who were fighting for the cause of England. Williams then mentioned the names of several surgeons in the town whom he wished to thank for the assistance they had rendered to the Brigade. He might say that he had had the honour of leadir g the Warrington Cheshire Lines team to victory four times in the championship of the Che,hire lenes Committee. (Applause.) Although he had been the leader of the team its success was not owing to his personal work but to the efforts of the members of it. He thanked them for the support they had given him He had always en- deavoured to do the utmost he could to assist ambulance work and be hoped he would be spared to return to the good old town of Warrington to continue to work for and increase the membership of the Warrington Ambulance Brigade. (Applause.) During the.evening, songs and recitations were ren- dered by the members of the corps and others and a vote ottleanke was passed to them for their services. The proceedings were brought ti a close at 11 o'clock With the singing of the Nateonel Anthem and "Auld Lang Syne."</p>
